research/erdos-cycle-1-judge.jsonl:13:{"id":"def-pointwise-uniform-and-uniformly-cauchy-convergence","model":"z-ai/glm-5.2","keep":true,"reason":"The three definitions (pointwise, uniform, uniformly Cauchy) are standard and correctly stated. The citation of [[def-function-space]] for real-valued functions is faithful (ℝ is a field). The citation of [[def-real-limit]] for pointwise convergence is correct. The rational-vs-real error equivalence argument is valid: [[cor-archimedean-reciprocal]] supplies a natural n≥1 with 1/n<η in the complete ordered field ℝ, and 1/n is a positive rational, so the rational-error condition implies the real-error condition. All page siblings and companion examples reference this definition consistently.","at":"2026-07-31T02:29:10.220Z"}
research/erdos-cycle-1-judge.jsonl:14:{"id":"def-pointwise-uniform-and-uniformly-cauchy-convergence","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro","keep":true,"reason":"Definitions are well-formed; the rational-equivalence remark correctly applies cor-archimedean-reciprocal and the argument is logically sound.","at":"2026-07-31T02:29:10.222Z"}
research/erdos-cycle-1-judge.jsonl:57:{"id":"cex-uniform-convergence-does-not-control-derivatives","model":"z-ai/glm-5.2","keep":true,"reason":"Verified the bound |f_k(x)|≤1/(2a_k) from (a_k|x|-1)^2≥0, the quotient-rule computation f_k'(0)=1, uniform convergence via Archimedean reciprocal, and that facts [L1]–[L4] faithfully restate their cited items. Step 2.1 uses the eventual form of the Archimedean property, whose monotonicity argument is explicitly described in the remarks of the cited cor-archimedean-reciprocal. No specific defect found.","context_sha256":"f21ec74936f04e57fd8455d8a8838bb266c0cbaf963b70861a537aeefa1e1827","at":"2026-07-31T03:14:12.272Z"}
