research/frontier-6-judge.jsonl:44:{"id":"thm-general-leibniz-rule","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","keep":false,"reason":"[L3] falsely attributes preservation of natural addition by \\(\\iota\\) to [[def-canonical-natural]]. That definition only gives the recursion and explicitly says the arithmetic is proved elsewhere ([[lem-of-naturals-positive]]); step 3.1 needs this unsupported claim.","context_sha256":"885c4be62c26518793de97f8ae3c65a6a0dcdd42796446eab10d12e5b1951be8","at":"2026-07-31T16:21:24.279Z"}
research/frontier-6-judge.jsonl:62:{"id":"def-taylor-polynomial-and-remainder","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","keep":false,"reason":"The denominator justification is mis-cited: [[lem-of-naturals-positive]] only gives positivity of \\(\\iota(m)\\) after \\(m\\ge1\\) is known; it does not state factorials are nonzero or establish \\(j!\\ge1\\). Thus \\(\\iota(j!)\\ne0\\) is not licensed as written.","context_sha256":"360ba8b66f2618421d454ab29c8796b0ea7039e9129d1a1f6c812d6768b7ce30","at":"2026-07-31T16:23:37.865Z"}
research/frontier-6-judge.jsonl:65:{"id":"thm-taylor-schlomilch-roche-remainder","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","keep":false,"reason":"Step 1.2 asserts \\(\\Psi'\\ne0\\) from \\(p\\ge1\\), but its tags [given, L1, algebra] never license \\(\\iota(p)>0\\) (or nonzero). This needs [[lem-of-naturals-positive]], which is only listed in deps and not supplied as a fact.","context_sha256":"991dd0aa928ade11dba2202942a11c4f6e5d0bda88f24db7640298d94e5a54a5","at":"2026-07-31T16:23:54.266Z"}
research/frontier-6-judge.jsonl:69:{"id":"cor-taylor-lagrange-and-cauchy-remainders","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","keep":false,"reason":"[L2] is not a faithful fact from its citations: it vaguely says factorials and powers “obey” definitions, but those definitions do not establish ι(n+1)ι(n!)=ι((n+1)!). def-canonical-natural explicitly defers preservation of products to lem-of-naturals-positive, which is uncited.","context_sha256":"3e6525bd0ceae050edbed91daf1933a570d6d6170f624b5560d79b8eacf3f29d","at":"2026-07-31T16:24:13.867Z"}
research/frontier-6-judge.jsonl:134:{"id":"lem-exponential-factorial-tail-bound","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","keep":false,"reason":"Step 1.1 requires the increasing property of ι (lem-of-naturals-positive) to get ι(k+1)≥ι(N+2) and bound the ratio, but the tag cites only [given, L1]; the justification is incomplete.","context_sha256":"3f36e1cf4b04df2b55f5645d8449a02e920ebf7426f5cc97ecf3250e3f3e3ac1","at":"2026-07-31T16:32:34.540Z"}
research/frontier-6-judge.jsonl:295:{"id":"ex-parabola-graph-has-content-zero","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","keep":false,"reason":"Step 1.2 uses ι(N) and the partition into N subintervals without citing [[def-canonical-natural]] or [[lem-of-naturals-positive]]; the step's tags [L1,given] do not support these moves.","context_sha256":"a017b5de0d341d503953b2faa779c7077d4bc2ea1a8ef5307bc28bc963f8cb15","at":"2026-07-31T16:55:50.385Z"}
research/frontier-6-judge.jsonl:435:{"id":"thm-multidimensional-integral-properties","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","keep":false,"reason":"[L3] asserts the reverse triangle inequality \\(\\bigl||u|-|v|\\bigr|\\le|u-v|\\), but neither cited item states it: the definition only defines \\(|\\cdot|\\), and lem-of-abs-value lists no triangle inequality. Thus L3 is an inaccurate citation and step 1.2 is unlicensed.","context_sha256":"207b2ad6a1e7834343c5a81e469dc8303e6f1dd76628308d3c50289facbb09c5","at":"2026-07-31T17:17:12.189Z"}
research/frontier-6-judge.jsonl:442:{"id":"thm-multidimensional-integral-properties","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","keep":false,"reason":"[L3] claims ||u|-|v|| ≤ |u-v| from def-abs-value and lem-of-abs-value, but those items do not state or imply that inequality; the proof of |f| integrability is therefore unsupported.","context_sha256":"207b2ad6a1e7834343c5a81e469dc8303e6f1dd76628308d3c50289facbb09c5","at":"2026-07-31T17:20:34.139Z"}
research/frontier-6-judge.jsonl:444:{"id":"lem-exponential-factorial-tail-bound","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","keep":false,"reason":"Step 1.1 implicitly uses that ι is increasing to deduce |x|/ι(k+1) ≤ 1/2 for k > N+1 from the given ι(N+2) ≥ 2|x|. That property is not supplied by [given, L1]; the step is missing a citation of lem-of-naturals-positive.","context_sha256":"2ce1b07b637c52ef261359711b361cf980c258ea522f7f38b0e895dcbf5b7f9a","at":"2026-07-31T17:22:09.028Z"}
research/frontier-6-judge.jsonl:467:{"id":"cor-taylor-lagrange-and-cauchy-remainders","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","keep":false,"reason":"Step 1.1 uses \\(\\iota(n+1)\\iota(n!)=\\iota((n+1)!)\\), but [L2] cites only definitions. \\(\\iota\\)'s multiplicativity is explicitly deferred by def-canonical-natural to lem-of-naturals-positive, which is not cited.","context_sha256":"22b55c55d740ff4750eb21a08d7c407437b65fa1c3f00ecb0112c98f99364212","at":"2026-07-31T18:39:36.469Z"}
research/frontier-6-judge.jsonl:487:{"id":"def-taylor-polynomial-and-remainder","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","keep":null,"reason":"UNPARSEABLE (304 chars): {\"keep\":false,\"reason\":\"The claim that factorials are nonzero is mis-cited: [[lem-of-naturals-positive]] only proves positivity of the canonical real of a natural already known to be ≥1. It neither states \\(j!\\\\ne0\\) nor establishes \\(j!\\\\ge1\\); that fact is in [[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]","context_sha256":"774d6ea96b6200fc97c0060fef0f805b75ab8c424c6baf28b566b7b112913446","at":"2026-07-31T18:40:28.257Z"}
research/frontier-6-judge.jsonl:489:{"id":"cor-taylor-lagrange-and-cauchy-remainders","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","keep":false,"reason":"Step 1.1 needs ι(n+1)ι(n!)=ι((n+1)!), but [L2] does not state or cite this. def-canonical-natural explicitly says preservation of products is proved in lem-of-naturals-positive, which is not cited; its vague “obey” wording does not license the move.","context_sha256":"22b55c55d740ff4750eb21a08d7c407437b65fa1c3f00ecb0112c98f99364212","at":"2026-07-31T18:40:34.377Z"}
research/frontier-6-judge.jsonl:512:{"id":"thm-taylor-schlomilch-roche-remainder","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","keep":false,"reason":"Step 1.2 does not justify \\(\\Psi'(t)\\ne0\\): \\(p\\ge1\\) and \\(x-t>0\\) require positivity/nonzeroness of \\(\\iota(p)\\), but it cites only given, L1, and algebra, not [[lem-of-naturals-positive]].","context_sha256":"57cbe9dec8ade67512290fd980d57367d4fc81f04501c4b71c1534d164b47c26","at":"2026-07-31T18:41:36.673Z"}
research/frontier-6-judge.jsonl:514:{"id":"thm-derivative-of-exponential","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","keep":false,"reason":"Step 2.1 cancels \\(\\iota(j+1)\\) and rewrites \\(\\iota((j+1)!)\\) as \\(\\iota(j+1)\\iota(j!)\\), but [L2] cites only definitions. \\(\\iota\\)'s multiplicativity and nonzero/positivity for positive naturals require [[lem-of-naturals-positive]], which is not cited.","context_sha256":"b179447d69d8646896570c1e0fc88792a5b463cdde2cc1e360d38efccd8cb290","at":"2026-07-31T18:41:43.299Z"}
research/frontier-6-judge.jsonl:531:{"id":"thm-derivative-of-exponential","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","keep":false,"reason":"Step 2.1 cancels ι(j+1) using factorial recurrence but does not cite that ι preserves products (e.g., [[lem-of-naturals-positive]]), leaving the simplification unjustified.","context_sha256":"b179447d69d8646896570c1e0fc88792a5b463cdde2cc1e360d38efccd8cb290","at":"2026-07-31T18:43:33.135Z"}
research/frontier-6-judge.jsonl:565:{"id":"thm-derivative-of-exponential","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","keep":false,"reason":"Step 2.1 cancels \\(\\iota(j+1)\\) and needs \\(\\iota((j+1)!)=\\iota(j+1)\\iota(j!)\\) with \\(\\iota(j+1)\\ne0\\). [L2] gives only the natural-number factorial recurrence; def-canonical-natural explicitly defers preservation of products and positivity to lem-of-naturals-positive, which is not cited.","context_sha256":"b179447d69d8646896570c1e0fc88792a5b463cdde2cc1e360d38efccd8cb290","at":"2026-07-31T18:46:36.520Z"}
research/frontier-6-judge.jsonl:568:{"id":"thm-derivative-of-exponential","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","keep":false,"reason":"Step 2.1 cancels iota(j+1)/iota((j+1)!) using factorial recurrence, which needs iota to preserve multiplication. Only def-canonical-natural is cited, which doesn't give that; missing lem-of-naturals-positive.","context_sha256":"b179447d69d8646896570c1e0fc88792a5b463cdde2cc1e360d38efccd8cb290","at":"2026-07-31T18:46:47.826Z"}
research/frontier-6-judge.jsonl:710:{"id":"def-oscillation-in-rn","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","keep":false,"reason":"The point-oscillation infimum is taken in extended reals but its existence is justified only by def-infimum (which defines infimum for R) and lem-of-abs-value; the correct citation is lem-extended-reals-complete.","context_sha256":"bacbaef3b0014cb86793479dd4a351e1a75ece366e124bc3331d4bf05b388467","at":"2026-07-31T19:00:22.885Z"}
research/frontier-6-judge.jsonl:821:{"id":"cex-exponential-not-uniformly-continuous-on-r","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","keep":false,"reason":"[L3] overstates [[cor-archimedean-reciprocal]]: its statement gives one n for each ε, not convergence of 1/ι(n). Its remarks say the eventual bound needs a monotonicity argument left to the caller, which this item never supplies.","context_sha256":"5ed201273307d814f815bb1cf60e5ffb0e36794cdef2f095f436939d67418936","at":"2026-07-31T20:02:09.956Z"}
research/frontier-6-judge.jsonl:837:{"id":"lem-exponential-series-has-infinite-radius","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","keep":false,"reason":"[L3] is mis-cited: its sources do not establish that embedded factorials \\(\\iota(n!)\\) are nonzero. The canonical-natural definition explicitly defers positivity/injectivity to [[lem-of-naturals-positive]], which is not cited; step 1.1 divides by \\(\\iota(n!)\\).","context_sha256":"ffbd5122ce58cc556c1711b98c2ddfecb39612401ca7e9eb76ddc65c2ccd3294","at":"2026-07-31T20:03:24.540Z"}
research/frontier-6-judge.jsonl:854:{"id":"lem-scaled-binomial-coefficients-converge","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","keep":false,"reason":"[L2] inaccurately restates [[cor-archimedean-reciprocal]] as the convergence \\(1/\\iota(n)\\to0\\). Its statement only supplies, for each \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), one natural with reciprocal below \\(\\varepsilon\\); the eventual threshold is only discussed in remarks.","context_sha256":"c5a3bcfb3019481433a38478a9032975dcae89039b236c996be4447b4e19a9f4","at":"2026-07-31T20:05:55.484Z"}
research/frontier-6-judge.jsonl:855:{"id":"cex-exponential-not-uniformly-continuous-on-r","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","keep":true,"reason":"Proof constructs sequences with |x_n-y_n|→0 while |exp(x_n)-exp(y_n)|→∞, contradicting uniform continuity. Every step is supported by cited facts; [L3] faithfully restates cor-archimedean-reciprocal including its remark's eventual form. No defect found.","context_sha256":"5ed201273307d814f815bb1cf60e5ffb0e36794cdef2f095f436939d67418936","at":"2026-07-31T20:06:04.940Z"}
research/frontier-6-judge.jsonl:856:{"id":"def-oscillation-in-rn","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","keep":false,"reason":"Infimum well-posed by citing def-infimum (defines infimum for R, not existence) and lem-of-abs-value; existence in extended reals requires lem-extended-reals-complete. No correct existence fact cited.","context_sha256":"67e4cedfa92ad2aae699f3a1c99c2dd63155ba09be2328eb920b7d879b7c58a5","at":"2026-07-31T20:06:18.118Z"}
research/frontier-6-judge.jsonl:872:{"id":"lem-scaled-binomial-coefficients-converge","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","keep":false,"reason":"Fact [L1] incorrectly cites def-canonical-natural for 'preserves the products involved'; that definition does not state or prove preservation. The correct source is lem-of-naturals-positive, so step 1.1's justification is flawed.","context_sha256":"c5a3bcfb3019481433a38478a9032975dcae89039b236c996be4447b4e19a9f4","at":"2026-07-31T20:08:01.854Z"}
research/frontier-6-judge.jsonl:886:{"id":"lem-jordan-set-integral-well-defined","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","keep":false,"reason":"Step 2.1 uses symbol 'ι(N)' not defined in this item or cited facts (cor-archimedean-reciprocal uses 1/n). Lemma also omits hypothesis that f is bounded (used in step 3.1).","context_sha256":"ef887e9d8d8dd51eb4b643752e46108fbc9b271b90cfb53fa583637ad8a03bd1","at":"2026-07-31T20:11:50.319Z"}
research/frontier-6-judge.jsonl:913:{"id":"thm-continuous-on-a-rectangle-is-riemann-integrable","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","keep":false,"reason":"Step 1.1 chooses a grid with mesh below a threshold without citing any lemma that such a grid exists (e.g., cor-archimedean-reciprocal or a grid-existence lemma); the tags [L1,L2,L3,given,choose] do not provide this existence.","context_sha256":"2bd449d9c7e17d9fb5f6b288fc739c27b8109b3dde074bf27f3e811a1b6ddbc6","at":"2026-07-31T20:19:09.586Z"}
research/frontier-6-judge.jsonl:923:{"id":"def-oscillation-in-rn","model":"gpt-5.6-terra","keep":false,"reason":"The claim that the pointwise infimum is well posed is mis-cited: it cites [[def-infimum]] (only subsets of R) and [[lem-of-abs-value]], but local oscillations may equal +∞. Extended-real infimum existence requires [[lem-extended-reals-complete]], which that step does not cite.","context_sha256":"67e4cedfa92ad2aae699f3a1c99c2dd63155ba09be2328eb920b7d879b7c58a5","at":"2026-08-01T00:23:49.815Z"}
research/audit/wave3-topology.proof-contracts.json:500:     "quote": "Let $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and let $\\varepsilon \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $\\varepsilon > 0$. The **$\\varepsilon$-neighbourhood of $x$** is $$N_\\varepsilon(x) \\;:=\\; \\{\\, y \\in \\mathbb{R} : |y - x| < \\varepsilon \\,\\},$$ and the **punctured $\\varepsilon$-neighbourhood of $x$** is $$N^{*}_\\varepsilon(x) \\;:=\\; N_\\varepsilon(x) \\setminus \\{x\\} \\;=\\; \\{\\, y \\in \\mathbb{R} : 0 < |y - x| < \\varepsilon \\,\\}.$$ The two descriptions of $N^{*}_\\varepsilon(x)$ agree because $|y - x| = 0$ holds exactly when $y = x$ ([[lem-of-abs-value]]). **A neighbourhood is an open interval.** For every $x$ and every $\\varepsilon > 0$, $$N_\\varepsilon(x) \\;=\\; (x - \\varepsilon,\\ x + \\varepsilon),$$ the interval of [[def-interval]]. Indeed [[lem-of-abs-value]] gives, for $\\varepsilon > 0$, the equivalence $|y - x| < \\varepsilon \\iff -\\varepsilon < y - x < \\varepsilon$, and adding $x$ throughout turns the right-hand side into $x - \\varepsilon < y < x + \\varepsilon$ ([[def-ordered-field]]).",
research/audit/wave3-topology.proof-contracts.json:3739:     "source": "cor-archimedean-reciprocal",
research/audit/wave3-topology.proof-contracts.json:3741:     "quote": "where $n \\cdot 1_F$ is the canonical natural of $F$ ([[thm-of-archimedean]]) and $1/(n \\cdot 1_F)$ is its multiplicative inverse ([[def-field]]). As is standard we abbreviate $n \\cdot 1_F$ to $n$ and write the conclusion $1/n < \\varepsilon$.",
research/audit/wave3-topology.proof-contracts.json:4818:     "quote": "Let $F$ be an Archimedean ordered field ([[def-archimedean-field]]) and let $\\iota : \\mathbb{Q} \\to F$ be the canonical embedding ([[lem-of-q-embeds]]). Then $\\iota(\\mathbb{Q})$ is dense in $F$: for any $x < y$ in $F$ there is a rational $q$ with $x < \\iota(q) < y$.",
