research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8745:items/ex-compactness-in-the-standard-topologies.md:75:[L6] The naturals embed in $\mathbb{R}$ by the canonical natural $\iota$ ([[def-canonical-natural]], [[def-natural-numbers]]); $\iota$ is injective, which is a lemma about ordered fields and not part of the definition ([[lem-of-naturals-positive]]), so $\{\iota(n) : n \in \mathbb{N}\}$ is a countably infinite subset of $\mathbb{R}$.
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8873:items/ex-one-point-compactifications-of-the-line-and-of-the-naturals.md:78:[L8] For every real $\varepsilon > 0$ there is a natural $m \ge 1$ with $1/m < \varepsilon$, and $\mathbb{R}$ is an ordered field ([[cor-archimedean-reciprocal]], [[def-canonical-natural]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]], [[def-interval]], [[def-topological-space]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:8932:items/ex-sigma-compactness-and-lindelof-in-the-line-and-the-rationals.md:62:[L3] For every real $t$ there is $n \in \mathbb{N}$ with $t < \iota(n)$ ([[thm-of-archimedean]], [[def-canonical-natural]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9000:items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:67:[L3] The order of $\mathbb{R}$ is total ([[def-real-order]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]]); for every real $t$ there is $n \in \mathbb{N}$ with $t < \iota(n)$ ([[thm-of-archimedean]], [[def-canonical-natural]]); and for reals $c<d$ there is a rational strictly between them ([[lem-of-q-dense]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9082:deps: [lem-compactness-of-a-subspace-is-ambient, def-compactness-variants, def-compact-space, def-locally-compact-space, thm-compactness-agrees-with-metric-compactness, thm-heine-borel-rn, def-metrizable-space, lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space, def-metric-topology, thm-rationals-countable, def-countable, lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n, def-countable-choice, def-interval, def-canonical-natural, thm-of-archimedean, def-subspace-topology-top, def-neighbourhood-top, lem-of-q-dense, lem-q-and-irrationals-dense-r, def-hereditary-property, def-topological-space, def-complete-ordered-field, thm-closure-characterisation-top, def-interior-closure-boundary-top]
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9137:[L3] For every real $t$ there is $n \in \mathbb{N}$ with $t < \iota(n)$ ([[thm-of-archimedean]], [[def-canonical-natural]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:9253:items/cex-a-continuous-image-of-a-locally-compact-space.md:59:[L3] Distinct naturals have distinct canonical naturals and $\iota(n+1) = \iota(n) + 1$, so the members of $N$ are spaced at distance at least $1$; and for every real $t$ there is a natural $n$ with $t < \iota(n)$ ([[def-canonical-natural]], [[def-natural-numbers]], [[thm-of-archimedean]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]]).
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:10281:research/audit/wave2-real-analysis.proof-contracts.json:1:{"version":1,"level":"published-audit-wave2-real-analysis","scope":["lem-sup-unique","lem-reflection","thm-infimum-property","lem-sup-epsilon","lem-inf-epsilon","lem-max-is-sup","lem-finite-set-has-max","lem-sup-monotone","lem-sup-translate","lem-sup-scale","lem-sup-sum","fs-sup-belongs-to-set","fs-every-set-has-sup","ex-sup-of-open-interval","ex-sup-of-closed-interval","ex-inf-of-reciprocals","ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two","ex-sup-of-sum-of-sets","cex-sup-not-attained","cex-unbounded-set-has-no-sup","cex-empty-set-has-no-sup"],"contracts":{"lem-sup-unique":{"citations":[{"fact":"L1","source":"def-complete-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.","uses":["1.1","2.1"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"def-infimum","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ and $\\ell \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then $\\ell$ is a\n**greatest lower bound**, or **infimum**, of $S$ if both of the following hold:\n\n- $\\ell$ is a lower bound of $S$ ([[def-bounded-set]]), that is, $\\ell \\le s$ for\n  every $s \\in S$;\n- $\\ell' \\le \\ell$ for every lower bound $\\ell'$ of $S$.\n\nWritten out in one line:\n\n$$\\ell \\text{ is an infimum of } S \\iff \\big[(\\forall s \\in S)\\, \\ell \\le s\\big] \\text{ and } \\big[(\\forall \\ell' \\in \\mathbb{R})\\, \\big((\\forall s \\in S)\\, \\ell' \\le s\\big) \\Rightarrow \\ell' \\le \\ell\\big].$$\n\nAn infimum, when it exists, is unique ([[lem-sup-unique]]), so we may write\n$\\inf S$ for it.","uses":["1.2","2.2"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"def-complete-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.","uses":["3.1"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"def-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"An **ordered field** is a field $F$ ([[def-field]]) together with a subset\n$P \\subseteq F$, the set of **positive** elements (the *positive cone*),\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(O1) Trichotomy.** For each $x \\in F$, exactly one of the following holds:\n  $x \\in P$, or $x = 0$, or $-x \\in P$.\n- **(O2) Closure.** If $x, y \\in P$ then $x + y \\in P$ and $x \\cdot y \\in P$.\n\nWe write $x > 0$ for $x \\in P$ (read \"$x$ is positive\"), and define the order by\n\n$$a < b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; b - a \\in P, \\qquad a \\le b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a < b \\text{ or } a = b.$$\n\nThus $a > b$ means $b < a$, and $a \\ge b$ means $b \\le a$. An element $x$ with\n$-x \\in P$ (equivalently $x < 0$) is called **negative**.","uses":["3.1"]}],"derivations":[{"id":"step-1-1","claim":"Assume $u_1$ and $u_2$ are both least upper bounds of $S$; in particular each of them is an upper bound of $S$ and each is $\\le$ every upper bound of $S$.","step":"1.1","inputs":["assume-hyp","L1"]},{"id":"step-1-2","claim":"Assume $\\ell_1$ and $\\ell_2$ are both greatest lower bounds of $S$; in particular each of them is a lower bound of $S$ and each is $\\ge$ every lower bound of $S$.","step":"1.2","inputs":["assume-hyp","L2"]},{"id":"step-2-1","claim":"Applying the leastness of $u_1$ to the upper bound $u_2$ gives $u_1 \\le u_2$, and applying the leastness of $u_2$ to the upper bound $u_1$ gives $u_2 \\le u_1$.","step":"2.1","inputs":["step 1.1","L1"]},{"id":"step-2-2","claim":"Applying the greatestness of $\\ell_1$ to the lower bound $\\ell_2$ gives $\\ell_2 \\le \\ell_1$, and applying the greatestness of $\\ell_2$ to the lower bound $\\ell_1$ gives $\\ell_1 \\le \\ell_2$.","step":"2.2","inputs":["step 1.2","L2"]},{"id":"step-3-1","claim":"By antisymmetry $u_1 = u_2$ and $\\ell_1 = \\ell_2$, so a least upper bound and a greatest lower bound of $S$ are each unique when they exist, which is what licenses writing $\\sup S$ and $\\inf S$.","step":"3.1","inputs":["step 2.1","step 2.2","L3"]}],"routine_steps":[],"boundaries":[{"case":"empty","status":"checked","evidence":"The statement allows arbitrary S, including the empty set; steps 2.1 and 2.2 use only conditional leastness/greatestness, so uniqueness remains valid."},{"case":"zero","status":"not_applicable","reason":"No zero index, zero divisor, or zero parameter boundary occurs in this statement or proof."},{"case":"one","status":"not_applicable","reason":"No unit index or unit-parameter boundary requires a separate case in this statement or proof."},{"case":"degenerate","status":"checked","evidence":"step 3.1 covers the equality collapse forced by the two opposite inequalities."},{"case":"endpoints","status":"not_applicable","reason":"No interval endpoint or finite-range endpoint occurs in this statement or proof."},{"case":"nonempty-choice","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The proof makes no choice from a set whose nonemptiness requires a separate discharge."},{"case":"iff-forward","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a forward implication proof."},{"case":"iff-reverse","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a reverse implication proof."}],"finite_smoke":[],"risk_review":{"status":"pending","reviewer":null,"notes":"Reserved for the independent Alpha proof-refuter if risk routing selects this item after A3."}},"lem-reflection":{"citations":[{"fact":"L1","source":"def-complete-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.","uses":["2.1"]},{"fact":"L1","source":"def-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"An **ordered field** is a field $F$ ([[def-field]]) together with a subset\n$P \\subseteq F$, the set of **positive** elements (the *positive cone*),\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(O1) Trichotomy.** For each $x \\in F$, exactly one of the following holds:\n  $x \\in P$, or $x = 0$, or $-x \\in P$.\n- **(O2) Closure.** If $x, y \\in P$ then $x + y \\in P$ and $x \\cdot y \\in P$.\n\nWe write $x > 0$ for $x \\in P$ (read \"$x$ is positive\"), and define the order by\n\n$$a < b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; b - a \\in P, \\qquad a \\le b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a < b \\text{ or } a = b.$$\n\nThus $a > b$ means $b < a$, and $a \\ge b$ means $b \\le a$. An element $x$ with\n$-x \\in P$ (equivalently $x < 0$) is called **negative**.","uses":["2.1"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"def-bounded-set","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Throughout, $\\mathbb{R}$ denotes the complete ordered field\n([[def-complete-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is a subset of it.\n\nThe notions **upper bound** and **bounded above** are already fixed by\n[[def-complete-ordered-field]] and are only recalled here, never redefined:\n$u \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is an *upper bound* of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$, and\n$S$ is *bounded above* if it has at least one upper bound. The dual notions are:\n\n- $\\ell \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is a **lower bound** of $S$ if $\\ell \\le s$ for all $s \\in S$.\n- $S$ is **bounded below** if it has at least one lower bound.\n- $S$ is **bounded** if it is both bounded above and bounded below, that is, if\n  there are $\\ell, u \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $\\ell \\le s \\le u$ for every $s \\in S$.","uses":["3.1","3.2","3.3","3.4","4.1"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"lem-of-inverse-unique","source_section":"Statement","quote":"In a field $F$ ([[def-field]]) the additive identity, the multiplicative\nidentity, each additive inverse, and each multiplicative inverse are unique.\nHence the notations $0$, $1$, $-x$, and $x^{-1}$ denote well-defined elements, as\nthe field definition and its consequences assume.","uses":["1.1","1.2"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"def-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"A **field** is a set $F$ equipped with two binary operations $+$ (addition) and\n$\\cdot$ (multiplication) and two distinguished elements $0 \\ne 1$ of $F$,\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(A)** $(F, +)$ is an abelian group with identity $0$: addition is\n  associative and commutative, $x + 0 = x$ for all $x$, and every $x$ has an\n  additive inverse $-x$ with $x + (-x) = 0$.\n- **(M)** Multiplication is associative and commutative **on all of $F$**, and\n  $x \\cdot 1 = x$ for every $x \\in F$; moreover $(F \\setminus \\{0\\}, \\cdot)$ is an\n  abelian group with identity $1$, so every $x \\ne 0$ has a multiplicative inverse\n  $x^{-1}$ with $x \\cdot x^{-1} = 1$.\n- **(D)** Multiplication distributes over addition: $x \\cdot (y + z) = x\\cdot y + x\\cdot z$.\n\nWe write $x - y := x + (-y)$ and $x/y := x \\cdot y^{-1}$ (for $y \\ne 0$), and\nabbreviate $x \\cdot y$ as $xy$.","uses":["1.1","1.2"]}],"derivations":[{"id":"step-1-1","claim":"For all $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}$ the field identity $y - x = (-x) - (-y)$ holds, and $-(-x) = x$.","step":"1.1","inputs":["L3","algebra"]},{"id":"step-1-2","claim":"The map $s \\mapsto -s$ sends $S$ onto $-S$ and $-S$ onto $-(-S)$, and since $-(-s) = s$ it is a bijection of $S$ with $-S$ whose inverse is itself; hence $-(-S) = S$, and $S$ is nonempty exactly when $-S$ is nonempty.","step":"1.2","inputs":["L3","algebra"]},{"id":"step-2-1","claim":"For all $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}$: $x < y$ holds exactly when $y - x$ is positive, which by 1.1 is exactly when $(-x) - (-y)$ is positive, which is exactly $-y < -x$; and $x = y$ holds exactly when $-x = -y$; hence $x \\le y$ if and only if $-y \\le -x$.","step":"2.1","inputs":["step 1.1","L1"]},{"id":"step-3-1","claim":"Suppose $u$ is an upper bound of $S$. Every element of $-S$ has the form $-s$ with $s \\in S$, and $s \\le u$ gives $-u \\le -s$; hence $-u$ is a lower bound of $-S$.","step":"3.1","inputs":["assume-hyp","step 2.1","L2"]},{"id":"step-3-2","claim":"Conversely, suppose $-u$ is a lower bound of $-S$. For $s \\in S$ we have $-s \\in -S$, so $-u \\le -s$, and applying 2.1 to this inequality gives $s \\le u$; hence $u$ is an upper bound of $S$. This together with 3.1 proves claim 1.","step":"3.2","inputs":["assume-hyp","step 2.1","L2","step 3.1"]},{"id":"step-3-3","claim":"Suppose $\\ell$ is a lower bound of $S$. For $s \\in S$ we have $\\ell \\le s$, hence $-s \\le -\\ell$, and every element of $-S$ is such a $-s$; hence $-\\ell$ is an upper bound of $-S$.","step":"3.3","inputs":["assume-hyp","step 2.1","L2"]},{"id":"step-3-4","claim":"Conversely, suppose $-\\ell$ is an upper bound of $-S$. For $s \\in S$ we have $-s \\in -S$, so $-s \\le -\\ell$, and applying 2.1 gives $\\ell \\le s$; hence $\\ell$ is a lower bound of $S$. This together with 3.3 proves claim 2.","step":"3.4","inputs":["assume-hyp","step 2.1","L2","step 3.3"]},{"id":"step-4-1","claim":"Claim 1 says the upper bounds of $S$ are exactly the negatives of the lower bounds of $-S$, so $S$ is bounded above exactly when $-S$ is bounded below; claim 2 says likewise that $S$ is bounded below exactly when $-S$ is bounded above; combining the two, $S$ is bounded exactly when $-S$ is bounded, and $-(-S) = S$ with $S$ nonempty exactly when $-S$ is nonempty.","step":"4.1","inputs":["step 3.1","step 3.2","step 3.3","step 3.4","step 1.2","L2"]}],"routine_steps":[],"boundaries":[{"case":"empty","status":"checked","evidence":"step 1.2 explicitly proves nonemptiness is preserved and the involution also sends the empty set to itself."},{"case":"zero","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 1.1 and 2.1 explicitly handle double negation and reflection through zero."},{"case":"one","status":"not_applicable","reason":"No unit index or unit-parameter boundary requires a separate case in this statement or proof."},{"case":"degenerate","status":"checked","evidence":"step 1.2 proves the reflection map is its own inverse, including fixed point zero."},{"case":"endpoints","status":"not_applicable","reason":"No interval endpoint or finite-range endpoint occurs in this statement or proof."},{"case":"nonempty-choice","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The proof makes no choice from a set whose nonemptiness requires a separate discharge."},{"case":"iff-forward","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 3.1 and 3.3 prove the two forward bound implications."},{"case":"iff-reverse","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 3.2 and 3.4 prove the two reverse bound implications."}],"finite_smoke":[],"risk_review":{"status":"pending","reviewer":null,"notes":"Reserved for the independent Alpha proof-refuter if risk routing selects this item after A3."}},"thm-infimum-property":{"citations":[{"fact":"L1","source":"def-complete-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.","uses":["3.1","4.2"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"lem-reflection","source_section":"Statement","quote":"For $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ write $-S := \\{-s : s \\in S\\}$. Then $-(-S) = S$,\nand $S$ is nonempty if and only if $-S$ is nonempty. Moreover, for all\n$u, \\ell \\in \\mathbb{R}$:\n\n1. $u$ is an upper bound of $S$ if and only if $-u$ is a lower bound of $-S$;\n2. $\\ell$ is a lower bound of $S$ if and only if $-\\ell$ is an upper bound of $-S$.\n\nConsequently $S$ is bounded above if and only if $-S$ is bounded below, $S$ is\nbounded below if and only if $-S$ is bounded above, and $S$ is bounded if and\nonly if $-S$ is bounded ([[def-bounded-set]]).","uses":["1.2","2.1","5.1"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"def-infimum","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ and $\\ell \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then $\\ell$ is a\n**greatest lower bound**, or **infimum**, of $S$ if both of the following hold:\n\n- $\\ell$ is a lower bound of $S$ ([[def-bounded-set]]), that is, $\\ell \\le s$ for\n  every $s \\in S$;\n- $\\ell' \\le \\ell$ for every lower bound $\\ell'$ of $S$.\n\nWritten out in one line:\n\n$$\\ell \\text{ is an infimum of } S \\iff \\big[(\\forall s \\in S)\\, \\ell \\le s\\big] \\text{ and } \\big[(\\forall \\ell' \\in \\mathbb{R})\\, \\big((\\forall s \\in S)\\, \\ell' \\le s\\big) \\Rightarrow \\ell' \\le \\ell\\big].$$\n\nAn infimum, when it exists, is unique ([[lem-sup-unique]]), so we may write\n$\\inf S$ for it.","uses":["6.1"]},{"fact":"L4","source":"lem-sup-unique","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$. If $u_1$ and $u_2$ are both least upper bounds of\n$S$ ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]), then $u_1 = u_2$. If $\\ell_1$ and $\\ell_2$\nare both greatest lower bounds of $S$ ([[def-infimum]]), then\n$\\ell_1 = \\ell_2$.\n\nConsequently a set has at most one supremum and at most one infimum, and the\nnotations $\\sup S$ and $\\inf S$ denote single, well-determined real numbers\nwhenever they exist.","uses":["3.1","6.1"]},{"fact":"L5","source":"def-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"A **field** is a set $F$ equipped with two binary operations $+$ (addition) and\n$\\cdot$ (multiplication) and two distinguished elements $0 \\ne 1$ of $F$,\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(A)** $(F, +)$ is an abelian group with identity $0$: addition is\n  associative and commutative, $x + 0 = x$ for all $x$, and every $x$ has an\n  additive inverse $-x$ with $x + (-x) = 0$.\n- **(M)** Multiplication is associative and commutative **on all of $F$**, and\n  $x \\cdot 1 = x$ for every $x \\in F$; moreover $(F \\setminus \\{0\\}, \\cdot)$ is an\n  abelian group with identity $1$, so every $x \\ne 0$ has a multiplicative inverse\n  $x^{-1}$ with $x \\cdot x^{-1} = 1$.\n- **(D)** Multiplication distributes over addition: $x \\cdot (y + z) = x\\cdot y + x\\cdot z$.\n\nWe write $x - y := x + (-y)$ and $x/y := x \\cdot y^{-1}$ (for $y \\ne 0$), and\nabbreviate $x \\cdot y$ as $xy$.","uses":["5.2"]},{"fact":"L5","source":"lem-of-inverse-unique","source_section":"Statement","quote":"In a field $F$ ([[def-field]]) the additive identity, the multiplicative\nidentity, each additive inverse, and each multiplicative inverse are unique.\nHence the notations $0$, $1$, $-x$, and $x^{-1}$ denote well-defined elements, as\nthe field definition and its consequences assume.","uses":["5.2"]},{"fact":"L5","source":"lem-of-add-order","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) with positive cone $P$, and\nlet $a, b, c, d \\in F$.\n\n1. **Translation invariance.** If $a < b$ then $a + c < b + c$.\n2. **Adding inequalities.** If $a < b$ and $c < d$ then $a + c < b + d$.","uses":["5.2"]}],"derivations":[{"id":"step-1-1","claim":"By hypothesis $S \\ne \\emptyset$ and $S$ is bounded below; fix a lower bound $\\ell_0$ of $S$, so $\\ell_0 \\le s$ for every $s \\in S$.","step":"1.1","inputs":["given","choose"]},{"id":"step-1-2","claim":"Let $\\ell'$ be an arbitrary lower bound of $S$; then $-\\ell'$ is an upper bound of $-S$.","step":"1.2","inputs":["assume-hyp","L2"]},{"id":"step-2-1","claim":"Since $S$ is nonempty, so is $-S$, and since $\\ell_0$ is a lower bound of $S$, its negative $-\\ell_0$ is an upper bound of $-S$; hence $-S$ is a nonempty subset of $\\mathbb{R}$ that is bounded above.","step":"2.1","inputs":["step 1.1","L2"]},{"id":"step-3-1","claim":"By the least-upper-bound property, $-S$ has a least upper bound in $\\mathbb{R}$; write $u := \\sup(-S)$, which is well defined by uniqueness.","step":"3.1","inputs":["step 2.1","L1","L4"]},{"id":"step-4-1","claim":"Define $\\ell := -u$.","step":"4.1","inputs":["step 3.1","construct"]},{"id":"step-4-2","claim":"The element $u$ is the least of the upper bounds of $-S$ and $-\\ell'$ is one of them, hence $u \\le -\\ell'$.","step":"4.2","inputs":["step 1.2","step 3.1","L1"]},{"id":"step-5-1","claim":"Apply the reflection fact to the set $-S$: since $u$ is an upper bound of $-S$, its negative $-u$ is a lower bound of $-(-S)$, and $-(-S) = S$; so $\\ell = -u$ is a lower bound of $S$.","step":"5.1","inputs":["step 4.1","step 3.1","L2"]},{"id":"step-5-2","claim":"Negating the inequality $u \\le -\\ell'$ reverses it, giving $-(-\\ell') \\le -u$, that is $\\ell' \\le \\ell$.","step":"5.2","inputs":["step 4.2","step 4.1","L5"]},{"id":"step-6-1","claim":"Thus $\\ell$ is a lower bound of $S$ satisfying $\\ell' \\le \\ell$ for every lower bound $\\ell'$ of $S$, so $\\ell$ is a greatest lower bound of $S$; it is the only one, so $\\inf S$ exists and $\\inf S = \\ell = -\\sup(-S)$.","step":"6.1","inputs":["step 5.1","step 5.2","L3","L4"]}],"routine_steps":[],"boundaries":[{"case":"empty","status":"checked","evidence":"The statement excludes the empty set, and step 1.1 anchors the nonempty hypothesis before any existence argument."},{"case":"zero","status":"checked","evidence":"step 4.1 defines the reflected candidate through additive zero and steps 5.1-5.2 verify it."},{"case":"one","status":"not_applicable","reason":"No unit index or unit-parameter boundary requires a separate case in this statement or proof."},{"case":"degenerate","status":"not_applicable","reason":"No equality-collapse or other degenerate parameter is excluded or used in the statement or proof."},{"case":"endpoints","status":"not_applicable","reason":"No interval endpoint or finite-range endpoint occurs in this statement or proof."},{"case":"nonempty-choice","status":"checked","evidence":"step 1.1 chooses a lower bound only after bounded-below existence is given, and step 3.1 uses completeness only after step 2.1 proves -S nonempty and bounded above."},{"case":"iff-forward","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a forward implication proof."},{"case":"iff-reverse","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a reverse implication proof."}],"finite_smoke":[],"risk_review":{"status":"pending","reviewer":null,"notes":"Reserved for the independent Alpha proof-refuter if risk routing selects this item after A3."}},"lem-sup-epsilon":{"citations":[{"fact":"L1","source":"def-complete-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.","uses":["1.1","3.1","4.2"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"lem-sup-unique","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$. If $u_1$ and $u_2$ are both least upper bounds of\n$S$ ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]), then $u_1 = u_2$. If $\\ell_1$ and $\\ell_2$\nare both greatest lower bounds of $S$ ([[def-infimum]]), then\n$\\ell_1 = \\ell_2$.\n\nConsequently a set has at most one supremum and at most one infimum, and the\nnotations $\\sup S$ and $\\inf S$ denote single, well-determined real numbers\nwhenever they exist.","uses":["1.1","4.2"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"def-complete-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.","uses":["2.1","2.2","3.1","3.2","4.1"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"def-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"An **ordered field** is a field $F$ ([[def-field]]) together with a subset\n$P \\subseteq F$, the set of **positive** elements (the *positive cone*),\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(O1) Trichotomy.** For each $x \\in F$, exactly one of the following holds:\n  $x \\in P$, or $x = 0$, or $-x \\in P$.\n- **(O2) Closure.** If $x, y \\in P$ then $x + y \\in P$ and $x \\cdot y \\in P$.\n\nWe write $x > 0$ for $x \\in P$ (read \"$x$ is positive\"), and define the order by\n\n$$a < b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; b - a \\in P, \\qquad a \\le b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a < b \\text{ or } a = b.$$\n\nThus $a > b$ means $b < a$, and $a \\ge b$ means $b \\le a$. An element $x$ with\n$-x \\in P$ (equivalently $x < 0$) is called **negative**.","uses":["2.1","2.2","3.1","3.2","4.1"]}],"derivations":[{"id":"step-1-1","claim":"For the forward implication assume $u = \\sup S$, that is, $u$ is an upper bound of $S$ that is $\\le$ every upper bound of $S$, and let $\\varepsilon > 0$ be arbitrary.","step":"1.1","inputs":["assume-hyp","L1","L2"]},{"id":"step-1-2","claim":"For the converse implication assume that $u$ is an upper bound of $S$ such that for every $\\varepsilon > 0$ there exists $s \\in S$ with $u - \\varepsilon < s$, and let $u'$ be an arbitrary upper bound of $S$.","step":"1.2","inputs":["assume-hyp"]},{"id":"step-2-1","claim":"Since $u - (u - \\varepsilon) = \\varepsilon > 0$, we have $u - \\varepsilon < u$.","step":"2.1","inputs":["step 1.1","L3","algebra"]},{"id":"step-2-2","claim":"By totality either $u \\le u'$ or $u' < u$; in the second case put $\\varepsilon_0 := u - u'$, so that $\\varepsilon_0 > 0$ and $u - \\varepsilon_0 = u'$.","step":"2.2","inputs":["step 1.2","L3","algebra"]},{"id":"step-3-1","claim":"The element $u - \\varepsilon$ is not an upper bound of $S$: if it were, the leastness of $u$ among upper bounds would give $u \\le u - \\varepsilon$, which contradicts $u - \\varepsilon < u$ by trichotomy.","step":"3.1","inputs":["step 2.1","step 1.1","L1","L3"]},{"id":"step-3-2","claim":"In that second case the hypothesis applied to $\\varepsilon_0$ yields $s_0 \\in S$ with $u' = u - \\varepsilon_0 < s_0$, so $s_0 \\le u'$ fails, contradicting that $u'$ is an upper bound of $S$; the second case is therefore impossible and $u \\le u'$.","step":"3.2","inputs":["step 2.2","step 1.2","L3"]},{"id":"step-4-1","claim":"Failing to be an upper bound of $S$ means precisely that some $s \\in S$ does not satisfy $s \\le u - \\varepsilon$, and by totality that says $u - \\varepsilon < s$; since $\\varepsilon > 0$ was arbitrary, the forward implication is proved.","step":"4.1","inputs":["step 3.1","L3"]},{"id":"step-4-2","claim":"Since $u'$ was an arbitrary upper bound of $S$, we get $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$; as $u$ is itself an upper bound, $u$ is a least upper bound of $S$, hence $u = \\sup S$ by uniqueness, which proves the converse implication.","step":"4.2","inputs":["step 3.2","step 1.2","L1","L2"]},{"id":"step-5-1","claim":"Both implications hold, so for an upper bound $u$ of a nonempty set $S$ bounded above, $u = \\sup S$ if and only if for every $\\varepsilon > 0$ there is $s \\in S$ with $u - \\varepsilon < s$.","step":"5.1","inputs":["step 4.1","step 4.2"]}],"routine_steps":[],"boundaries":[{"case":"empty","status":"checked","evidence":"The statement assumes S nonempty and step 1.1 carries that hypothesis into the forward direction."},{"case":"zero","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 2.1 and 2.2 explicitly require epsilon>0, excluding the zero perturbation."},{"case":"one","status":"not_applicable","reason":"No unit index or unit-parameter boundary requires a separate case in this statement or proof."},{"case":"degenerate","status":"checked","evidence":"step 2.2 includes the equality case u=u-prime in the u<=u-prime branch."},{"case":"endpoints","status":"not_applicable","reason":"No interval endpoint or finite-range endpoint occurs in this statement or proof."},{"case":"nonempty-choice","status":"checked","evidence":"step 4.1 obtains an element of S from failure of the universal upper-bound condition; the statement already asserts S is nonempty."},{"case":"iff-forward","status":"checked","evidence":"step 4.1 completes the supremum-to-epsilon implication."},{"case":"iff-reverse","status":"checked","evidence":"step 4.2 completes the epsilon-to-least-upper-bound implication."}],"finite_smoke":[],"risk_review":{"status":"pending","reviewer":null,"notes":"Reserved for the independent Alpha proof-refuter if risk routing selects this item after A3."}},"lem-inf-epsilon":{"citations":[{"fact":"L1","source":"lem-reflection","source_section":"Statement","quote":"For $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ write $-S := \\{-s : s \\in S\\}$. Then $-(-S) = S$,\nand $S$ is nonempty if and only if $-S$ is nonempty. Moreover, for all\n$u, \\ell \\in \\mathbb{R}$:\n\n1. $u$ is an upper bound of $S$ if and only if $-u$ is a lower bound of $-S$;\n2. $\\ell$ is a lower bound of $S$ if and only if $-\\ell$ is an upper bound of $-S$.\n\nConsequently $S$ is bounded above if and only if $-S$ is bounded below, $S$ is\nbounded below if and only if $-S$ is bounded above, and $S$ is bounded if and\nonly if $-S$ is bounded ([[def-bounded-set]]).","uses":["1.1","1.2","2.1","4.1"]},{"fact":"L1","source":"def-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"A **field** is a set $F$ equipped with two binary operations $+$ (addition) and\n$\\cdot$ (multiplication) and two distinguished elements $0 \\ne 1$ of $F$,\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(A)** $(F, +)$ is an abelian group with identity $0$: addition is\n  associative and commutative, $x + 0 = x$ for all $x$, and every $x$ has an\n  additive inverse $-x$ with $x + (-x) = 0$.\n- **(M)** Multiplication is associative and commutative **on all of $F$**, and\n  $x \\cdot 1 = x$ for every $x \\in F$; moreover $(F \\setminus \\{0\\}, \\cdot)$ is an\n  abelian group with identity $1$, so every $x \\ne 0$ has a multiplicative inverse\n  $x^{-1}$ with $x \\cdot x^{-1} = 1$.\n- **(D)** Multiplication distributes over addition: $x \\cdot (y + z) = x\\cdot y + x\\cdot z$.\n\nWe write $x - y := x + (-y)$ and $x/y := x \\cdot y^{-1}$ (for $y \\ne 0$), and\nabbreviate $x \\cdot y$ as $xy$.","uses":["1.1","1.2","2.1","4.1"]},{"fact":"L1","source":"lem-of-inverse-unique","source_section":"Statement","quote":"In a field $F$ ([[def-field]]) the additive identity, the multiplicative\nidentity, each additive inverse, and each multiplicative inverse are unique.\nHence the notations $0$, $1$, $-x$, and $x^{-1}$ denote well-defined elements, as\nthe field definition and its consequences assume.","uses":["1.1","1.2","2.1","4.1"]},{"fact":"L1","source":"lem-of-add-order","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) with positive cone $P$, and\nlet $a, b, c, d \\in F$.\n\n1. **Translation invariance.** If $a < b$ then $a + c < b + c$.\n2. **Adding inequalities.** If $a < b$ and $c < d$ then $a + c < b + d$.","uses":["1.1","1.2","2.1","4.1"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"thm-infimum-property","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ be nonempty and bounded below. Then $S$ has a\ngreatest lower bound in $\\mathbb{R}$ ([[def-infimum]]), and it is given by\n\n$$\\inf S = -\\sup(-S), \\qquad \\text{where } -S = \\{-s : s \\in S\\}.$$\n\nIn particular the complete ordered field $\\mathbb{R}$ has the\ngreatest-lower-bound property, which is therefore not an extra axiom: it is a\nconsequence of the least-upper-bound property.","uses":["2.1"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"lem-sup-epsilon","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ be nonempty and bounded above, and let $u$ be an\nupper bound of $S$ ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]). Then\n\n$$u = \\sup S \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow \\quad \\text{for every } \\varepsilon > 0 \\text{ there exists } s \\in S \\text{ with } u - \\varepsilon < s.$$\n\nIn words: among the upper bounds of $S$, the supremum is exactly the one that\ncannot be lowered by any positive amount and still bound $S$.","uses":["3.1"]}],"derivations":[{"id":"step-1-1","claim":"Since $S$ is nonempty and bounded below and $\\ell$ is a lower bound of $S$, the set $T$ is nonempty and $-\\ell$ is an upper bound of $T$, so $T$ is nonempty and bounded above.","step":"1.1","inputs":["given","L1"]},{"id":"step-1-2","claim":"For $s \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and $\\varepsilon > 0$, negation turns the inequality $(-\\ell) - \\varepsilon < -s$ into $s < \\ell + \\varepsilon$ and back, because $-(-s) = s$ and $-\\big((-\\ell) - \\varepsilon\\big) = \\ell + \\varepsilon$.","step":"1.2","inputs":["L1","algebra"]},{"id":"step-2-1","claim":"By [L2] the infimum of $S$ exists and equals $-\\sup T$; hence $\\ell = \\inf S$ holds if and only if $\\ell = -\\sup T$, which by negating both sides holds if and only if $-\\ell = \\sup T$.","step":"2.1","inputs":["step 1.1","L2","L1"]},{"id":"step-3-1","claim":"Applying [L3] to the nonempty bounded-above set $T$ and its upper bound $-\\ell$: $-\\ell = \\sup T$ if and only if for every $\\varepsilon > 0$ there is $t \\in T$ with $(-\\ell) - \\varepsilon < t$.","step":"3.1","inputs":["step 1.1","step 2.1","L3"]},{"id":"step-4-1","claim":"The elements of $T$ are exactly the $-s$ with $s \\in S$, so by 1.2 the condition \"there is $t \\in T$ with $(-\\ell) - \\varepsilon < t$\" is equivalent to \"there is $s \\in S$ with $s < \\ell + \\varepsilon$\".","step":"4.1","inputs":["step 1.2","step 3.1","L1"]},{"id":"step-5-1","claim":"Chaining the equivalences, $\\ell = \\inf S$ if and only if for every $\\varepsilon > 0$ there is $s \\in S$ with $s < \\ell + \\varepsilon$.","step":"5.1","inputs":["step 2.1","step 3.1","step 4.1"]}],"routine_steps":[],"boundaries":[{"case":"empty","status":"checked","evidence":"The statement assumes S nonempty and step 1.1 proves its reflection T is nonempty."},{"case":"zero","status":"checked","evidence":"step 1.2 treats every epsilon>0 and the zero-centered reflection algebra explicitly."},{"case":"one","status":"not_applicable","reason":"No unit index or unit-parameter boundary requires a separate case in this statement or proof."},{"case":"degenerate","status":"checked","evidence":"step 2.1 verifies equality survives double negation."},{"case":"endpoints","status":"not_applicable","reason":"No interval endpoint or finite-range endpoint occurs in this statement or proof."},{"case":"nonempty-choice","status":"checked","evidence":"step 3.1 invokes the sourced epsilon characterization only after step 1.1 establishes all nonempty/bounded hypotheses."},{"case":"iff-forward","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 2.1-4.1 transport the forward implication from the supremum characterization."},{"case":"iff-reverse","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 2.1-5.1 transport the reverse implication back to S."}],"finite_smoke":[],"risk_review":{"status":"pending","reviewer":null,"notes":"Reserved for the independent Alpha proof-refuter if risk routing selects this item after A3."}},"lem-max-is-sup":{"citations":[{"fact":"L1","source":"def-max-min","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ and $m \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n- $m$ is a **maximum** (or *greatest element*) of $S$ if $m \\in S$ and $s \\le m$\n  for every $s \\in S$.\n- $m$ is a **minimum** (or *least element*) of $S$ if $m \\in S$ and $m \\le s$\n  for every $s \\in S$.\n\nA set has at most one maximum: if $m_1$ and $m_2$ are both maxima then\n$m_1 \\in S$ gives $m_1 \\le m_2$ and $m_2 \\in S$ gives $m_2 \\le m_1$, so\n$m_1 = m_2$ by antisymmetry of the order, which is immediate from the trichotomy\naxiom of an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]]).\nThe same argument applies to minima, so we may write $\\max S$ and $\\min S$.","uses":["1.1","2.2","3.1"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"lem-sup-unique","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$. If $u_1$ and $u_2$ are both least upper bounds of\n$S$ ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]), then $u_1 = u_2$. If $\\ell_1$ and $\\ell_2$\nare both greatest lower bounds of $S$ ([[def-infimum]]), then\n$\\ell_1 = \\ell_2$.\n\nConsequently a set has at most one supremum and at most one infimum, and the\nnotations $\\sup S$ and $\\inf S$ denote single, well-determined real numbers\nwhenever they exist.","uses":["1.1","1.2","2.1","2.2","3.1"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"def-complete-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.","uses":["1.1","1.2","2.1","2.2","3.1"]}],"derivations":[{"id":"step-1-1","claim":"For claim 1 assume $S$ has a maximum $m$: then $m \\in S$ and $s \\le m$ for every $s \\in S$, so $m$ is in particular an upper bound of $S$.","step":"1.1","inputs":["assume-hyp","L1","L2"]},{"id":"step-1-2","claim":"For claim 2 assume $\\sup S$ exists and lies in $S$, and write $u := \\sup S \\in S$.","step":"1.2","inputs":["assume-hyp","L2"]},{"id":"step-2-1","claim":"Let $u'$ be an arbitrary upper bound of $S$; since $m \\in S$, the defining property of an upper bound applied to the element $m$ gives $m \\le u'$.","step":"2.1","inputs":["step 1.1","L2"]},{"id":"step-2-2","claim":"Since $u = \\sup S$ is an upper bound of $S$ we have $s \\le u$ for every $s \\in S$, and by assumption $u \\in S$; these are exactly the two requirements for $u$ to be a maximum of $S$, so $\\max S$ exists and equals $u = \\sup S$ by uniqueness of the maximum, proving claim 2.","step":"2.2","inputs":["step 1.2","L1","L2"]},{"id":"step-3-1","claim":"Thus $m$ is an upper bound of $S$ with $m \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$, which is exactly the definition of a least upper bound; hence $\\sup S$ exists and, by uniqueness of the least upper bound, $\\sup S = m = \\max S$, proving claim 1.","step":"3.1","inputs":["step 1.1","step 2.1","L1","L2"]},{"id":"step-4-1","claim":"Combining the two claims: when $\\sup S$ exists, $\\sup S \\in S$ holds if and only if $S$ has a maximum, and in that case $\\sup S = \\max S$.","step":"4.1","inputs":["step 2.2","step 3.1"]}],"routine_steps":[],"boundaries":[{"case":"empty","status":"checked","evidence":"The statement assumes S nonempty; steps 1.1 and 1.2 each begin under the relevant attainment hypothesis."},{"case":"zero","status":"not_applicable","reason":"No zero index, zero divisor, or zero parameter boundary occurs in this statement or proof."},{"case":"one","status":"not_applicable","reason":"No unit index or unit-parameter boundary requires a separate case in this statement or proof."},{"case":"degenerate","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 2.1 and 2.2 allow the candidate bound to equal every element, including singleton sets."},{"case":"endpoints","status":"not_applicable","reason":"No interval endpoint or finite-range endpoint occurs in this statement or proof."},{"case":"nonempty-choice","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The proof makes no choice from a set whose nonemptiness requires a separate discharge."},{"case":"iff-forward","status":"checked","evidence":"step 3.1 proves maximum implies an attained supremum."},{"case":"iff-reverse","status":"checked","evidence":"step 2.2 proves an attained supremum is a maximum."}],"finite_smoke":[],"risk_review":{"status":"pending","reviewer":null,"notes":"Reserved for the independent Alpha proof-refuter if risk routing selects this item after A3."}},"lem-finite-set-has-max":{"citations":[{"fact":"L1","source":"def-max-min","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ and $m \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n- $m$ is a **maximum** (or *greatest element*) of $S$ if $m \\in S$ and $s \\le m$\n  for every $s \\in S$.\n- $m$ is a **minimum** (or *least element*) of $S$ if $m \\in S$ and $m \\le s$\n  for every $s \\in S$.\n\nA set has at most one maximum: if $m_1$ and $m_2$ are both maxima then\n$m_1 \\in S$ gives $m_1 \\le m_2$ and $m_2 \\in S$ gives $m_2 \\le m_1$, so\n$m_1 = m_2$ by antisymmetry of the order, which is immediate from the trichotomy\naxiom of an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]]).\nThe same argument applies to minima, so we may write $\\max S$ and $\\min S$.","uses":["1.1","2.1","3.1","3.2"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"thm-induction-principle","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{N}$. If $0 \\in S$ and $\\sigma(n) \\in S$ whenever $n \\in S$, then $S = \\mathbb{N}$. Equivalently (property form): if a property $P$ satisfies $P(0)$ and ($P(n) \\Rightarrow P(\\sigma(n))$) for all $n$, then $P(n)$ holds for all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$. This is the induction principle, the basis of proof by induction.","uses":["5.1"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"def-nat-addition","source_section":"Definition","quote":"**Addition** $+ : \\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{N}$ is defined by\nrecursion on the second argument: for each fixed $m \\in \\mathbb{N}$,\n\n$$m + 0 = m, \\qquad m + \\sigma(n) = \\sigma(m + n).$$","uses":["5.1"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"def-complete-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.","uses":["1.1","3.1","3.2"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"def-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"An **ordered field** is a field $F$ ([[def-field]]) together with a subset\n$P \\subseteq F$, the set of **positive** elements (the *positive cone*),\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(O1) Trichotomy.** For each $x \\in F$, exactly one of the following holds:\n  $x \\in P$, or $x = 0$, or $-x \\in P$.\n- **(O2) Closure.** If $x, y \\in P$ then $x + y \\in P$ and $x \\cdot y \\in P$.\n\nWe write $x > 0$ for $x \\in P$ (read \"$x$ is positive\"), and define the order by\n\n$$a < b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; b - a \\in P, \\qquad a \\le b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a < b \\text{ or } a = b.$$\n\nThus $a > b$ means $b < a$, and $a \\ge b$ means $b \\le a$. An element $x$ with\n$-x \\in P$ (equivalently $x < 0$) is called **negative**.","uses":["1.1","3.1","3.2"]}],"derivations":[{"id":"step-1-1","claim":"Base case: $F_0 = \\{a_0\\}$, and $a_0 \\in F_0$ with $a_0 \\le a_0$ by reflexivity, so $a_0$ is both a maximum and a minimum of $F_0$; hence $P(0)$ holds.","step":"1.1","inputs":["base","A1","L1","L3"]},{"id":"step-1-2","claim":"Inductive hypothesis: fix $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ and assume $P(n)$, that is, for all reals $a_0, \\dots, a_n$ the set $F_n$ has a maximum and a minimum.","step":"1.2","inputs":["ih","A1"]},{"id":"step-2-1","claim":"Let $a_0, \\dots, a_{n+1} \\in \\mathbb{R}$ be arbitrary; by the inductive hypothesis the set $F_n$ has a maximum $M$ and a minimum $m$, and $F_{n+1} = F_n \\cup \\{a_{n+1}\\}$.","step":"2.1","inputs":["step 1.2","L1"]},{"id":"step-3-1","claim":"By totality at least one of $a_{n+1} \\le M$ and $M \\le a_{n+1}$ holds. If $a_{n+1} \\le M$, then $M \\in F_n \\subseteq F_{n+1}$, every element of $F_n$ is $\\le M$ because $M = \\max F_n$, and $a_{n+1} \\le M$ as well, so $M$ is a maximum of $F_{n+1}$. If $M \\le a_{n+1}$, then $a_{n+1} \\in F_{n+1}$, every $x \\in F_n$ satisfies $x \\le M \\le a_{n+1}$ hence $x \\le a_{n+1}$ by transitivity, and $a_{n+1} \\le a_{n+1}$, so $a_{n+1}$ is a maximum of $F_{n+1}$. Either way $F_{n+1}$ has a maximum.","step":"3.1","inputs":["step 2.1","L1","L3"]},{"id":"step-3-2","claim":"Dually, at least one of $m \\le a_{n+1}$ and $a_{n+1} \\le m$ holds. If $m \\le a_{n+1}$, then $m \\in F_{n+1}$ and every element of $F_{n+1}$ is $\\ge m$, so $m$ is a minimum of $F_{n+1}$. If $a_{n+1} \\le m$, then $a_{n+1} \\in F_{n+1}$ and every $x \\in F_n$ satisfies $a_{n+1} \\le m \\le x$ hence $a_{n+1} \\le x$ by transitivity, so $a_{n+1}$ is a minimum of $F_{n+1}$. Either way $F_{n+1}$ has a minimum.","step":"3.2","inputs":["step 2.1","L1","L3"]},{"id":"step-4-1","claim":"Since $a_0, \\dots, a_{n+1}$ were arbitrary, $F_{n+1}$ has a maximum and a minimum for every such list, that is, $P(n)$ implies $P(n+1)$.","step":"4.1","inputs":["step 3.1","step 3.2","A1"]},{"id":"step-5-1","claim":"The base case and the inductive step give $P(n)$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ by the induction principle; since a nonempty finite subset of $\\mathbb{R}$ is exactly a set of the form $F_n$, every nonempty finite subset of $\\mathbb{R}$ has both a maximum and a minimum.","step":"5.1","inputs":["step 1.1","step 4.1","given","L2","discharge-induction"]}],"routine_steps":[],"boundaries":[{"case":"empty","status":"checked","evidence":"The displayed statement starts with a0, so the represented set is nonempty; step 1.1 checks the singleton base."},{"case":"zero","status":"checked","evidence":"step 1.1 is the n=0 base case."},{"case":"one","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 2.1-4.1 explicitly pass from n entries through the successor n+1 case."},{"case":"degenerate","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 3.1 and 3.2 remain valid when a_(n+1) repeats an earlier value or equals the current extremum."},{"case":"endpoints","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 1.1 and 2.1 anchor the first and newly appended list positions."},{"case":"nonempty-choice","status":"checked","evidence":"step 2.1 uses maxima/minima supplied by the induction hypothesis, not an unlicensed global choice."},{"case":"iff-forward","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a forward implication proof."},{"case":"iff-reverse","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a reverse implication proof."}],"finite_smoke":[],"risk_review":{"status":"pending","reviewer":null,"notes":"Reserved for the independent Alpha proof-refuter if risk routing selects this item after A3."}},"lem-sup-monotone":{"citations":[{"fact":"L1","source":"def-complete-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.","uses":["2.1","3.1","4.1","5.1"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"lem-sup-unique","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$. If $u_1$ and $u_2$ are both least upper bounds of\n$S$ ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]), then $u_1 = u_2$. If $\\ell_1$ and $\\ell_2$\nare both greatest lower bounds of $S$ ([[def-infimum]]), then\n$\\ell_1 = \\ell_2$.\n\nConsequently a set has at most one supremum and at most one infimum, and the\nnotations $\\sup S$ and $\\inf S$ denote single, well-determined real numbers\nwhenever they exist.","uses":["3.1"]}],"derivations":[{"id":"step-1-1","claim":"Since $T$ is bounded above, fix an upper bound $u$ of $T$, so $t \\le u$ for every $t \\in T$.","step":"1.1","inputs":["given","choose"]},{"id":"step-1-2","claim":"By hypothesis $S$ is nonempty and $S \\subseteq T$, so $T$ is nonempty as well.","step":"1.2","inputs":["given"]},{"id":"step-2-1","claim":"Every $s \\in S$ lies in $T$ and therefore satisfies $s \\le u$; hence $u$ is an upper bound of $S$ and $S$ is bounded above.","step":"2.1","inputs":["step 1.1","step 1.2","L1"]},{"id":"step-3-1","claim":"Both $S$ and $T$ are nonempty and bounded above, so by the least-upper-bound property $\\sup S$ and $\\sup T$ exist, each uniquely.","step":"3.1","inputs":["step 1.2","step 2.1","L1","L2"]},{"id":"step-4-1","claim":"As $\\sup T$ is an upper bound of $T$, every $t \\in T$ satisfies $t \\le \\sup T$; since $S \\subseteq T$, every $s \\in S$ satisfies $s \\le \\sup T$, so $\\sup T$ is an upper bound of $S$.","step":"4.1","inputs":["step 3.1","step 1.2","L1"]},{"id":"step-5-1","claim":"The number $\\sup S$ is the least of the upper bounds of $S$, and $\\sup T$ is one of them, hence $\\sup S \\le \\sup T$.","step":"5.1","inputs":["step 4.1","step 3.1","L1"]}],"routine_steps":[],"boundaries":[{"case":"empty","status":"checked","evidence":"step 1.2 derives T nonempty from nonempty S subset T, and step 2.1 preserves nonemptiness of S."},{"case":"zero","status":"not_applicable","reason":"No zero index, zero divisor, or zero parameter boundary occurs in this statement or proof."},{"case":"one","status":"not_applicable","reason":"No unit index or unit-parameter boundary requires a separate case in this statement or proof."},{"case":"degenerate","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 4.1-5.1 also cover S=T, yielding equality as a permitted case."},{"case":"endpoints","status":"not_applicable","reason":"No interval endpoint or finite-range endpoint occurs in this statement or proof."},{"case":"nonempty-choice","status":"checked","evidence":"step 1.1 chooses an upper bound only from the explicit bounded-above hypothesis."},{"case":"iff-forward","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a forward implication proof."},{"case":"iff-reverse","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a reverse implication proof."}],"finite_smoke":[],"risk_review":{"status":"pending","reviewer":null,"notes":"Reserved for the independent Alpha proof-refuter if risk routing selects this item after A3."}},"lem-sup-translate":{"citations":[{"fact":"L1","source":"lem-sup-epsilon","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ be nonempty and bounded above, and let $u$ be an\nupper bound of $S$ ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]). Then\n\n$$u = \\sup S \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow \\quad \\text{for every } \\varepsilon > 0 \\text{ there exists } s \\in S \\text{ with } u - \\varepsilon < s.$$\n\nIn words: among the upper bounds of $S$, the supremum is exactly the one that\ncannot be lowered by any positive amount and still bound $S$.","uses":["2.2","4.1"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"lem-of-add-order","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) with positive cone $P$, and\nlet $a, b, c, d \\in F$.\n\n1. **Translation invariance.** If $a < b$ then $a + c < b + c$.\n2. **Adding inequalities.** If $a < b$ and $c < d$ then $a + c < b + d$.","uses":["2.1","2.2"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"def-complete-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.","uses":["1.1","3.1"]}],"derivations":[{"id":"step-1-1","claim":"Since $S$ is nonempty and bounded above, the least-upper-bound property gives $u := \\sup S$, which is an upper bound of $S$.","step":"1.1","inputs":["given","L3"]},{"id":"step-1-2","claim":"The set $a + S$ is nonempty, because $S$ has an element $s$ and then $a + s \\in a + S$.","step":"1.2","inputs":["given"]},{"id":"step-2-1","claim":"Every $s \\in S$ satisfies $s \\le u$, hence $a + s \\le a + u$; as the elements of $a + S$ are exactly these $a + s$, the number $a + u$ is an upper bound of $a + S$, so $a + S$ is bounded above.","step":"2.1","inputs":["step 1.1","L2"]},{"id":"step-2-2","claim":"Let $\\varepsilon > 0$. Applying the epsilon characterisation to $S$ and its supremum $u$ produces $s \\in S$ with $u - \\varepsilon < s$, and adding $a$ gives $(a + u) - \\varepsilon = a + (u - \\varepsilon) < a + s$, where $a + s \\in a + S$.","step":"2.2","inputs":["step 1.1","L1","L2","algebra"]},{"id":"step-3-1","claim":"The set $a + S$ is nonempty and bounded above, so $\\sup(a + S)$ exists.","step":"3.1","inputs":["step 1.2","step 2.1","L3"]},{"id":"step-4-1","claim":"Now $a + u$ is an upper bound of $a + S$ and for every $\\varepsilon > 0$ some element of $a + S$ exceeds $(a + u) - \\varepsilon$, so the epsilon characterisation applied to $a + S$ gives $\\sup(a + S) = a + u = a + \\sup S$.","step":"4.1","inputs":["step 2.1","step 2.2","step 3.1","L1"]}],"routine_steps":[],"boundaries":[{"case":"empty","status":"checked","evidence":"step 1.2 proves a+S nonempty from the stated nonemptiness of S."},{"case":"zero","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 2.1-2.2 remain valid for translation parameter a=0 and arbitrary epsilon>0."},{"case":"one","status":"not_applicable","reason":"No unit index or unit-parameter boundary requires a separate case in this statement or proof."},{"case":"degenerate","status":"checked","evidence":"step 4.1 allows a=0 and singleton S without an extra distinctness assumption."},{"case":"endpoints","status":"not_applicable","reason":"No interval endpoint or finite-range endpoint occurs in this statement or proof."},{"case":"nonempty-choice","status":"checked","evidence":"step 1.2 selects s only after S is given nonempty, and step 2.2 uses the sourced epsilon witness."},{"case":"iff-forward","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a forward implication proof."},{"case":"iff-reverse","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a reverse implication proof."}],"finite_smoke":[],"risk_review":{"status":"pending","reviewer":null,"notes":"Reserved for the independent Alpha proof-refuter if risk routing selects this item after A3."}},"lem-sup-scale":{"citations":[{"fact":"L1","source":"def-complete-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.","uses":["1.1","3.1","3.2"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"lem-of-sign-rules","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) with positive cone $P$, and\nlet $a, b, c \\in F$.\n\n1. If $a > 0$ and $b > 0$ then $ab > 0$.\n2. If $a > 0$ and $b < 0$ then $ab < 0$.\n3. If $a < 0$ and $b < 0$ then $ab > 0$.\n4. If $c > 0$ then $a < b \\iff ac < bc$.\n5. If $c < 0$ then $a < b \\iff bc < ac$ (multiplication by a negative reverses order).","uses":["2.1","2.2","2.3","2.4"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"lem-sup-epsilon","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ be nonempty and bounded above, and let $u$ be an\nupper bound of $S$ ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]). Then\n\n$$u = \\sup S \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow \\quad \\text{for every } \\varepsilon > 0 \\text{ there exists } s \\in S \\text{ with } u - \\varepsilon < s.$$\n\nIn words: among the upper bounds of $S$, the supremum is exactly the one that\ncannot be lowered by any positive amount and still bound $S$.","uses":["2.3","3.1","3.2"]},{"fact":"L4","source":"thm-infimum-property","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ be nonempty and bounded below. Then $S$ has a\ngreatest lower bound in $\\mathbb{R}$ ([[def-infimum]]), and it is given by\n\n$$\\inf S = -\\sup(-S), \\qquad \\text{where } -S = \\{-s : s \\in S\\}.$$\n\nIn particular the complete ordered field $\\mathbb{R}$ has the\ngreatest-lower-bound property, which is therefore not an extra axiom: it is a\nconsequence of the least-upper-bound property.","uses":["1.2","2.4"]},{"fact":"L4","source":"def-infimum","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ and $\\ell \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then $\\ell$ is a\n**greatest lower bound**, or **infimum**, of $S$ if both of the following hold:\n\n- $\\ell$ is a lower bound of $S$ ([[def-bounded-set]]), that is, $\\ell \\le s$ for\n  every $s \\in S$;\n- $\\ell' \\le \\ell$ for every lower bound $\\ell'$ of $S$.\n\nWritten out in one line:\n\n$$\\ell \\text{ is an infimum of } S \\iff \\big[(\\forall s \\in S)\\, \\ell \\le s\\big] \\text{ and } \\big[(\\forall \\ell' \\in \\mathbb{R})\\, \\big((\\forall s \\in S)\\, \\ell' \\le s\\big) \\Rightarrow \\ell' \\le \\ell\\big].$$\n\nAn infimum, when it exists, is unique ([[lem-sup-unique]]), so we may write\n$\\inf S$ for it.","uses":["1.2","2.4"]},{"fact":"L5","source":"def-complete-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.","uses":["2.4","4.1"]},{"fact":"L5","source":"def-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"An **ordered field** is a field $F$ ([[def-field]]) together with a subset\n$P \\subseteq F$, the set of **positive** elements (the *positive cone*),\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(O1) Trichotomy.** For each $x \\in F$, exactly one of the following holds:\n  $x \\in P$, or $x = 0$, or $-x \\in P$.\n- **(O2) Closure.** If $x, y \\in P$ then $x + y \\in P$ and $x \\cdot y \\in P$.\n\nWe write $x > 0$ for $x \\in P$ (read \"$x$ is positive\"), and define the order by\n\n$$a < b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; b - a \\in P, \\qquad a \\le b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a < b \\text{ or } a = b.$$\n\nThus $a > b$ means $b < a$, and $a \\ge b$ means $b \\le a$. An element $x$ with\n$-x \\in P$ (equivalently $x < 0$) is called **negative**.","uses":["2.4","4.1"]},{"fact":"L6","source":"def-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"A **field** is a set $F$ equipped with two binary operations $+$ (addition) and\n$\\cdot$ (multiplication) and two distinguished elements $0 \\ne 1$ of $F$,\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(A)** $(F, +)$ is an abelian group with identity $0$: addition is\n  associative and commutative, $x + 0 = x$ for all $x$, and every $x$ has an\n  additive inverse $-x$ with $x + (-x) = 0$.\n- **(M)** Multiplication is associative and commutative **on all of $F$**, and\n  $x \\cdot 1 = x$ for every $x \\in F$; moreover $(F \\setminus \\{0\\}, \\cdot)$ is an\n  abelian group with identity $1$, so every $x \\ne 0$ has a multiplicative inverse\n  $x^{-1}$ with $x \\cdot x^{-1} = 1$.\n- **(D)** Multiplication distributes over addition: $x \\cdot (y + z) = x\\cdot y + x\\cdot z$.\n\nWe write $x - y := x + (-y)$ and $x/y := x \\cdot y^{-1}$ (for $y \\ne 0$), and\nabbreviate $x \\cdot y$ as $xy$.","uses":["2.3","2.4"]},{"fact":"L6","source":"lem-of-zero-mult","source_section":"Statement","quote":"In any field $F$ ([[def-field]]), for every $a \\in F$ we have $0 \\cdot a = 0$.","uses":["2.3","2.4"]},{"fact":"L6","source":"lem-of-add-order","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) with positive cone $P$, and\nlet $a, b, c, d \\in F$.\n\n1. **Translation invariance.** If $a < b$ then $a + c < b + c$.\n2. **Adding inequalities.** If $a < b$ and $c < d$ then $a + c < b + d$.","uses":["2.3","2.4"]}],"derivations":[{"id":"step-1-1","claim":"Case $c > 0$, in which $S$ is nonempty and bounded above: the least-upper-bound property supplies $u := \\sup S$, an upper bound of $S$ that is $\\le$ every upper bound of $S$.","step":"1.1","inputs":["assume-case pos","L1"]},{"id":"step-1-2","claim":"Case $c < 0$, in which $S$ is nonempty and bounded below: $S$ has a greatest lower bound, and we set $\\ell := \\inf S$, a lower bound of $S$ with $\\ell' \\le \\ell$ for every lower bound $\\ell'$ of $S$.","step":"1.2","inputs":["assume-case neg","L4"]},{"id":"step-2-1","claim":"In the case $c > 0$, every $s \\in S$ satisfies $s \\le u$, hence $sc \\le uc$, that is $cs \\le cu$; since the elements of $cS$ are exactly these $cs$ and $S \\ne \\emptyset$, the set $cS$ is nonempty and $cu$ is an upper bound of it.","step":"2.1","inputs":["step 1.1","L2"]},{"id":"step-2-2","claim":"In the case $c < 0$, every $s \\in S$ satisfies $\\ell \\le s$, and multiplying by the negative $c$ reverses this to $sc \\le \\ell c$, that is $cs \\le c\\ell$; so $cS$ is nonempty and $c\\ell$ is an upper bound of it.","step":"2.2","inputs":["step 1.2","L2"]},{"id":"step-2-3","claim":"In the case $c > 0$, let $\\varepsilon > 0$ and put $\\delta := \\varepsilon c^{-1}$, so that $\\delta c = \\varepsilon$; from $0 \\cdot c = 0 < \\varepsilon = \\delta c$ and $c > 0$ the equivalence form of [L2] gives $\\delta > 0$, so the epsilon characterisation applied to $S$ and $u$ yields $s \\in S$ with $u - \\delta < s$, and multiplying that inequality by $c > 0$ gives $cu - \\varepsilon = (u - \\delta)c < sc = cs$, an element of $cS$.","step":"2.3","inputs":["step 1.1","L2","L3","L6","algebra"]},{"id":"step-2-4","claim":"In the case $c < 0$, let $\\varepsilon > 0$ and put $\\delta := -\\varepsilon c^{-1}$, so that $\\delta c = -\\varepsilon < 0 = 0 \\cdot c$, which for the negative multiplier $c$ gives $\\delta > 0$ by [L2]; then $\\ell < \\ell + \\delta$, and $\\ell + \\delta$ cannot be a lower bound of $S$, since greatestness of $\\ell$ would force $\\ell + \\delta \\le \\ell$ and hence $\\delta \\le 0$; so some $s \\in S$ fails $\\ell + \\delta \\le s$, which by trichotomy means $s < \\ell + \\delta$, and multiplying by $c < 0$ reverses it to $c\\ell - \\varepsilon = (\\ell + \\delta)c < sc = cs$, an element of $cS$.","step":"2.4","inputs":["step 1.2","L2","L4","L5","L6","algebra"]},{"id":"step-3-1","claim":"In the case $c > 0$, the set $cS$ is nonempty and bounded above by $cu$, and for every $\\varepsilon > 0$ some element of $cS$ exceeds $cu - \\varepsilon$, so $\\sup(cS)$ exists and the epsilon characterisation identifies it: $\\sup(cS) = cu = c \\sup S$, which is claim 1.","step":"3.1","inputs":["step 2.1","step 2.3","L1","L3"]},{"id":"step-3-2","claim":"In the case $c < 0$, the set $cS$ is nonempty and bounded above by $c\\ell$, and for every $\\varepsilon > 0$ some element of $cS$ exceeds $c\\ell - \\varepsilon$, so $\\sup(cS)$ exists and equals $c\\ell = c \\inf S$, which is claim 2.","step":"3.2","inputs":["step 2.2","step 2.4","L1","L3"]},{"id":"step-4-1","claim":"A nonzero $c$ satisfies exactly one of $c > 0$ and $c < 0$, so the two cases are mutually exclusive and together exhaust the hypothesis $c \\ne 0$, and each has been settled; both claims therefore hold.","step":"4.1","inputs":["step 3.1","step 3.2","L5","cases-exhaustive"]}],"routine_steps":[],"boundaries":[{"case":"empty","status":"checked","evidence":"The statement assumes S nonempty and steps 2.1-2.2 explicitly preserve nonemptiness of cS."},{"case":"zero","status":"checked","evidence":"The statement excludes c=0; step 4.1 uses trichotomy to exhaust exactly c>0 and c<0, while epsilon remains strictly positive in steps 2.3-2.4."},{"case":"one","status":"checked","evidence":"The c=1 case lies in the positive branch and is covered by steps 2.1, 2.3 and 3.1."},{"case":"degenerate","status":"checked","evidence":"step 4.1 explicitly discharges the only excluded scalar degeneracy c=0."},{"case":"endpoints","status":"not_applicable","reason":"No interval endpoint or finite-range endpoint occurs in this statement or proof."},{"case":"nonempty-choice","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 2.3 and 2.4 obtain witnesses only from the established supremum/infimum characterizations."},{"case":"iff-forward","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a forward implication proof."},{"case":"iff-reverse","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a reverse implication proof."}],"finite_smoke":[],"risk_review":{"status":"pending","reviewer":null,"notes":"Reserved for the independent Alpha proof-refuter if risk routing selects this item after A3."}},"lem-sup-sum":{"citations":[{"fact":"L1","source":"lem-sup-epsilon","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ be nonempty and bounded above, and let $u$ be an\nupper bound of $S$ ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]). Then\n\n$$u = \\sup S \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow \\quad \\text{for every } \\varepsilon > 0 \\text{ there exists } s \\in S \\text{ with } u - \\varepsilon < s.$$\n\nIn words: among the upper bounds of $S$, the supremum is exactly the one that\ncannot be lowered by any positive amount and still bound $S$.","uses":["2.2","4.1"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"lem-of-add-order","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) with positive cone $P$, and\nlet $a, b, c, d \\in F$.\n\n1. **Translation invariance.** If $a < b$ then $a + c < b + c$.\n2. **Adding inequalities.** If $a < b$ and $c < d$ then $a + c < b + d$.","uses":["2.1","2.2"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"def-complete-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.","uses":["1.1","3.1"]},{"fact":"L4","source":"cor-of-one-positive","source_section":"Statement","quote":"In any ordered field $F$ with positive cone $P$, the multiplicative identity\nsatisfies $0 < 1$; that is, $1 \\in P$.","uses":["2.2"]},{"fact":"L4","source":"def-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"An **ordered field** is a field $F$ ([[def-field]]) together with a subset\n$P \\subseteq F$, the set of **positive** elements (the *positive cone*),\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(O1) Trichotomy.** For each $x \\in F$, exactly one of the following holds:\n  $x \\in P$, or $x = 0$, or $-x \\in P$.\n- **(O2) Closure.** If $x, y \\in P$ then $x + y \\in P$ and $x \\cdot y \\in P$.\n\nWe write $x > 0$ for $x \\in P$ (read \"$x$ is positive\"), and define the order by\n\n$$a < b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; b - a \\in P, \\qquad a \\le b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a < b \\text{ or } a = b.$$\n\nThus $a > b$ means $b < a$, and $a \\ge b$ means $b \\le a$. An element $x$ with\n$-x \\in P$ (equivalently $x < 0$) is called **negative**.","uses":["2.2"]},{"fact":"L4","source":"def-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"A **field** is a set $F$ equipped with two binary operations $+$ (addition) and\n$\\cdot$ (multiplication) and two distinguished elements $0 \\ne 1$ of $F$,\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(A)** $(F, +)$ is an abelian group with identity $0$: addition is\n  associative and commutative, $x + 0 = x$ for all $x$, and every $x$ has an\n  additive inverse $-x$ with $x + (-x) = 0$.\n- **(M)** Multiplication is associative and commutative **on all of $F$**, and\n  $x \\cdot 1 = x$ for every $x \\in F$; moreover $(F \\setminus \\{0\\}, \\cdot)$ is an\n  abelian group with identity $1$, so every $x \\ne 0$ has a multiplicative inverse\n  $x^{-1}$ with $x \\cdot x^{-1} = 1$.\n- **(D)** Multiplication distributes over addition: $x \\cdot (y + z) = x\\cdot y + x\\cdot z$.\n\nWe write $x - y := x + (-y)$ and $x/y := x \\cdot y^{-1}$ (for $y \\ne 0$), and\nabbreviate $x \\cdot y$ as $xy$.","uses":["2.2"]},{"fact":"L4","source":"lem-of-zero-mult","source_section":"Statement","quote":"In any field $F$ ([[def-field]]), for every $a \\in F$ we have $0 \\cdot a = 0$.","uses":["2.2"]},{"fact":"L4","source":"lem-of-sign-rules","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) with positive cone $P$, and\nlet $a, b, c \\in F$.\n\n1. If $a > 0$ and $b > 0$ then $ab > 0$.\n2. If $a > 0$ and $b < 0$ then $ab < 0$.\n3. If $a < 0$ and $b < 0$ then $ab > 0$.\n4. If $c > 0$ then $a < b \\iff ac < bc$.\n5. If $c < 0$ then $a < b \\iff bc < ac$ (multiplication by a negative reverses order).","uses":["2.2"]}],"derivations":[{"id":"step-1-1","claim":"Both $S$ and $T$ are nonempty and bounded above, so the least-upper-bound property supplies $u := \\sup S$ and $v := \\sup T$, upper bounds of $S$ and of $T$ respectively.","step":"1.1","inputs":["given","L3"]},{"id":"step-1-2","claim":"The sumset $S + T$ is nonempty: picking $s \\in S$ and $t \\in T$, which is possible since both sets are nonempty, gives $s + t \\in S + T$.","step":"1.2","inputs":["given"]},{"id":"step-2-1","claim":"For $s \\in S$ and $t \\in T$ we have $s \\le u$ and $t \\le v$, and adding these inequalities gives $s + t \\le u + v$; since every element of $S + T$ has this form, $u + v$ is an upper bound of $S + T$, so $S + T$ is bounded above.","step":"2.1","inputs":["step 1.1","L2"]},{"id":"step-2-2","claim":"Let $\\varepsilon > 0$ and put $\\eta := \\varepsilon \\cdot 2^{-1}$, so that $\\eta \\cdot 2 = \\varepsilon$ and $\\eta + \\eta = \\eta(1 + 1) = \\varepsilon$; from $0 \\cdot 2 = 0 < \\varepsilon = \\eta \\cdot 2$ and $2 > 0$ we get $\\eta > 0$, so the epsilon characterisation applied to $S$ with $u$ and to $T$ with $v$ produces $s \\in S$ with $u - \\eta < s$ and $t \\in T$ with $v - \\eta < t$, and adding these strict inequalities gives $(u + v) - \\varepsilon = (u - \\eta) + (v - \\eta) < s + t$, an element of $S + T$.","step":"2.2","inputs":["step 1.1","L1","L2","L4","algebra"]},{"id":"step-3-1","claim":"The set $S + T$ is nonempty and bounded above, so $\\sup(S + T)$ exists.","step":"3.1","inputs":["step 1.2","step 2.1","L3"]},{"id":"step-4-1","claim":"Now $u + v$ is an upper bound of $S + T$ and for every $\\varepsilon > 0$ some element of $S + T$ exceeds $(u + v) - \\varepsilon$, so the epsilon characterisation applied to $S + T$ gives $\\sup(S + T) = u + v = \\sup S + \\sup T$.","step":"4.1","inputs":["step 2.1","step 2.2","step 3.1","L1"]}],"routine_steps":[],"boundaries":[{"case":"empty","status":"checked","evidence":"The statement assumes both sets nonempty and step 1.2 explicitly proves the sumset nonempty."},{"case":"zero","status":"checked","evidence":"step 2.2 proves eta=epsilon/2 is positive before using it."},{"case":"one","status":"checked","evidence":"step 2.2 derives positivity and invertibility of 2 from 0<1 rather than assuming a characteristic boundary."},{"case":"degenerate","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 2.1-4.1 also cover singleton or equal summand sets."},{"case":"endpoints","status":"not_applicable","reason":"No interval endpoint or finite-range endpoint occurs in this statement or proof."},{"case":"nonempty-choice","status":"checked","evidence":"step 1.2 chooses one element from each explicitly nonempty set, and step 2.2 uses two licensed epsilon witnesses."},{"case":"iff-forward","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a forward implication proof."},{"case":"iff-reverse","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a reverse implication proof."}],"finite_smoke":[],"risk_review":{"status":"pending","reviewer":null,"notes":"Reserved for the independent Alpha proof-refuter if risk routing selects this item after A3."}},"fs-sup-belongs-to-set":{"citations":[{"fact":"L1","source":"lem-sup-epsilon","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ be nonempty and bounded above, and let $u$ be an\nupper bound of $S$ ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]). Then\n\n$$u = \\sup S \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow \\quad \\text{for every } \\varepsilon > 0 \\text{ there exists } s \\in S \\text{ with } u - \\varepsilon < s.$$\n\nIn words: among the upper bounds of $S$, the supremum is exactly the one that\ncannot be lowered by any positive amount and still bound $S$.","uses":["3.1"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"def-max-min","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ and $m \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n- $m$ is a **maximum** (or *greatest element*) of $S$ if $m \\in S$ and $s \\le m$\n  for every $s \\in S$.\n- $m$ is a **minimum** (or *least element*) of $S$ if $m \\in S$ and $m \\le s$\n  for every $s \\in S$.\n\nA set has at most one maximum: if $m_1$ and $m_2$ are both maxima then\n$m_1 \\in S$ gives $m_1 \\le m_2$ and $m_2 \\in S$ gives $m_2 \\le m_1$, so\n$m_1 = m_2$ by antisymmetry of the order, which is immediate from the trichotomy\naxiom of an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]]).\nThe same argument applies to minima, so we may write $\\max S$ and $\\min S$.","uses":["2.3"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"def-complete-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.","uses":["1.1","1.2","1.3","2.1","2.2","2.3"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"def-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"An **ordered field** is a field $F$ ([[def-field]]) together with a subset\n$P \\subseteq F$, the set of **positive** elements (the *positive cone*),\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(O1) Trichotomy.** For each $x \\in F$, exactly one of the following holds:\n  $x \\in P$, or $x = 0$, or $-x \\in P$.\n- **(O2) Closure.** If $x, y \\in P$ then $x + y \\in P$ and $x \\cdot y \\in P$.\n\nWe write $x > 0$ for $x \\in P$ (read \"$x$ is positive\"), and define the order by\n\n$$a < b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; b - a \\in P, \\qquad a \\le b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a < b \\text{ or } a = b.$$\n\nThus $a > b$ means $b < a$, and $a \\ge b$ means $b \\le a$. An element $x$ with\n$-x \\in P$ (equivalently $x < 0$) is called **negative**.","uses":["1.1","1.2","1.3","2.1","2.2","2.3"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"lem-of-add-order","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) with positive cone $P$, and\nlet $a, b, c, d \\in F$.\n\n1. **Translation invariance.** If $a < b$ then $a + c < b + c$.\n2. **Adding inequalities.** If $a < b$ and $c < d$ then $a + c < b + d$.","uses":["1.1","1.2","1.3","2.1","2.2","2.3"]},{"fact":"L4","source":"cor-of-one-positive","source_section":"Statement","quote":"In any ordered field $F$ with positive cone $P$, the multiplicative identity\nsatisfies $0 < 1$; that is, $1 \\in P$.","uses":["1.1","1.3","2.3"]},{"fact":"L4","source":"def-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"An **ordered field** is a field $F$ ([[def-field]]) together with a subset\n$P \\subseteq F$, the set of **positive** elements (the *positive cone*),\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(O1) Trichotomy.** For each $x \\in F$, exactly one of the following holds:\n  $x \\in P$, or $x = 0$, or $-x \\in P$.\n- **(O2) Closure.** If $x, y \\in P$ then $x + y \\in P$ and $x \\cdot y \\in P$.\n\nWe write $x > 0$ for $x \\in P$ (read \"$x$ is positive\"), and define the order by\n\n$$a < b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; b - a \\in P, \\qquad a \\le b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a < b \\text{ or } a = b.$$\n\nThus $a > b$ means $b < a$, and $a \\ge b$ means $b \\le a$. An element $x$ with\n$-x \\in P$ (equivalently $x < 0$) is called **negative**.","uses":["1.1","1.3","2.3"]},{"fact":"L4","source":"def-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"A **field** is a set $F$ equipped with two binary operations $+$ (addition) and\n$\\cdot$ (multiplication) and two distinguished elements $0 \\ne 1$ of $F$,\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(A)** $(F, +)$ is an abelian group with identity $0$: addition is\n  associative and commutative, $x + 0 = x$ for all $x$, and every $x$ has an\n  additive inverse $-x$ with $x + (-x) = 0$.\n- **(M)** Multiplication is associative and commutative **on all of $F$**, and\n  $x \\cdot 1 = x$ for every $x \\in F$; moreover $(F \\setminus \\{0\\}, \\cdot)$ is an\n  abelian group with identity $1$, so every $x \\ne 0$ has a multiplicative inverse\n  $x^{-1}$ with $x \\cdot x^{-1} = 1$.\n- **(D)** Multiplication distributes over addition: $x \\cdot (y + z) = x\\cdot y + x\\cdot z$.\n\nWe write $x - y := x + (-y)$ and $x/y := x \\cdot y^{-1}$ (for $y \\ne 0$), and\nabbreviate $x \\cdot y$ as $xy$.","uses":["1.1","1.3","2.3"]},{"fact":"L4","source":"lem-of-zero-mult","source_section":"Statement","quote":"In any field $F$ ([[def-field]]), for every $a \\in F$ we have $0 \\cdot a = 0$.","uses":["1.1","1.3","2.3"]},{"fact":"L4","source":"lem-of-sign-rules","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) with positive cone $P$, and\nlet $a, b, c \\in F$.\n\n1. If $a > 0$ and $b > 0$ then $ab > 0$.\n2. If $a > 0$ and $b < 0$ then $ab < 0$.\n3. If $a < 0$ and $b < 0$ then $ab > 0$.\n4. If $c > 0$ then $a < b \\iff ac < bc$.\n5. If $c < 0$ then $a < b \\iff bc < ac$ (multiplication by a negative reverses order).","uses":["1.1","1.3","2.3"]}],"derivations":[{"id":"step-1-1","claim":"Since $0 < 1$, the element $2 = 1 + 1$ is positive, hence nonzero, so $2^{-1}$ exists; from $2^{-1} \\cdot 2 = 1 > 0 = 0 \\cdot 2$ we get $2^{-1} > 0$, and from $2^{-1} \\cdot 2 = 1 < 2 = 1 \\cdot 2$ (the inequality $1 < 2$ holding because $2 - 1 = 1 > 0$) we get $2^{-1} < 1$; therefore $2^{-1} \\in S$ and $S \\ne \\emptyset$.","step":"1.1","inputs":["L3","L4","algebra"]},{"id":"step-1-2","claim":"Every $x \\in S$ satisfies $x < 1$ and hence $x \\le 1$, so $1$ is an upper bound of $S$ and $S$ is bounded above.","step":"1.2","inputs":["L3"]},{"id":"step-1-3","claim":"Let $\\varepsilon > 0$. Then $1 + \\varepsilon > 0$ is nonzero, so we may put $\\eta := \\varepsilon (1 + \\varepsilon)^{-1}$, which satisfies $\\eta (1 + \\varepsilon) = \\varepsilon$; multiplying by the positive $1 + \\varepsilon$ is an equivalence, so $\\eta > 0$ follows from $\\eta(1+\\varepsilon) = \\varepsilon > 0 = 0 \\cdot (1 + \\varepsilon)$, next $\\eta < 1$ follows from $\\eta(1+\\varepsilon) = \\varepsilon < 1 + \\varepsilon = 1 \\cdot (1 + \\varepsilon)$, and finally $\\eta < \\varepsilon$ follows from $\\eta(1+\\varepsilon) = \\varepsilon < \\varepsilon + \\varepsilon^2 = \\varepsilon(1 + \\varepsilon)$, the last inequality holding because $\\varepsilon^2 > 0$.","step":"1.3","inputs":["L3","L4","algebra"]},{"id":"step-2-1","claim":"Put $s := 1 - \\eta$. From $0 < \\eta$ we get $s = 1 - \\eta < 1$, from $\\eta < 1$ we get $0 = 1 - 1 < 1 - \\eta = s$, so $s \\in S$; and from $\\eta < \\varepsilon$ we get $1 - \\varepsilon < 1 - \\eta = s$. Since $\\varepsilon > 0$ was arbitrary, for every $\\varepsilon > 0$ there is an element of $S$ strictly greater than $1 - \\varepsilon$.","step":"2.1","inputs":["step 1.3","L3","algebra"]},{"id":"step-2-2","claim":"The number $1$ is not an element of $S$, because membership in $S$ requires $x < 1$ and $1 < 1$ is impossible by trichotomy.","step":"2.2","inputs":["step 1.1","L3"]},{"id":"step-2-3","claim":"The set $S$ has no maximum: if $m$ were one then $m \\in S$, so $0 < m < 1$; putting $m' := (m + 1) 2^{-1}$, so that $m' \\cdot 2 = m + 1$, the inequality $m \\cdot 2 = m + m < m + 1 = m' \\cdot 2$ gives $m < m'$, the inequality $m' \\cdot 2 = m + 1 < 1 + 1 = 1 \\cdot 2$ gives $m' < 1$, and $m' \\cdot 2 = m + 1 > 0 = 0 \\cdot 2$ gives $m' > 0$; hence $m' \\in S$ with $m < m'$, contradicting the requirement $m' \\le m$ for a maximum.","step":"2.3","inputs":["step 1.1","L2","L3","L4","algebra"]},{"id":"step-3-1","claim":"The set $S$ is nonempty and bounded above with upper bound $1$, and every $1 - \\varepsilon$ with $\\varepsilon > 0$ is exceeded by some element of $S$, so the epsilon characterisation gives $\\sup S = 1$.","step":"3.1","inputs":["step 1.1","step 1.2","step 2.1","L1"]},{"id":"step-4-1","claim":"Thus $S$ is a nonempty subset of $\\mathbb{R}$ that is bounded above, its supremum exists and equals $1$, and $1 \\notin S$; the claim that the supremum of a set belongs to the set is therefore false, and correspondingly $S$ has no maximum, so no element of $S$ could have served as its supremum.","step":"4.1","inputs":["step 3.1","step 2.2","step 2.3"]}],"routine_steps":[],"boundaries":[{"case":"empty","status":"checked","evidence":"step 1.1 constructs 1/2 in the witness set, so the counterexample satisfies nonemptiness."},{"case":"zero","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 1.1 and 2.1 verify the lower endpoint and positivity of the guarded witness."},{"case":"one","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 1.2, 2.1 and 2.2 verify the upper endpoint 1 is a bound but is excluded."},{"case":"degenerate","status":"checked","evidence":"step 1.3 treats arbitrarily large and small positive epsilon through a rational guard."},{"case":"endpoints","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 2.1 and 2.2 explicitly check both open endpoints."},{"case":"nonempty-choice","status":"checked","evidence":"All witnesses are explicitly constructed in steps 1.1, 1.3 and 2.1."},{"case":"iff-forward","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a forward implication proof."},{"case":"iff-reverse","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a reverse implication proof."}],"finite_smoke":[],"risk_review":{"status":"pending","reviewer":null,"notes":"Reserved for the independent Alpha proof-refuter if risk routing selects this item after A3."}},"fs-every-set-has-sup":{"citations":[{"fact":"L1","source":"def-complete-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.","uses":["1.1","2.1","2.2","3.1"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"thm-of-archimedean","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Every complete ordered field $F$ ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]) is Archimedean:\nfor every $x \\in F$ there is a natural number $n \\ge 1$ with $x < n \\cdot 1_F$,\nwhere $n \\cdot 1_F$ is the canonical natural of the ordered field $F$\n([[def-ordered-field]]). Equivalently, the canonical naturals are cofinal in $F$.","uses":["2.2"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"cor-of-one-positive","source_section":"Statement","quote":"In any ordered field $F$ with positive cone $P$, the multiplicative identity\nsatisfies $0 < 1$; that is, $1 \\in P$.","uses":["2.1","2.2"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"def-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"An **ordered field** is a field $F$ ([[def-field]]) together with a subset\n$P \\subseteq F$, the set of **positive** elements (the *positive cone*),\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(O1) Trichotomy.** For each $x \\in F$, exactly one of the following holds:\n  $x \\in P$, or $x = 0$, or $-x \\in P$.\n- **(O2) Closure.** If $x, y \\in P$ then $x + y \\in P$ and $x \\cdot y \\in P$.\n\nWe write $x > 0$ for $x \\in P$ (read \"$x$ is positive\"), and define the order by\n\n$$a < b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; b - a \\in P, \\qquad a \\le b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a < b \\text{ or } a = b.$$\n\nThus $a > b$ means $b < a$, and $a \\ge b$ means $b \\le a$. An element $x$ with\n$-x \\in P$ (equivalently $x < 0$) is called **negative**.","uses":["2.1","2.2"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"lem-of-add-order","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) with positive cone $P$, and\nlet $a, b, c, d \\in F$.\n\n1. **Translation invariance.** If $a < b$ then $a + c < b + c$.\n2. **Adding inequalities.** If $a < b$ and $c < d$ then $a + c < b + d$.","uses":["2.1","2.2"]}],"derivations":[{"id":"step-1-1","claim":"Every real number $w$ is an upper bound of $\\emptyset$: the requirement \"$x \\le w$ for all $x \\in \\emptyset$\" quantifies over no elements and so holds vacuously. In particular $\\emptyset$ is bounded above.","step":"1.1","inputs":["L1"]},{"id":"step-1-2","claim":"The set $A$ is a nonempty subset of $\\mathbb{R}$, since $1 \\cdot 1_{\\mathbb{R}} = 1_{\\mathbb{R}} \\in A$.","step":"1.2","inputs":["given"]},{"id":"step-2-1","claim":"The empty set has no least upper bound: were $w$ one, then $0 < 1$ gives $w - 1 < w$, while $w - 1$ is an upper bound of $\\emptyset$, so leastness of $w$ would force $w \\le w - 1$ and hence, adding $1 - w$ to both sides, $1 \\le 0$, which contradicts $0 < 1$ by trichotomy. So the first witness $\\emptyset$ has no supremum although it is bounded above.","step":"2.1","inputs":["step 1.1","L1","L3"]},{"id":"step-2-2","claim":"The set $A$ has no upper bound whatsoever: given any $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, the Archimedean property produces $n \\ge 1$ with $x < n \\cdot 1_{\\mathbb{R}}$, and $n \\cdot 1_{\\mathbb{R}} \\in A$, so by trichotomy $n \\cdot 1_{\\mathbb{R}} \\le x$ fails and $x$ does not bound $A$ above. A supremum is in particular an upper bound, so the second witness $A$ has no supremum although it is nonempty.","step":"2.2","inputs":["step 1.2","L1","L2","L3"]},{"id":"step-3-1","claim":"Each witness refutes the claim on its own, and they refute it for different reasons: $\\emptyset$ is bounded above but not nonempty, while $A$ is nonempty but not bounded above. So the claim is false, and moreover neither hypothesis of the least-upper-bound property can be dropped, since each fails alone on one of these two sets.","step":"3.1","inputs":["step 2.1","step 2.2","L1"]}],"routine_steps":[],"boundaries":[{"case":"empty","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 1.1 and 2.1 explicitly treat the empty set, including vacuous boundedness and failure of a least upper bound."},{"case":"zero","status":"checked","evidence":"step 2.1 uses 0<1 and the candidate w-1 to rule out a least real upper bound."},{"case":"one","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 1.2 and 2.2 anchor the canonical natural witness at n=1."},{"case":"degenerate","status":"checked","evidence":"step 3.1 separates the two independent missing-hypothesis degeneracies."},{"case":"endpoints","status":"not_applicable","reason":"No interval endpoint or finite-range endpoint occurs in this statement or proof."},{"case":"nonempty-choice","status":"checked","evidence":"step 2.2 invokes the Archimedean existential for each proposed real upper bound."},{"case":"iff-forward","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a forward implication proof."},{"case":"iff-reverse","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a reverse implication proof."}],"finite_smoke":[],"risk_review":{"status":"pending","reviewer":null,"notes":"Reserved for the independent Alpha proof-refuter if risk routing selects this item after A3."}},"ex-sup-of-open-interval":{"citations":[{"fact":"L1","source":"lem-sup-epsilon","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ be nonempty and bounded above, and let $u$ be an\nupper bound of $S$ ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]). Then\n\n$$u = \\sup S \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow \\quad \\text{for every } \\varepsilon > 0 \\text{ there exists } s \\in S \\text{ with } u - \\varepsilon < s.$$\n\nIn words: among the upper bounds of $S$, the supremum is exactly the one that\ncannot be lowered by any positive amount and still bound $S$.","uses":["3.1"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"lem-inf-epsilon","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ be nonempty and bounded below, and let $\\ell$ be a\nlower bound of $S$ ([[def-bounded-set]]). Then\n\n$$\\ell = \\inf S \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow \\quad \\text{for every } \\varepsilon > 0 \\text{ there exists } s \\in S \\text{ with } s < \\ell + \\varepsilon.$$\n\nIn words: among the lower bounds of $S$, the infimum is exactly the one that\ncannot be raised by any positive amount and still bound $S$ from below.","uses":["3.2"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"thm-infimum-property","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ be nonempty and bounded below. Then $S$ has a\ngreatest lower bound in $\\mathbb{R}$ ([[def-infimum]]), and it is given by\n\n$$\\inf S = -\\sup(-S), \\qquad \\text{where } -S = \\{-s : s \\in S\\}.$$\n\nIn particular the complete ordered field $\\mathbb{R}$ has the\ngreatest-lower-bound property, which is therefore not an extra axiom: it is a\nconsequence of the least-upper-bound property.","uses":["3.2"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"def-infimum","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ and $\\ell \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then $\\ell$ is a\n**greatest lower bound**, or **infimum**, of $S$ if both of the following hold:\n\n- $\\ell$ is a lower bound of $S$ ([[def-bounded-set]]), that is, $\\ell \\le s$ for\n  every $s \\in S$;\n- $\\ell' \\le \\ell$ for every lower bound $\\ell'$ of $S$.\n\nWritten out in one line:\n\n$$\\ell \\text{ is an infimum of } S \\iff \\big[(\\forall s \\in S)\\, \\ell \\le s\\big] \\text{ and } \\big[(\\forall \\ell' \\in \\mathbb{R})\\, \\big((\\forall s \\in S)\\, \\ell' \\le s\\big) \\Rightarrow \\ell' \\le \\ell\\big].$$\n\nAn infimum, when it exists, is unique ([[lem-sup-unique]]), so we may write\n$\\inf S$ for it.","uses":["3.2"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"lem-of-add-order","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) with positive cone $P$, and\nlet $a, b, c, d \\in F$.\n\n1. **Translation invariance.** If $a < b$ then $a + c < b + c$.\n2. **Adding inequalities.** If $a < b$ and $c < d$ then $a + c < b + d$.","uses":["1.2","1.3","2.1","2.2","2.3","2.4","4.2"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"def-complete-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.","uses":["1.2","1.3","2.1","2.2","2.3","2.4","4.2"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"def-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"An **ordered field** is a field $F$ ([[def-field]]) together with a subset\n$P \\subseteq F$, the set of **positive** elements (the *positive cone*),\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(O1) Trichotomy.** For each $x \\in F$, exactly one of the following holds:\n  $x \\in P$, or $x = 0$, or $-x \\in P$.\n- **(O2) Closure.** If $x, y \\in P$ then $x + y \\in P$ and $x \\cdot y \\in P$.\n\nWe write $x > 0$ for $x \\in P$ (read \"$x$ is positive\"), and define the order by\n\n$$a < b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; b - a \\in P, \\qquad a \\le b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a < b \\text{ or } a = b.$$\n\nThus $a > b$ means $b < a$, and $a \\ge b$ means $b \\le a$. An element $x$ with\n$-x \\in P$ (equivalently $x < 0$) is called **negative**.","uses":["1.2","1.3","2.1","2.2","2.3","2.4","4.2"]},{"fact":"L4","source":"cor-of-one-positive","source_section":"Statement","quote":"In any ordered field $F$ with positive cone $P$, the multiplicative identity\nsatisfies $0 < 1$; that is, $1 \\in P$.","uses":["1.1","2.1","2.2","2.3","2.4"]},{"fact":"L4","source":"lem-of-add-order","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) with positive cone $P$, and\nlet $a, b, c, d \\in F$.\n\n1. **Translation invariance.** If $a < b$ then $a + c < b + c$.\n2. **Adding inequalities.** If $a < b$ and $c < d$ then $a + c < b + d$.","uses":["1.1","2.1","2.2","2.3","2.4"]},{"fact":"L4","source":"prop-of-reciprocal-order","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field and let $r \\in F$ with $r > 0$. Then:\n\n$$0 < r < 1 \\iff \\tfrac{1}{r} > 1, \\qquad r > 1 \\iff 0 < \\tfrac{1}{r} < 1, \\qquad r = 1 \\iff \\tfrac{1}{r} = 1.$$\n\nHere $\\tfrac{1}{r}$ denotes the multiplicative inverse $r^{-1}$.","uses":["1.1","2.1","2.2","2.3","2.4"]},{"fact":"L4","source":"prop-of-ab-less-b","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field and let $a, b \\in F$ with $a > 0$ and $b > 0$. Then:\n\n$$0 < ab < b \\iff 0 < a < 1.$$","uses":["1.1","2.1","2.2","2.3","2.4"]},{"fact":"L4","source":"def-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"A **field** is a set $F$ equipped with two binary operations $+$ (addition) and\n$\\cdot$ (multiplication) and two distinguished elements $0 \\ne 1$ of $F$,\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(A)** $(F, +)$ is an abelian group with identity $0$: addition is\n  associative and commutative, $x + 0 = x$ for all $x$, and every $x$ has an\n  additive inverse $-x$ with $x + (-x) = 0$.\n- **(M)** Multiplication is associative and commutative **on all of $F$**, and\n  $x \\cdot 1 = x$ for every $x \\in F$; moreover $(F \\setminus \\{0\\}, \\cdot)$ is an\n  abelian group with identity $1$, so every $x \\ne 0$ has a multiplicative inverse\n  $x^{-1}$ with $x \\cdot x^{-1} = 1$.\n- **(D)** Multiplication distributes over addition: $x \\cdot (y + z) = x\\cdot y + x\\cdot z$.\n\nWe write $x - y := x + (-y)$ and $x/y := x \\cdot y^{-1}$ (for $y \\ne 0$), and\nabbreviate $x \\cdot y$ as $xy$.","uses":["1.1","2.1","2.2","2.3","2.4"]},{"fact":"L5","source":"lem-finite-set-has-max","source_section":"Statement","quote":"For every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ and all $a_0, a_1, \\dots, a_n \\in \\mathbb{R}$, the\nset $\\{a_0, a_1, \\dots, a_n\\}$ has a maximum and a minimum ([[def-max-min]]).\n\nWhat is proved below is exactly the displayed statement, by induction on $n$.\n\nThe usual reading, that *every nonempty finite subset of $\\mathbb{R}$ has a\nmaximum and a minimum*, follows once one identifies the nonempty finite subsets\nof $\\mathbb{R}$ with the sets listable as $\\{a_0, \\dots, a_n\\}$. That\nidentification is recorded as a stipulation in the Given below, because this page\nhas no definition of finiteness to prove it against. **It is discharged, not\nmerely assumed**: [[lem-finite-subsets-listable]] proves that the two\ndescriptions of a nonempty finite subset of $\\mathbb{R}$ agree. That lemma is\nrecorded in `justified_by` rather than in `deps`, since it is about the sets this\nlemma quantifies over and therefore depends on this one. This is what licenses\nthe notation\n$\\max\\{a_1, \\dots, a_n\\}$ and $\\min\\{a_1, \\dots, a_n\\}$ for finite sets of\n**real** numbers from this page onwards.","uses":["1.4","2.2","2.3","2.4"]},{"fact":"L5","source":"def-max-min","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ and $m \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n- $m$ is a **maximum** (or *greatest element*) of $S$ if $m \\in S$ and $s \\le m$\n  for every $s \\in S$.\n- $m$ is a **minimum** (or *least element*) of $S$ if $m \\in S$ and $m \\le s$\n  for every $s \\in S$.\n\nA set has at most one maximum: if $m_1$ and $m_2$ are both maxima then\n$m_1 \\in S$ gives $m_1 \\le m_2$ and $m_2 \\in S$ gives $m_2 \\le m_1$, so\n$m_1 = m_2$ by antisymmetry of the order, which is immediate from the trichotomy\naxiom of an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]]).\nThe same argument applies to minima, so we may write $\\max S$ and $\\min S$.","uses":["1.4","2.2","2.3","2.4"]},{"fact":"L6","source":"def-complete-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.","uses":["1.1","1.2","1.3","4.2"]},{"fact":"L6","source":"def-bounded-set","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Throughout, $\\mathbb{R}$ denotes the complete ordered field\n([[def-complete-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is a subset of it.\n\nThe notions **upper bound** and **bounded above** are already fixed by\n[[def-complete-ordered-field]] and are only recalled here, never redefined:\n$u \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is an *upper bound* of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$, and\n$S$ is *bounded above* if it has at least one upper bound. The dual notions are:\n\n- $\\ell \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is a **lower bound** of $S$ if $\\ell \\le s$ for all $s \\in S$.\n- $S$ is **bounded below** if it has at least one lower bound.\n- $S$ is **bounded** if it is both bounded above and bounded below, that is, if\n  there are $\\ell, u \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $\\ell \\le s \\le u$ for every $s \\in S$.","uses":["1.1","1.2","1.3","4.2"]},{"fact":"L6","source":"def-max-min","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ and $m \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n- $m$ is a **maximum** (or *greatest element*) of $S$ if $m \\in S$ and $s \\le m$\n  for every $s \\in S$.\n- $m$ is a **minimum** (or *least element*) of $S$ if $m \\in S$ and $m \\le s$\n  for every $s \\in S$.\n\nA set has at most one maximum: if $m_1$ and $m_2$ are both maxima then\n$m_1 \\in S$ gives $m_1 \\le m_2$ and $m_2 \\in S$ gives $m_2 \\le m_1$, so\n$m_1 = m_2$ by antisymmetry of the order, which is immediate from the trichotomy\naxiom of an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]]).\nThe same argument applies to minima, so we may write $\\max S$ and $\\min S$.","uses":["1.1","1.2","1.3","4.2"]},{"fact":"L7","source":"lem-max-is-sup","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ be nonempty.\n\n1. If $S$ has a maximum ([[def-max-min]]), then $\\sup S$ exists and\n   $\\sup S = \\max S$.\n2. If $\\sup S$ exists and $\\sup S \\in S$, then $S$ has a maximum and\n   $\\max S = \\sup S$.\n\nHence, for a set whose supremum exists, the supremum is attained (belongs to the\nset) precisely when the set has a maximum, and then the two agree.","uses":["4.1"]},{"fact":"L8","source":"def-infimum","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ and $\\ell \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then $\\ell$ is a\n**greatest lower bound**, or **infimum**, of $S$ if both of the following hold:\n\n- $\\ell$ is a lower bound of $S$ ([[def-bounded-set]]), that is, $\\ell \\le s$ for\n  every $s \\in S$;\n- $\\ell' \\le \\ell$ for every lower bound $\\ell'$ of $S$.\n\nWritten out in one line:\n\n$$\\ell \\text{ is an infimum of } S \\iff \\big[(\\forall s \\in S)\\, \\ell \\le s\\big] \\text{ and } \\big[(\\forall \\ell' \\in \\mathbb{R})\\, \\big((\\forall s \\in S)\\, \\ell' \\le s\\big) \\Rightarrow \\ell' \\le \\ell\\big].$$\n\nAn infimum, when it exists, is unique ([[lem-sup-unique]]), so we may write\n$\\inf S$ for it.","uses":["4.2"]}],"derivations":[{"id":"step-1-1","claim":"The element $2^{-1}$ lies in $I$, so $I \\ne \\emptyset$: by [L4] one has $0 < 2^{-1} < 1$, which is exactly the membership condition.","step":"1.1","inputs":["L4","L6"]},{"id":"step-1-2","claim":"The number $1$ is an upper bound of $I$ and the number $0$ is a lower bound of $I$: every $x \\in I$ satisfies $0 < x < 1$, hence $0 \\le x$ and $x \\le 1$; in particular $I$ is bounded above and bounded below.","step":"1.2","inputs":["L3","L6"]},{"id":"step-1-3","claim":"Neither $1$ nor $0$ lies in $I$: membership requires $x < 1$ and $0 < x$, and both $1 < 1$ and $0 < 0$ are impossible by irreflexivity.","step":"1.3","inputs":["L3","L6"]},{"id":"step-1-4","claim":"Let $\\varepsilon > 0$ be arbitrary and put $s_\\varepsilon := \\max\\{2^{-1},\\ 1 - \\varepsilon/2\\}$ and $t_\\varepsilon := \\min\\{2^{-1},\\ \\varepsilon/2\\}$, both of which exist.","step":"1.4","inputs":["assume-hyp","L5","choose"]},{"id":"step-2-1","claim":"$s_\\varepsilon \\in I$: on the one hand $s_\\varepsilon \\ge 2^{-1} > 0$, so $s_\\varepsilon > 0$; on the other hand $s_\\varepsilon$ is one of the two entries $2^{-1}$ and $1 - \\varepsilon/2$, and each of them is $< 1$, the first by [L4] and the second because $\\varepsilon/2 > 0$ gives $1 - \\varepsilon/2 < 1$; hence $s_\\varepsilon < 1$.","step":"2.1","inputs":["step 1.4","L4","L3"]},{"id":"step-2-2","claim":"$1 - \\varepsilon < s_\\varepsilon$: from $\\varepsilon/2 < \\varepsilon$ one gets $1 - \\varepsilon < 1 - \\varepsilon/2$, and $1 - \\varepsilon/2 \\le s_\\varepsilon$ because $s_\\varepsilon$ dominates that entry.","step":"2.2","inputs":["step 1.4","L4","L3","L5"]},{"id":"step-2-3","claim":"$t_\\varepsilon \\in I$: $t_\\varepsilon$ is one of the two entries $2^{-1}$ and $\\varepsilon/2$, both of which are positive by [L4], so $t_\\varepsilon > 0$; and $t_\\varepsilon \\le 2^{-1} < 1$, so $t_\\varepsilon < 1$.","step":"2.3","inputs":["step 1.4","L4","L3","L5"]},{"id":"step-2-4","claim":"$t_\\varepsilon < 0 + \\varepsilon$: indeed $t_\\varepsilon \\le \\varepsilon/2 < \\varepsilon = 0 + \\varepsilon$, using $\\varepsilon/2 < \\varepsilon$ from [L4] and transitivity.","step":"2.4","inputs":["step 1.4","L4","L3","L5"]},{"id":"step-3-1","claim":"$I$ is nonempty and bounded above by $1$, and for every $\\varepsilon > 0$ the element $s_\\varepsilon$ of $I$ satisfies $1 - \\varepsilon < s_\\varepsilon$; the epsilon characterisation therefore gives $\\sup I = 1$.","step":"3.1","inputs":["step 1.1","step 1.2","step 2.1","step 2.2","L1"]},{"id":"step-3-2","claim":"$I$ is nonempty and bounded below by $0$, and for every $\\varepsilon > 0$ the element $t_\\varepsilon$ of $I$ satisfies $t_\\varepsilon < 0 + \\varepsilon$; the dual characterisation therefore gives $\\inf I = 0$.","step":"3.2","inputs":["step 1.1","step 1.2","step 2.3","step 2.4","L2"]},{"id":"step-4-1","claim":"$I$ has no maximum: if $m$ were one, the attainment criterion would give $\\sup I = m \\in I$, whereas $\\sup I = 1 \\notin I$; the two are incompatible, so no maximum exists.","step":"4.1","inputs":["step 3.1","step 1.3","L7"]},{"id":"step-4-2","claim":"$I$ has no minimum: a minimum of $I$ would be a lower bound of $I$ lying in $I$, hence at most the greatest lower bound $\\inf I = 0$; but every element of $I$ satisfies $0 < x$, and $x \\le 0$ together with $0 < x$ is impossible by trichotomy.","step":"4.2","inputs":["step 3.2","L3","L6","L8"]},{"id":"step-5-1","claim":"Hence $\\sup I = 1$ and $\\inf I = 0$, while $1 \\notin I$ and $0 \\notin I$, and $I$ has neither a maximum nor a minimum, so both bounds are approached by $I$ and reached by neither.","step":"5.1","inputs":["step 3.1","step 3.2","step 1.3","step 4.1","step 4.2"]}],"routine_steps":[],"boundaries":[{"case":"empty","status":"checked","evidence":"step 1.1 constructs 1/2 in I, establishing nonemptiness."},{"case":"zero","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 1.2-1.3 and 2.3-3.2 explicitly verify the lower endpoint 0 and its nonattainment."},{"case":"one","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 1.2-1.3 and 2.1-3.1 explicitly verify the upper endpoint 1 and its nonattainment."},{"case":"degenerate","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 2.1-2.4 use max/min guards, so the witnesses stay in I even for large epsilon."},{"case":"endpoints","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 1.3, 3.1 and 3.2 anchor both open endpoints."},{"case":"nonempty-choice","status":"checked","evidence":"step 1.4 uses maxima/minima of explicit two-point sets, whose existence is cited."},{"case":"iff-forward","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a forward implication proof."},{"case":"iff-reverse","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a reverse implication proof."}],"finite_smoke":[],"risk_review":{"status":"pending","reviewer":null,"notes":"Reserved for the independent Alpha proof-refuter if risk routing selects this item after A3."}},"ex-sup-of-closed-interval":{"citations":[{"fact":"L1","source":"lem-max-is-sup","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ be nonempty.\n\n1. If $S$ has a maximum ([[def-max-min]]), then $\\sup S$ exists and\n   $\\sup S = \\max S$.\n2. If $\\sup S$ exists and $\\sup S \\in S$, then $S$ has a maximum and\n   $\\max S = \\sup S$.\n\nHence, for a set whose supremum exists, the supremum is attained (belongs to the\nset) precisely when the set has a maximum, and then the two agree.","uses":["3.1","4.1"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"def-max-min","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ and $m \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n- $m$ is a **maximum** (or *greatest element*) of $S$ if $m \\in S$ and $s \\le m$\n  for every $s \\in S$.\n- $m$ is a **minimum** (or *least element*) of $S$ if $m \\in S$ and $m \\le s$\n  for every $s \\in S$.\n\nA set has at most one maximum: if $m_1$ and $m_2$ are both maxima then\n$m_1 \\in S$ gives $m_1 \\le m_2$ and $m_2 \\in S$ gives $m_2 \\le m_1$, so\n$m_1 = m_2$ by antisymmetry of the order, which is immediate from the trichotomy\naxiom of an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]]).\nThe same argument applies to minima, so we may write $\\max S$ and $\\min S$.","uses":["2.1","2.2","4.1"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"def-complete-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.","uses":["1.2","3.2"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"def-bounded-set","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Throughout, $\\mathbb{R}$ denotes the complete ordered field\n([[def-complete-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is a subset of it.\n\nThe notions **upper bound** and **bounded above** are already fixed by\n[[def-complete-ordered-field]] and are only recalled here, never redefined:\n$u \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is an *upper bound* of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$, and\n$S$ is *bounded above* if it has at least one upper bound. The dual notions are:\n\n- $\\ell \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is a **lower bound** of $S$ if $\\ell \\le s$ for all $s \\in S$.\n- $S$ is **bounded below** if it has at least one lower bound.\n- $S$ is **bounded** if it is both bounded above and bounded below, that is, if\n  there are $\\ell, u \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $\\ell \\le s \\le u$ for every $s \\in S$.","uses":["1.2","3.2"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"def-infimum","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ and $\\ell \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then $\\ell$ is a\n**greatest lower bound**, or **infimum**, of $S$ if both of the following hold:\n\n- $\\ell$ is a lower bound of $S$ ([[def-bounded-set]]), that is, $\\ell \\le s$ for\n  every $s \\in S$;\n- $\\ell' \\le \\ell$ for every lower bound $\\ell'$ of $S$.\n\nWritten out in one line:\n\n$$\\ell \\text{ is an infimum of } S \\iff \\big[(\\forall s \\in S)\\, \\ell \\le s\\big] \\text{ and } \\big[(\\forall \\ell' \\in \\mathbb{R})\\, \\big((\\forall s \\in S)\\, \\ell' \\le s\\big) \\Rightarrow \\ell' \\le \\ell\\big].$$\n\nAn infimum, when it exists, is unique ([[lem-sup-unique]]), so we may write\n$\\inf S$ for it.","uses":["1.2","3.2"]},{"fact":"L4","source":"cor-of-one-positive","source_section":"Statement","quote":"In any ordered field $F$ with positive cone $P$, the multiplicative identity\nsatisfies $0 < 1$; that is, $1 \\in P$.","uses":["1.1"]},{"fact":"L4","source":"def-complete-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.","uses":["1.1"]},{"fact":"L4","source":"def-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"An **ordered field** is a field $F$ ([[def-field]]) together with a subset\n$P \\subseteq F$, the set of **positive** elements (the *positive cone*),\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(O1) Trichotomy.** For each $x \\in F$, exactly one of the following holds:\n  $x \\in P$, or $x = 0$, or $-x \\in P$.\n- **(O2) Closure.** If $x, y \\in P$ then $x + y \\in P$ and $x \\cdot y \\in P$.\n\nWe write $x > 0$ for $x \\in P$ (read \"$x$ is positive\"), and define the order by\n\n$$a < b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; b - a \\in P, \\qquad a \\le b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a < b \\text{ or } a = b.$$\n\nThus $a > b$ means $b < a$, and $a \\ge b$ means $b \\le a$. An element $x$ with\n$-x \\in P$ (equivalently $x < 0$) is called **negative**.","uses":["1.1"]}],"derivations":[{"id":"step-1-1","claim":"Both $0$ and $1$ lie in $J$, so $J \\ne \\emptyset$: from $0 < 1$ we get $0 \\le 1$, and reflexivity gives $0 \\le 0$ and $1 \\le 1$, so each of $0$ and $1$ satisfies the membership condition $0 \\le x \\le 1$.","step":"1.1","inputs":["L4"]},{"id":"step-1-2","claim":"Every $x \\in J$ satisfies $x \\le 1$ and $0 \\le x$, directly by the membership condition; so $1$ is an upper bound of $J$ and $0$ is a lower bound of $J$.","step":"1.2","inputs":["L3"]},{"id":"step-2-1","claim":"$1 = \\max J$: the number $1$ lies in $J$ and dominates every element of $J$.","step":"2.1","inputs":["step 1.1","step 1.2","L2"]},{"id":"step-2-2","claim":"$0 = \\min J$: the number $0$ lies in $J$ and is dominated by every element of $J$.","step":"2.2","inputs":["step 1.1","step 1.2","L2"]},{"id":"step-3-1","claim":"Since $J$ is nonempty and has the maximum $1$, the attainment criterion gives that $\\sup J$ exists and $\\sup J = \\max J = 1$.","step":"3.1","inputs":["step 2.1","step 1.1","L1"]},{"id":"step-3-2","claim":"$\\inf J = 0$: the number $0$ is a lower bound of $J$, and any lower bound $\\ell'$ of $J$ satisfies $\\ell' \\le 0$ because $0$ is itself an element of $J$; so $0$ is the greatest lower bound of $J$, and it equals $\\min J$.","step":"3.2","inputs":["step 2.2","step 1.2","L3"]},{"id":"step-4-1","claim":"Therefore $\\sup J = 1 \\in J$ and $\\inf J = 0 \\in J$: both bounds are attained, and they coincide with the maximum and the minimum of $J$.","step":"4.1","inputs":["step 3.1","step 3.2","L1","L2"]}],"routine_steps":[],"boundaries":[{"case":"empty","status":"checked","evidence":"step 1.1 proves J is nonempty by exhibiting both endpoints."},{"case":"zero","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 1.1, 2.2 and 3.2 explicitly verify zero as the attained infimum/minimum."},{"case":"one","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 1.1, 2.1 and 3.1 explicitly verify one as the attained supremum/maximum."},{"case":"degenerate","status":"checked","evidence":"The defining inequalities are nonstrict, and steps 2.1-3.2 correctly include equality at each endpoint."},{"case":"endpoints","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 1.1-3.2 explicitly verify both closed endpoints."},{"case":"nonempty-choice","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The proof makes no choice from a set whose nonemptiness requires a separate discharge."},{"case":"iff-forward","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a forward implication proof."},{"case":"iff-reverse","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a reverse implication proof."}],"finite_smoke":[],"risk_review":{"status":"pending","reviewer":null,"notes":"Reserved for the independent Alpha proof-refuter if risk routing selects this item after A3."}},"ex-inf-of-reciprocals":{"citations":[{"fact":"L1","source":"lem-of-naturals-positive","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field, with field structure as in [[def-field]], and for\n$n \\ge 1$ let $n \\cdot 1_F$ denote the canonical natural number\n$\\underbrace{1_F + \\cdots + 1_F}_{n}$, defined recursively by $1 \\cdot 1_F = 1_F$\nand $(n+1) \\cdot 1_F = n \\cdot 1_F + 1_F$. Then for every $n \\ge 1$:\n\n- $n \\cdot 1_F > 0$ and $(n+1) \\cdot 1_F > n \\cdot 1_F$;\n- the map $n \\mapsto n \\cdot 1_F$ is strictly increasing, hence injective, on $\\{1, 2, 3, \\dots\\}$;\n- $(m+n) \\cdot 1_F = m \\cdot 1_F + n \\cdot 1_F$ and $(mn) \\cdot 1_F = (m \\cdot 1_F)(n \\cdot 1_F)$ for all $m, n \\ge 1$.","uses":["1.1","1.2","1.3"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"thm-of-archimedean","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Every complete ordered field $F$ ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]) is Archimedean:\nfor every $x \\in F$ there is a natural number $n \\ge 1$ with $x < n \\cdot 1_F$,\nwhere $n \\cdot 1_F$ is the canonical natural of the ordered field $F$\n([[def-ordered-field]]). Equivalently, the canonical naturals are cofinal in $F$.","uses":["1.4"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"def-archimedean-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]). For a natural number\n$n \\ge 1$, write $n \\cdot 1_F := \\underbrace{1_F + \\cdots + 1_F}_{n}$ for the\n$n$-fold sum of the multiplicative identity, and $0 \\cdot 1_F := 0$. These are\nthe **canonical natural numbers** of $F$.\n\n$F$ is **Archimedean** if for every $x \\in F$ there is a natural number $n$ with\n\n$$x < n \\cdot 1_F.$$\n\nEquivalently, the canonical naturals $(n \\cdot 1_F)_{n \\ge 1}$ are **cofinal**:\nno single element of $F$ is an upper bound for all of them.","uses":["1.4"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"lem-of-inverse-positive","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) with positive cone $P$, and\nlet $a, b \\in F$.\n\n1. If $a > 0$ then $a^{-1} > 0$.\n2. If $0 < a < b$ then $0 < b^{-1} < a^{-1}$.","uses":["1.1","1.2","1.3","1.4","2.1"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"def-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"A **field** is a set $F$ equipped with two binary operations $+$ (addition) and\n$\\cdot$ (multiplication) and two distinguished elements $0 \\ne 1$ of $F$,\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(A)** $(F, +)$ is an abelian group with identity $0$: addition is\n  associative and commutative, $x + 0 = x$ for all $x$, and every $x$ has an\n  additive inverse $-x$ with $x + (-x) = 0$.\n- **(M)** Multiplication is associative and commutative **on all of $F$**, and\n  $x \\cdot 1 = x$ for every $x \\in F$; moreover $(F \\setminus \\{0\\}, \\cdot)$ is an\n  abelian group with identity $1$, so every $x \\ne 0$ has a multiplicative inverse\n  $x^{-1}$ with $x \\cdot x^{-1} = 1$.\n- **(D)** Multiplication distributes over addition: $x \\cdot (y + z) = x\\cdot y + x\\cdot z$.\n\nWe write $x - y := x + (-y)$ and $x/y := x \\cdot y^{-1}$ (for $y \\ne 0$), and\nabbreviate $x \\cdot y$ as $xy$.","uses":["1.1","1.2","1.3","1.4","2.1"]},{"fact":"L4","source":"lem-of-sign-rules","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) with positive cone $P$, and\nlet $a, b, c \\in F$.\n\n1. If $a > 0$ and $b > 0$ then $ab > 0$.\n2. If $a > 0$ and $b < 0$ then $ab < 0$.\n3. If $a < 0$ and $b < 0$ then $ab > 0$.\n4. If $c > 0$ then $a < b \\iff ac < bc$.\n5. If $c < 0$ then $a < b \\iff bc < ac$ (multiplication by a negative reverses order).","uses":["1.3"]},{"fact":"L4","source":"def-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"An **ordered field** is a field $F$ ([[def-field]]) together with a subset\n$P \\subseteq F$, the set of **positive** elements (the *positive cone*),\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(O1) Trichotomy.** For each $x \\in F$, exactly one of the following holds:\n  $x \\in P$, or $x = 0$, or $-x \\in P$.\n- **(O2) Closure.** If $x, y \\in P$ then $x + y \\in P$ and $x \\cdot y \\in P$.\n\nWe write $x > 0$ for $x \\in P$ (read \"$x$ is positive\"), and define the order by\n\n$$a < b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; b - a \\in P, \\qquad a \\le b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a < b \\text{ or } a = b.$$\n\nThus $a > b$ means $b < a$, and $a \\ge b$ means $b \\le a$. An element $x$ with\n$-x \\in P$ (equivalently $x < 0$) is called **negative**.","uses":["1.3"]},{"fact":"L5","source":"lem-inf-epsilon","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ be nonempty and bounded below, and let $\\ell$ be a\nlower bound of $S$ ([[def-bounded-set]]). Then\n\n$$\\ell = \\inf S \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow \\quad \\text{for every } \\varepsilon > 0 \\text{ there exists } s \\in S \\text{ with } s < \\ell + \\varepsilon.$$\n\nIn words: among the lower bounds of $S$, the infimum is exactly the one that\ncannot be raised by any positive amount and still bound $S$ from below.","uses":["3.1"]},{"fact":"L5","source":"def-infimum","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ and $\\ell \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then $\\ell$ is a\n**greatest lower bound**, or **infimum**, of $S$ if both of the following hold:\n\n- $\\ell$ is a lower bound of $S$ ([[def-bounded-set]]), that is, $\\ell \\le s$ for\n  every $s \\in S$;\n- $\\ell' \\le \\ell$ for every lower bound $\\ell'$ of $S$.\n\nWritten out in one line:\n\n$$\\ell \\text{ is an infimum of } S \\iff \\big[(\\forall s \\in S)\\, \\ell \\le s\\big] \\text{ and } \\big[(\\forall \\ell' \\in \\mathbb{R})\\, \\big((\\forall s \\in S)\\, \\ell' \\le s\\big) \\Rightarrow \\ell' \\le \\ell\\big].$$\n\nAn infimum, when it exists, is unique ([[lem-sup-unique]]), so we may write\n$\\inf S$ for it.","uses":["3.1"]},{"fact":"L6","source":"def-max-min","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ and $m \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n- $m$ is a **maximum** (or *greatest element*) of $S$ if $m \\in S$ and $s \\le m$\n  for every $s \\in S$.\n- $m$ is a **minimum** (or *least element*) of $S$ if $m \\in S$ and $m \\le s$\n  for every $s \\in S$.\n\nA set has at most one maximum: if $m_1$ and $m_2$ are both maxima then\n$m_1 \\in S$ gives $m_1 \\le m_2$ and $m_2 \\in S$ gives $m_2 \\le m_1$, so\n$m_1 = m_2$ by antisymmetry of the order, which is immediate from the trichotomy\naxiom of an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]]).\nThe same argument applies to minima, so we may write $\\max S$ and $\\min S$.","uses":["2.3","3.2","5.1"]},{"fact":"L6","source":"lem-max-is-sup","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ be nonempty.\n\n1. If $S$ has a maximum ([[def-max-min]]), then $\\sup S$ exists and\n   $\\sup S = \\max S$.\n2. If $\\sup S$ exists and $\\sup S \\in S$, then $S$ has a maximum and\n   $\\max S = \\sup S$.\n\nHence, for a set whose supremum exists, the supremum is attained (belongs to the\nset) precisely when the set has a maximum, and then the two agree.","uses":["2.3","3.2","5.1"]},{"fact":"L7","source":"def-complete-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.","uses":["2.1","2.2","4.1"]},{"fact":"L7","source":"def-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"An **ordered field** is a field $F$ ([[def-field]]) together with a subset\n$P \\subseteq F$, the set of **positive** elements (the *positive cone*),\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(O1) Trichotomy.** For each $x \\in F$, exactly one of the following holds:\n  $x \\in P$, or $x = 0$, or $-x \\in P$.\n- **(O2) Closure.** If $x, y \\in P$ then $x + y \\in P$ and $x \\cdot y \\in P$.\n\nWe write $x > 0$ for $x \\in P$ (read \"$x$ is positive\"), and define the order by\n\n$$a < b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; b - a \\in P, \\qquad a \\le b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a < b \\text{ or } a = b.$$\n\nThus $a > b$ means $b < a$, and $a \\ge b$ means $b \\le a$. An element $x$ with\n$-x \\in P$ (equivalently $x < 0$) is called **negative**.","uses":["2.1","2.2","4.1"]},{"fact":"L7","source":"cor-of-one-positive","source_section":"Statement","quote":"In any ordered field $F$ with positive cone $P$, the multiplicative identity\nsatisfies $0 < 1$; that is, $1 \\in P$.","uses":["2.1","2.2","4.1"]},{"fact":"L8","source":"def-bounded-set","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Throughout, $\\mathbb{R}$ denotes the complete ordered field\n([[def-complete-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is a subset of it.\n\nThe notions **upper bound** and **bounded above** are already fixed by\n[[def-complete-ordered-field]] and are only recalled here, never redefined:\n$u \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is an *upper bound* of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$, and\n$S$ is *bounded above* if it has at least one upper bound. The dual notions are:\n\n- $\\ell \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is a **lower bound** of $S$ if $\\ell \\le s$ for all $s \\in S$.\n- $S$ is **bounded below** if it has at least one lower bound.\n- $S$ is **bounded** if it is both bounded above and bounded below, that is, if\n  there are $\\ell, u \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $\\ell \\le s \\le u$ for every $s \\in S$.","uses":["1.2"]},{"fact":"L9","source":"def-max-min","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ and $m \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n- $m$ is a **maximum** (or *greatest element*) of $S$ if $m \\in S$ and $s \\le m$\n  for every $s \\in S$.\n- $m$ is a **minimum** (or *least element*) of $S$ if $m \\in S$ and $m \\le s$\n  for every $s \\in S$.\n\nA set has at most one maximum: if $m_1$ and $m_2$ are both maxima then\n$m_1 \\in S$ gives $m_1 \\le m_2$ and $m_2 \\in S$ gives $m_2 \\le m_1$, so\n$m_1 = m_2$ by antisymmetry of the order, which is immediate from the trichotomy\naxiom of an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]]).\nThe same argument applies to minima, so we may write $\\max S$ and $\\min S$.","uses":["4.1"]},{"fact":"L9","source":"def-infimum","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ and $\\ell \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then $\\ell$ is a\n**greatest lower bound**, or **infimum**, of $S$ if both of the following hold:\n\n- $\\ell$ is a lower bound of $S$ ([[def-bounded-set]]), that is, $\\ell \\le s$ for\n  every $s \\in S$;\n- $\\ell' \\le \\ell$ for every lower bound $\\ell'$ of $S$.\n\nWritten out in one line:\n\n$$\\ell \\text{ is an infimum of } S \\iff \\big[(\\forall s \\in S)\\, \\ell \\le s\\big] \\text{ and } \\big[(\\forall \\ell' \\in \\mathbb{R})\\, \\big((\\forall s \\in S)\\, \\ell' \\le s\\big) \\Rightarrow \\ell' \\le \\ell\\big].$$\n\nAn infimum, when it exists, is unique ([[lem-sup-unique]]), so we may write\n$\\inf S$ for it.","uses":["4.1"]}],"derivations":[{"id":"step-1-1","claim":"$H$ is nonempty: taking $n = 1$ gives the canonical natural $1 \\cdot 1 = 1$ by [L1], and $1/1 = 1^{-1} = 1$ by [L3], so $1 \\in H$.","step":"1.1","inputs":["L1","L3"]},{"id":"step-1-2","claim":"Every element of $H$ is positive, so $0$ is a lower bound of $H$ and $H$ is bounded below: for $n \\ge 1$ one has $n \\cdot 1 > 0$, hence $1/n = (n \\cdot 1)^{-1} > 0$ and in particular $0 \\le 1/n$.","step":"1.2","inputs":["L1","L3","L8"]},{"id":"step-1-3","claim":"Every element of $H$ is $\\le 1$: fix $n \\ge 1$ and multiply by the positive constant $n \\cdot 1$, which by [L4] turns the inequality $(n \\cdot 1)^{-1} \\le 1$ into the equivalent inequality $1 = (n \\cdot 1)^{-1}(n \\cdot 1) \\le 1 \\cdot (n \\cdot 1) = n \\cdot 1$, and the latter holds by [L1].","step":"1.3","inputs":["L1","L3","L4"]},{"id":"step-1-4","claim":"Let $\\varepsilon > 0$ be arbitrary; then $\\varepsilon^{-1} > 0$, and the Archimedean property applied to $\\varepsilon^{-1}$ supplies a natural $n_\\varepsilon \\ge 1$ with $\\varepsilon^{-1} < n_\\varepsilon \\cdot 1$.","step":"1.4","inputs":["assume-hyp","L2","L3","choose"]},{"id":"step-2-1","claim":"That index witnesses the approximation: from $0 < \\varepsilon^{-1} < n_\\varepsilon \\cdot 1$ and [L3] one gets $0 < (n_\\varepsilon \\cdot 1)^{-1} < (\\varepsilon^{-1})^{-1} = \\varepsilon$, that is $1/n_\\varepsilon \\in H$ with $1/n_\\varepsilon < 0 + \\varepsilon$.","step":"2.1","inputs":["step 1.4","L3","L7"]},{"id":"step-2-2","claim":"$0 \\notin H$: every element of $H$ is $> 0$ by 1.2, whereas $0 > 0$ is impossible by irreflexivity.","step":"2.2","inputs":["step 1.2","L7"]},{"id":"step-2-3","claim":"$1 = \\max H$: the element $1$ lies in $H$ by 1.1 and dominates every element of $H$ by 1.3.","step":"2.3","inputs":["step 1.1","step 1.3","L6"]},{"id":"step-3-1","claim":"$H$ is nonempty and bounded below by $0$, and for every $\\varepsilon > 0$ some element of $H$ is $< 0 + \\varepsilon$; the epsilon characterisation therefore gives $\\inf H = 0$.","step":"3.1","inputs":["step 1.1","step 1.2","step 2.1","L5"]},{"id":"step-3-2","claim":"Since $H$ is nonempty with maximum $1$, the attainment criterion gives that $\\sup H$ exists and $\\sup H = \\max H = 1 \\in H$.","step":"3.2","inputs":["step 2.3","step 1.1","L6"]},{"id":"step-4-1","claim":"$H$ has no minimum: a minimum of $H$ would be a lower bound of $H$ lying in $H$, hence at most the greatest lower bound $\\inf H = 0$; but every element of $H$ is $> 0$ by 1.2, and $x \\le 0$ together with $0 < x$ is impossible by trichotomy.","step":"4.1","inputs":["step 3.1","step 1.2","L7","L9"]},{"id":"step-5-1","claim":"Hence $\\inf H = 0 \\notin H$ and $\\sup H = 1 \\in H$: the infimum of $H$ is not attained and $H$ has no minimum, while the supremum is attained and is the maximum.","step":"5.1","inputs":["step 3.1","step 3.2","step 2.2","step 4.1","L6"]}],"routine_steps":[],"boundaries":[{"case":"empty","status":"checked","evidence":"step 1.1 exhibits 1 in H."},{"case":"zero","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 1.2, 2.2 and 3.1 verify zero as lower bound, nonmember and infimum."},{"case":"one","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 1.1, 2.3 and 3.2 verify one as member, maximum and supremum."},{"case":"degenerate","status":"checked","evidence":"The index restriction n>=1 excludes division by the zero natural; steps 1.1-1.3 anchor the first index."},{"case":"endpoints","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 2.2-3.2 explicitly check the lower and upper extremal values 0 and 1."},{"case":"nonempty-choice","status":"checked","evidence":"step 1.4 chooses n_epsilon using the cited Archimedean theorem after epsilon>0 is fixed."},{"case":"iff-forward","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a forward implication proof."},{"case":"iff-reverse","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a reverse implication proof."}],"finite_smoke":[],"risk_review":{"status":"pending","reviewer":null,"notes":"Reserved for the independent Alpha proof-refuter if risk routing selects this item after A3."}},"ex-sup-rationals-below-sqrt-two":{"citations":[{"fact":"L1","source":"thm-of-square-roots","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $F$ be a complete ordered field ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]). Then every\n$a \\in F$ with $a \\ge 0$ has a **unique** $s \\in F$ with $s \\ge 0$ and $s^2 = a$;\nwe write $s = \\sqrt{a}$. Consequently the positive elements of $F$ are exactly the\nnonzero squares: $x > 0$ if and only if $x = y^2$ for some $y \\neq 0$.","uses":["1.1"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"lem-of-square-monotone","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field. For $a, b \\ge 0$:\n\n$$a < b \\iff a^2 < b^2, \\qquad a \\le b \\iff a^2 \\le b^2.$$\n\nMore generally, for arbitrary $a, b \\in F$, $a^2 < b^2 \\iff |a| < |b|$.","uses":["1.5","2.1","3.1"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"lem-of-q-embeds","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]). There is a unique field\nhomomorphism $\\iota : \\mathbb{Q} \\to F$ ([[def-field-homomorphism]]). On the\nintegers it is given by $n \\mapsto n \\cdot 1_F$ (with $-n \\mapsto -(n \\cdot 1_F)$\nand $0 \\mapsto 0$), and on a rational written as $p/q$ with $q \\ge 1$ by\n$\\iota(p/q) = \\iota(p)\\,(q \\cdot 1_F)^{-1}$. Moreover $\\iota$ is injective and\norder-preserving, so it is an embedding of $\\mathbb{Q}$ as an ordered subfield of\n$F$, and it is the only field homomorphism $\\mathbb{Q} \\to F$.","uses":["1.5","2.1","2.2","2.4","3.1","6.1"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"def-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"An **ordered field** is a field $F$ ([[def-field]]) together with a subset\n$P \\subseteq F$, the set of **positive** elements (the *positive cone*),\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(O1) Trichotomy.** For each $x \\in F$, exactly one of the following holds:\n  $x \\in P$, or $x = 0$, or $-x \\in P$.\n- **(O2) Closure.** If $x, y \\in P$ then $x + y \\in P$ and $x \\cdot y \\in P$.\n\nWe write $x > 0$ for $x \\in P$ (read \"$x$ is positive\"), and define the order by\n\n$$a < b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; b - a \\in P, \\qquad a \\le b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a < b \\text{ or } a = b.$$\n\nThus $a > b$ means $b < a$, and $a \\ge b$ means $b \\le a$. An element $x$ with\n$-x \\in P$ (equivalently $x < 0$) is called **negative**.","uses":["1.5","2.1","2.2","2.4","3.1","6.1"]},{"fact":"L4","source":"thm-of-archimedean","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Every complete ordered field $F$ ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]) is Archimedean:\nfor every $x \\in F$ there is a natural number $n \\ge 1$ with $x < n \\cdot 1_F$,\nwhere $n \\cdot 1_F$ is the canonical natural of the ordered field $F$\n([[def-ordered-field]]). Equivalently, the canonical naturals are cofinal in $F$.","uses":["3.1","6.1"]},{"fact":"L4","source":"def-archimedean-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]). For a natural number\n$n \\ge 1$, write $n \\cdot 1_F := \\underbrace{1_F + \\cdots + 1_F}_{n}$ for the\n$n$-fold sum of the multiplicative identity, and $0 \\cdot 1_F := 0$. These are\nthe **canonical natural numbers** of $F$.\n\n$F$ is **Archimedean** if for every $x \\in F$ there is a natural number $n$ with\n\n$$x < n \\cdot 1_F.$$\n\nEquivalently, the canonical naturals $(n \\cdot 1_F)_{n \\ge 1}$ are **cofinal**:\nno single element of $F$ is an upper bound for all of them.","uses":["3.1","6.1"]},{"fact":"L4","source":"lem-of-q-dense","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $F$ be an Archimedean ordered field ([[def-archimedean-field]]) and let\n$\\iota : \\mathbb{Q} \\to F$ be the canonical embedding ([[lem-of-q-embeds]]). Then\n$\\iota(\\mathbb{Q})$ is dense in $F$: for any $x < y$ in $F$ there is a rational\n$q$ with $x < \\iota(q) < y$.","uses":["3.1","6.1"]},{"fact":"L5","source":"fs-sqrt2-rational","source_section":"Statement","quote":"**False claim:** there exists $x \\in \\mathbb{Q}$ with $x^2 = 2$.","uses":["2.2"]},{"fact":"L6","source":"lem-sup-epsilon","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ be nonempty and bounded above, and let $u$ be an\nupper bound of $S$ ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]). Then\n\n$$u = \\sup S \\quad \\Longleftrightarrow \\quad \\text{for every } \\varepsilon > 0 \\text{ there exists } s \\in S \\text{ with } u - \\varepsilon < s.$$\n\nIn words: among the upper bounds of $S$, the supremum is exactly the one that\ncannot be lowered by any positive amount and still bound $S$.","uses":["4.1"]},{"fact":"L7","source":"lem-finite-set-has-max","source_section":"Statement","quote":"For every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ and all $a_0, a_1, \\dots, a_n \\in \\mathbb{R}$, the\nset $\\{a_0, a_1, \\dots, a_n\\}$ has a maximum and a minimum ([[def-max-min]]).\n\nWhat is proved below is exactly the displayed statement, by induction on $n$.\n\nThe usual reading, that *every nonempty finite subset of $\\mathbb{R}$ has a\nmaximum and a minimum*, follows once one identifies the nonempty finite subsets\nof $\\mathbb{R}$ with the sets listable as $\\{a_0, \\dots, a_n\\}$. That\nidentification is recorded as a stipulation in the Given below, because this page\nhas no definition of finiteness to prove it against. **It is discharged, not\nmerely assumed**: [[lem-finite-subsets-listable]] proves that the two\ndescriptions of a nonempty finite subset of $\\mathbb{R}$ agree. That lemma is\nrecorded in `justified_by` rather than in `deps`, since it is about the sets this\nlemma quantifies over and therefore depends on this one. This is what licenses\nthe notation\n$\\max\\{a_1, \\dots, a_n\\}$ and $\\min\\{a_1, \\dots, a_n\\}$ for finite sets of\n**real** numbers from this page onwards.","uses":["2.3"]},{"fact":"L7","source":"def-max-min","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ and $m \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n- $m$ is a **maximum** (or *greatest element*) of $S$ if $m \\in S$ and $s \\le m$\n  for every $s \\in S$.\n- $m$ is a **minimum** (or *least element*) of $S$ if $m \\in S$ and $m \\le s$\n  for every $s \\in S$.\n\nA set has at most one maximum: if $m_1$ and $m_2$ are both maxima then\n$m_1 \\in S$ gives $m_1 \\le m_2$ and $m_2 \\in S$ gives $m_2 \\le m_1$, so\n$m_1 = m_2$ by antisymmetry of the order, which is immediate from the trichotomy\naxiom of an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]]).\nThe same argument applies to minima, so we may write $\\max S$ and $\\min S$.","uses":["2.3"]},{"fact":"L8","source":"lem-of-zero-mult","source_section":"Statement","quote":"In any field $F$ ([[def-field]]), for every $a \\in F$ we have $0 \\cdot a = 0$.","uses":["1.1","1.2","1.5","2.1","2.3","5.1","6.1","7.1"]},{"fact":"L8","source":"cor-of-one-positive","source_section":"Statement","quote":"In any ordered field $F$ with positive cone $P$, the multiplicative identity\nsatisfies $0 < 1$; that is, $1 \\in P$.","uses":["1.1","1.2","1.5","2.1","2.3","5.1","6.1","7.1"]},{"fact":"L8","source":"lem-of-add-order","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) with positive cone $P$, and\nlet $a, b, c, d \\in F$.\n\n1. **Translation invariance.** If $a < b$ then $a + c < b + c$.\n2. **Adding inequalities.** If $a < b$ and $c < d$ then $a + c < b + d$.","uses":["1.1","1.2","1.5","2.1","2.3","5.1","6.1","7.1"]},{"fact":"L8","source":"def-complete-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.","uses":["1.1","1.2","1.5","2.1","2.3","5.1","6.1","7.1"]},{"fact":"L8","source":"def-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"An **ordered field** is a field $F$ ([[def-field]]) together with a subset\n$P \\subseteq F$, the set of **positive** elements (the *positive cone*),\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(O1) Trichotomy.** For each $x \\in F$, exactly one of the following holds:\n  $x \\in P$, or $x = 0$, or $-x \\in P$.\n- **(O2) Closure.** If $x, y \\in P$ then $x + y \\in P$ and $x \\cdot y \\in P$.\n\nWe write $x > 0$ for $x \\in P$ (read \"$x$ is positive\"), and define the order by\n\n$$a < b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; b - a \\in P, \\qquad a \\le b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a < b \\text{ or } a = b.$$\n\nThus $a > b$ means $b < a$, and $a \\ge b$ means $b \\le a$. An element $x$ with\n$-x \\in P$ (equivalently $x < 0$) is called **negative**.","uses":["1.1","1.2","1.5","2.1","2.3","5.1","6.1","7.1"]},{"fact":"L8","source":"def-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"A **field** is a set $F$ equipped with two binary operations $+$ (addition) and\n$\\cdot$ (multiplication) and two distinguished elements $0 \\ne 1$ of $F$,\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(A)** $(F, +)$ is an abelian group with identity $0$: addition is\n  associative and commutative, $x + 0 = x$ for all $x$, and every $x$ has an\n  additive inverse $-x$ with $x + (-x) = 0$.\n- **(M)** Multiplication is associative and commutative **on all of $F$**, and\n  $x \\cdot 1 = x$ for every $x \\in F$; moreover $(F \\setminus \\{0\\}, \\cdot)$ is an\n  abelian group with identity $1$, so every $x \\ne 0$ has a multiplicative inverse\n  $x^{-1}$ with $x \\cdot x^{-1} = 1$.\n- **(D)** Multiplication distributes over addition: $x \\cdot (y + z) = x\\cdot y + x\\cdot z$.\n\nWe write $x - y := x + (-y)$ and $x/y := x \\cdot y^{-1}$ (for $y \\ne 0$), and\nabbreviate $x \\cdot y$ as $xy$.","uses":["1.1","1.2","1.5","2.1","2.3","5.1","6.1","7.1"]}],"derivations":[{"id":"step-1-1","claim":"In $\\mathbb{R}$ we have $2 = 1 + 1 > 0$, so [L1] supplies a unique $r \\ge 0$ with $r^2 = 2$; and $r \\ne 0$ because $r = 0$ would give $r^2 = 0 \\ne 2$, so $r > 0$. Write $r =: \\sqrt{2}$.","step":"1.1","inputs":["L1","L8","choose"]},{"id":"step-1-2","claim":"$S$ is nonempty: the rational $1$ satisfies $1 > 0$ and $1^2 = 1 < 2$, so $1 \\in S$.","step":"1.2","inputs":["L8"]},{"id":"step-1-3","claim":"Let $u \\in \\mathbb{Q}$ be an arbitrary upper bound of $S$ in $\\mathbb{Q}$, that is, $q \\le u$ for every $q \\in S$.","step":"1.3","inputs":["assume-hyp"]},{"id":"step-1-4","claim":"Let $\\varepsilon > 0$ in $\\mathbb{R}$ be arbitrary.","step":"1.4","inputs":["assume-hyp"]},{"id":"step-1-5","claim":"The rational $2$ is an upper bound of $S$ in $\\mathbb{Q}$, so $S$ is bounded above there: for $q \\in S$ order preservation gives $\\iota(q) > 0$ and $\\iota(q)^2 = \\iota(q^2) < \\iota(2) = 2 < 2 + 2 = 2 \\cdot 2 = \\iota(2)^2$, whence $\\iota(q) < \\iota(2)$ by [L2] and $q < 2$ by order reflection.","step":"1.5","inputs":["L2","L3","L8"]},{"id":"step-2-1","claim":"$r$ is an upper bound of $\\iota(S)$ in $\\mathbb{R}$: for $q \\in S$ order preservation gives $\\iota(q) > 0$ and $\\iota(q)^2 = \\iota(q^2) < \\iota(2) = 2 = r^2$, so $\\iota(q) < r$ by [L2], hence $\\iota(q) \\le r$.","step":"2.1","inputs":["step 1.1","L2","L3","L8"]},{"id":"step-2-2","claim":"$r$ is not the image of any rational: if $r = \\iota(q)$ then $\\iota(q^2) = \\iota(q)^2 = r^2 = 2 = \\iota(2)$, so $q^2 = 2$ by injectivity of $\\iota$, which [L5] forbids.","step":"2.2","inputs":["step 1.1","L3","L5"]},{"id":"step-2-3","claim":"Put $a_\\varepsilon := \\max\\{0,\\ r - \\varepsilon\\}$, which exists by [L7]; then $0 \\le a_\\varepsilon$, and $a_\\varepsilon < r$ because $a_\\varepsilon$ is one of the two entries $0$ and $r - \\varepsilon$, of which the first is $< r$ by 1.1 and the second is $< r$ because $\\varepsilon > 0$.","step":"2.3","inputs":["step 1.1","step 1.4","L7","L8","choose"]},{"id":"step-2-4","claim":"The image $\\iota(u)$ of the bound $u$ is an upper bound of $\\iota(S)$ in $\\mathbb{R}$: for $q \\in S$ the inequality $q \\le u$ in $\\mathbb{Q}$ gives $\\iota(q) \\le \\iota(u)$ by order preservation.","step":"2.4","inputs":["step 1.3","L3"]},{"id":"step-3-1","claim":"Density applied to $a_\\varepsilon < r$ produces a rational $q_\\varepsilon$ with $a_\\varepsilon < \\iota(q_\\varepsilon) < r$; then $\\iota(q_\\varepsilon) > a_\\varepsilon \\ge 0$ forces $q_\\varepsilon > 0$ by order reflection, and $0 \\le \\iota(q_\\varepsilon) < r$ gives $\\iota(q_\\varepsilon^2) = \\iota(q_\\varepsilon)^2 < r^2 = 2 = \\iota(2)$ by [L2], hence $q_\\varepsilon^2 < 2$ in $\\mathbb{Q}$; so $q_\\varepsilon \\in S$ and $r - \\varepsilon \\le a_\\varepsilon < \\iota(q_\\varepsilon)$.","step":"3.1","inputs":["step 2.3","step 1.1","L4","L2","L3"]},{"id":"step-4-1","claim":"The set $\\iota(S)$ is nonempty and bounded above in $\\mathbb{R}$ by $r$, and for every $\\varepsilon > 0$ its element $\\iota(q_\\varepsilon)$ satisfies $r - \\varepsilon < \\iota(q_\\varepsilon)$; the epsilon characterisation therefore gives $\\sup \\iota(S) = r = \\sqrt{2}$.","step":"4.1","inputs":["step 1.2","step 2.1","step 3.1","L6"]},{"id":"step-5-1","claim":"Since $r$ is the least upper bound of $\\iota(S)$ and $\\iota(u)$ is an upper bound of it, we get $r \\le \\iota(u)$; and $r \\ne \\iota(u)$ by 2.2, so $r < \\iota(u)$.","step":"5.1","inputs":["step 4.1","step 2.4","step 2.2","L8"]},{"id":"step-6-1","claim":"Density applied to $r < \\iota(u)$ produces a rational $v$ with $r < \\iota(v) < \\iota(u)$; every $q \\in S$ satisfies $\\iota(q) \\le r < \\iota(v)$, so $q < v$ by order reflection and $v$ is an upper bound of $S$ in $\\mathbb{Q}$; and $v < u$, again by order reflection. Hence $u$ is not $\\le$ every upper bound of $S$ in $\\mathbb{Q}$, so $u$ is not a supremum of $S$ in $\\mathbb{Q}$.","step":"6.1","inputs":["step 5.1","step 2.1","L4","L3","L8"]},{"id":"step-7-1","claim":"The upper bound $u$ was an arbitrary one, so no upper bound of $S$ in $\\mathbb{Q}$ is least: $S$ has a supremum in $\\mathbb{R}$, equal to $\\sqrt{2}$, and has no supremum in $\\mathbb{Q}$ even though it is nonempty and bounded above there, for instance by $2$.","step":"7.1","inputs":["step 6.1","step 4.1","step 1.5","L8"]}],"routine_steps":[],"boundaries":[{"case":"empty","status":"checked","evidence":"step 1.2 exhibits rational 1 in S."},{"case":"zero","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 1.1-1.2 verify positivity and exclude the zero square from the boundary value 2."},{"case":"one","status":"checked","evidence":"step 1.2 verifies the first simple rational lies below the square-root boundary."},{"case":"degenerate","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 2.2 and 5.1 exclude the equality case between sqrt(2) and a rational upper bound."},{"case":"endpoints","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 2.1-4.1 verify sqrt(2) as the upper endpoint approached from below."},{"case":"nonempty-choice","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 3.1 and 6.1 use density only after strict nonempty intervals are established; step 1.1 uses the sourced square-root existence theorem."},{"case":"iff-forward","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a forward implication proof."},{"case":"iff-reverse","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a reverse implication proof."}],"finite_smoke":[],"risk_review":{"status":"pending","reviewer":null,"notes":"Reserved for the independent Alpha proof-refuter if risk routing selects this item after A3."}},"ex-sup-of-sum-of-sets":{"citations":[{"fact":"L1","source":"ex-sup-of-open-interval","source_section":"Example","quote":"The open unit interval $I = (0,1) = \\{x \\in \\mathbb{R} : 0 < x < 1\\}$ has\n$\\sup I = 1$ and $\\inf I = 0$, and neither value belongs to $I$. This is the\nsmallest interesting computation in the subject: both bounds exist by the\nleast-upper-bound property ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]) and its dual\n([[thm-infimum-property]]), and both are missed by the set, so $I$ has neither a\nmaximum ([[lem-max-is-sup]]) nor a minimum. The maximum is ruled out by the\nattainment criterion; the minimum is ruled out directly, since a minimum would be\na lower bound of $I$ lying in $I$ and therefore at most $\\inf I = 0$\n([[def-max-min]], [[def-infimum]]).\n\nNothing here is asserted by inspection. That $1$ bounds $I$ above is checked from\nthe definition; that no smaller number does is checked with the epsilon\ncharacterisation ([[lem-sup-epsilon]]), by exhibiting, for each $\\varepsilon > 0$,\nan explicit element of $I$ lying strictly above $1 - \\varepsilon$. The witness is\n$\\max\\{1/2,\\ 1 - \\varepsilon/2\\}$: the second entry does the approximating, and\nthe first keeps the witness inside $I$ when $\\varepsilon$ is large. The infimum is\nhandled symmetrically with [[lem-inf-epsilon]] and the witness\n$\\min\\{1/2,\\ \\varepsilon/2\\}$.","uses":["2.3","3.2"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"lem-sup-translate","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ be nonempty and bounded above and let\n$a \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Write $a + S := \\{a + s : s \\in S\\}$. Then $a + S$ is\nnonempty and bounded above, and\n\n$$\\sup(a + S) = a + \\sup S.$$","uses":["2.3"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"lem-sup-sum","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $S, T \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ be nonempty and bounded above, and write\n$S + T := \\{s + t : s \\in S,\\ t \\in T\\}$. Then $S + T$ is nonempty and bounded\nabove, and\n\n$$\\sup(S + T) = \\sup S + \\sup T.$$","uses":["3.2","4.1","5.1"]},{"fact":"L4","source":"lem-of-add-order","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) with positive cone $P$, and\nlet $a, b, c, d \\in F$.\n\n1. **Translation invariance.** If $a < b$ then $a + c < b + c$.\n2. **Adding inequalities.** If $a < b$ and $c < d$ then $a + c < b + d$.","uses":["1.1","1.2","2.1","2.2"]},{"fact":"L4","source":"def-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"An **ordered field** is a field $F$ ([[def-field]]) together with a subset\n$P \\subseteq F$, the set of **positive** elements (the *positive cone*),\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(O1) Trichotomy.** For each $x \\in F$, exactly one of the following holds:\n  $x \\in P$, or $x = 0$, or $-x \\in P$.\n- **(O2) Closure.** If $x, y \\in P$ then $x + y \\in P$ and $x \\cdot y \\in P$.\n\nWe write $x > 0$ for $x \\in P$ (read \"$x$ is positive\"), and define the order by\n\n$$a < b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; b - a \\in P, \\qquad a \\le b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a < b \\text{ or } a = b.$$\n\nThus $a > b$ means $b < a$, and $a \\ge b$ means $b \\le a$. An element $x$ with\n$-x \\in P$ (equivalently $x < 0$) is called **negative**.","uses":["1.1","1.2","2.1","2.2"]},{"fact":"L5","source":"lem-of-zero-mult","source_section":"Statement","quote":"In any field $F$ ([[def-field]]), for every $a \\in F$ we have $0 \\cdot a = 0$.","uses":["1.3","2.1","2.2"]},{"fact":"L5","source":"def-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"A **field** is a set $F$ equipped with two binary operations $+$ (addition) and\n$\\cdot$ (multiplication) and two distinguished elements $0 \\ne 1$ of $F$,\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(A)** $(F, +)$ is an abelian group with identity $0$: addition is\n  associative and commutative, $x + 0 = x$ for all $x$, and every $x$ has an\n  additive inverse $-x$ with $x + (-x) = 0$.\n- **(M)** Multiplication is associative and commutative **on all of $F$**, and\n  $x \\cdot 1 = x$ for every $x \\in F$; moreover $(F \\setminus \\{0\\}, \\cdot)$ is an\n  abelian group with identity $1$, so every $x \\ne 0$ has a multiplicative inverse\n  $x^{-1}$ with $x \\cdot x^{-1} = 1$.\n- **(D)** Multiplication distributes over addition: $x \\cdot (y + z) = x\\cdot y + x\\cdot z$.\n\nWe write $x - y := x + (-y)$ and $x/y := x \\cdot y^{-1}$ (for $y \\ne 0$), and\nabbreviate $x \\cdot y$ as $xy$.","uses":["1.3","2.1","2.2"]},{"fact":"L5","source":"lem-of-sign-rules","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) with positive cone $P$, and\nlet $a, b, c \\in F$.\n\n1. If $a > 0$ and $b > 0$ then $ab > 0$.\n2. If $a > 0$ and $b < 0$ then $ab < 0$.\n3. If $a < 0$ and $b < 0$ then $ab > 0$.\n4. If $c > 0$ then $a < b \\iff ac < bc$.\n5. If $c < 0$ then $a < b \\iff bc < ac$ (multiplication by a negative reverses order).","uses":["1.3","2.1","2.2"]},{"fact":"L5","source":"cor-of-one-positive","source_section":"Statement","quote":"In any ordered field $F$ with positive cone $P$, the multiplicative identity\nsatisfies $0 < 1$; that is, $1 \\in P$.","uses":["1.3","2.1","2.2"]},{"fact":"L5","source":"def-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"An **ordered field** is a field $F$ ([[def-field]]) together with a subset\n$P \\subseteq F$, the set of **positive** elements (the *positive cone*),\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(O1) Trichotomy.** For each $x \\in F$, exactly one of the following holds:\n  $x \\in P$, or $x = 0$, or $-x \\in P$.\n- **(O2) Closure.** If $x, y \\in P$ then $x + y \\in P$ and $x \\cdot y \\in P$.\n\nWe write $x > 0$ for $x \\in P$ (read \"$x$ is positive\"), and define the order by\n\n$$a < b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; b - a \\in P, \\qquad a \\le b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a < b \\text{ or } a = b.$$\n\nThus $a > b$ means $b < a$, and $a \\ge b$ means $b \\le a$. An element $x$ with\n$-x \\in P$ (equivalently $x < 0$) is called **negative**.","uses":["1.3","2.1","2.2"]},{"fact":"L6","source":"def-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"A **field** is a set $F$ equipped with two binary operations $+$ (addition) and\n$\\cdot$ (multiplication) and two distinguished elements $0 \\ne 1$ of $F$,\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(A)** $(F, +)$ is an abelian group with identity $0$: addition is\n  associative and commutative, $x + 0 = x$ for all $x$, and every $x$ has an\n  additive inverse $-x$ with $x + (-x) = 0$.\n- **(M)** Multiplication is associative and commutative **on all of $F$**, and\n  $x \\cdot 1 = x$ for every $x \\in F$; moreover $(F \\setminus \\{0\\}, \\cdot)$ is an\n  abelian group with identity $1$, so every $x \\ne 0$ has a multiplicative inverse\n  $x^{-1}$ with $x \\cdot x^{-1} = 1$.\n- **(D)** Multiplication distributes over addition: $x \\cdot (y + z) = x\\cdot y + x\\cdot z$.\n\nWe write $x - y := x + (-y)$ and $x/y := x \\cdot y^{-1}$ (for $y \\ne 0$), and\nabbreviate $x \\cdot y$ as $xy$.","uses":["1.1","1.2","2.1","2.2","2.3","3.2"]},{"fact":"L6","source":"def-complete-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.","uses":["1.1","1.2","2.1","2.2","2.3","3.2"]},{"fact":"L7","source":"def-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"An **ordered field** is a field $F$ ([[def-field]]) together with a subset\n$P \\subseteq F$, the set of **positive** elements (the *positive cone*),\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(O1) Trichotomy.** For each $x \\in F$, exactly one of the following holds:\n  $x \\in P$, or $x = 0$, or $-x \\in P$.\n- **(O2) Closure.** If $x, y \\in P$ then $x + y \\in P$ and $x \\cdot y \\in P$.\n\nWe write $x > 0$ for $x \\in P$ (read \"$x$ is positive\"), and define the order by\n\n$$a < b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; b - a \\in P, \\qquad a \\le b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a < b \\text{ or } a = b.$$\n\nThus $a > b$ means $b < a$, and $a \\ge b$ means $b \\le a$. An element $x$ with\n$-x \\in P$ (equivalently $x < 0$) is called **negative**.","uses":["4.2"]},{"fact":"L7","source":"def-complete-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.","uses":["4.2"]},{"fact":"L8","source":"def-max-min","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ and $m \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n- $m$ is a **maximum** (or *greatest element*) of $S$ if $m \\in S$ and $s \\le m$\n  for every $s \\in S$.\n- $m$ is a **minimum** (or *least element*) of $S$ if $m \\in S$ and $m \\le s$\n  for every $s \\in S$.\n\nA set has at most one maximum: if $m_1$ and $m_2$ are both maxima then\n$m_1 \\in S$ gives $m_1 \\le m_2$ and $m_2 \\in S$ gives $m_2 \\le m_1$, so\n$m_1 = m_2$ by antisymmetry of the order, which is immediate from the trichotomy\naxiom of an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]]).\nThe same argument applies to minima, so we may write $\\max S$ and $\\min S$.","uses":["5.1"]},{"fact":"L8","source":"lem-max-is-sup","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ be nonempty.\n\n1. If $S$ has a maximum ([[def-max-min]]), then $\\sup S$ exists and\n   $\\sup S = \\max S$.\n2. If $\\sup S$ exists and $\\sup S \\in S$, then $S$ has a maximum and\n   $\\max S = \\sup S$.\n\nHence, for a set whose supremum exists, the supremum is attained (belongs to the\nset) precisely when the set has a maximum, and then the two agree.","uses":["5.1"]}],"derivations":[{"id":"step-1-1","claim":"$T = 2 + S$: for $s \\in \\mathbb{R}$, adding $2$ to each part of $0 < s < 1$ gives $2 < 2 + s < 2 + 1 = 3$, and conversely each $y$ with $2 < y < 3$ equals $2 + (y - 2)$ where subtracting $2$ from each part gives $0 < y - 2 < 1$; the two directions are equivalences because adding a constant is one.","step":"1.1","inputs":["L4","L6"]},{"id":"step-1-2","claim":"$S + T \\subseteq U$: for $s \\in S$ and $t \\in T$, adding $0 < s$ to $2 < t$ gives $2 = 0 + 2 < s + t$, and adding $s < 1$ to $t < 3$ gives $s + t < 1 + 3 = 4$.","step":"1.2","inputs":["L4","L6"]},{"id":"step-1-3","claim":"Let $x \\in U$ be arbitrary, so $2 < x < 4$, and put $s_x := (x - 2) \\cdot 2^{-1}$ and $t_x := (x + 2) \\cdot 2^{-1}$.","step":"1.3","inputs":["assume-hyp","L5","choose"]},{"id":"step-2-1","claim":"$s_x \\in S$ and $s_x + t_x = x$: subtracting $2$ from each part of $2 < x < 4$ gives $0 < x - 2 < 2$, that is $0 \\cdot 2 < s_x \\cdot 2 < 1 \\cdot 2$, so $0 < s_x < 1$ by [L5]; and $s_x + t_x = \\big((x - 2) + (x + 2)\\big) \\cdot 2^{-1} = (x + x) \\cdot 2^{-1} = (x \\cdot 2) \\cdot 2^{-1} = x$.","step":"2.1","inputs":["step 1.3","L4","L5","L6"]},{"id":"step-2-2","claim":"$t_x \\in T$: adding $2$ to each part of $2 < x < 4$ gives $2 \\cdot 2 = 4 < x + 2 < 4 + 2 = 3 \\cdot 2$, and $t_x \\cdot 2 = x + 2$, so $2 < t_x < 3$ by [L5].","step":"2.2","inputs":["step 1.3","L4","L5","L6"]},{"id":"step-2-3","claim":"$\\sup T = 3$, and $T$ is nonempty and bounded above: applying the translation identity to the nonempty bounded-above set $S$ with $a = 2$ gives that $2 + S$ is nonempty and bounded above with $\\sup(2 + S) = 2 + \\sup S = 2 + 1 = 3$, and $2 + S = T$ by 1.1.","step":"2.3","inputs":["step 1.1","L1","L2","L6"]},{"id":"step-3-1","claim":"$U \\subseteq S + T$, hence $S + T = U$: for arbitrary $x \\in U$ the elements $s_x \\in S$ and $t_x \\in T$ satisfy $s_x + t_x = x$, so $x \\in S + T$; combined with the inclusion of 1.2 this gives $S + T = U$, that is $(0,1) + (2,3) = (2,4)$.","step":"3.1","inputs":["step 2.1","step 2.2","step 1.2","step 1.3"]},{"id":"step-3-2","claim":"$\\sup(S + T) = 4$: both $S$ and $T$ are nonempty and bounded above, so the sumset identity applies and gives $\\sup(S + T) = \\sup S + \\sup T = 1 + 3 = 4$.","step":"3.2","inputs":["step 2.3","L1","L3","L6"]},{"id":"step-4-1","claim":"Therefore $(0,1) + (2,3)$ is exactly the interval $(2,4)$, and its supremum is $4$, which is the sum $\\sup(0,1) + \\sup(2,3)$ of the two suprema; in particular $\\sup U = 4$.","step":"4.1","inputs":["step 3.1","step 3.2","L3"]},{"id":"step-4-2","claim":"The value $4$ does not belong to $S + T$: by 3.1 one has $S + T = U$, membership in $U$ requires $x < 4$, and $4 < 4$ is impossible.","step":"4.2","inputs":["step 3.1","L7"]},{"id":"step-5-1","claim":"Hence $S + T$ is nonempty with $\\sup(S + T) = 4 \\notin S + T$, so by the attainment criterion $S + T$ has no maximum: the identity $\\sup(S+T) = \\sup S + \\sup T$ holds with the left-hand supremum unattained.","step":"5.1","inputs":["step 4.1","step 4.2","L3","L8"]}],"routine_steps":[],"boundaries":[{"case":"empty","status":"checked","evidence":"The source intervals are nonempty by the earlier example, and step 3.2 invokes the sum theorem only after step 2.3 supplies T nonempty."},{"case":"zero","status":"checked","evidence":"step 2.1 checks the lower open endpoint after subtracting 2 and halving."},{"case":"one","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 1.1 and 2.1 use the unit-interval endpoints explicitly."},{"case":"degenerate","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 2.1-2.2 prove the half-scaled decomposition for every x in the full open interval, with no endpoint equality assumed."},{"case":"endpoints","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 1.2, 3.1 and 4.2 check both strict endpoints 2 and 4."},{"case":"nonempty-choice","status":"checked","evidence":"step 1.3 explicitly constructs s_x and t_x for an arbitrary x rather than invoking an unproved selection."},{"case":"iff-forward","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a forward implication proof."},{"case":"iff-reverse","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a reverse implication proof."}],"finite_smoke":[],"risk_review":{"status":"pending","reviewer":null,"notes":"Reserved for the independent Alpha proof-refuter if risk routing selects this item after A3."}},"cex-sup-not-attained":{"citations":[{"fact":"L1","source":"ex-sup-of-open-interval","source_section":"Example","quote":"The open unit interval $I = (0,1) = \\{x \\in \\mathbb{R} : 0 < x < 1\\}$ has\n$\\sup I = 1$ and $\\inf I = 0$, and neither value belongs to $I$. This is the\nsmallest interesting computation in the subject: both bounds exist by the\nleast-upper-bound property ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]) and its dual\n([[thm-infimum-property]]), and both are missed by the set, so $I$ has neither a\nmaximum ([[lem-max-is-sup]]) nor a minimum. The maximum is ruled out by the\nattainment criterion; the minimum is ruled out directly, since a minimum would be\na lower bound of $I$ lying in $I$ and therefore at most $\\inf I = 0$\n([[def-max-min]], [[def-infimum]]).\n\nNothing here is asserted by inspection. That $1$ bounds $I$ above is checked from\nthe definition; that no smaller number does is checked with the epsilon\ncharacterisation ([[lem-sup-epsilon]]), by exhibiting, for each $\\varepsilon > 0$,\nan explicit element of $I$ lying strictly above $1 - \\varepsilon$. The witness is\n$\\max\\{1/2,\\ 1 - \\varepsilon/2\\}$: the second entry does the approximating, and\nthe first keeps the witness inside $I$ when $\\varepsilon$ is large. The infimum is\nhandled symmetrically with [[lem-inf-epsilon]] and the witness\n$\\min\\{1/2,\\ \\varepsilon/2\\}$.","uses":["1.1","1.2"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"lem-max-is-sup","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ be nonempty.\n\n1. If $S$ has a maximum ([[def-max-min]]), then $\\sup S$ exists and\n   $\\sup S = \\max S$.\n2. If $\\sup S$ exists and $\\sup S \\in S$, then $S$ has a maximum and\n   $\\max S = \\sup S$.\n\nHence, for a set whose supremum exists, the supremum is attained (belongs to the\nset) precisely when the set has a maximum, and then the two agree.","uses":["2.2"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"def-max-min","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ and $m \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n- $m$ is a **maximum** (or *greatest element*) of $S$ if $m \\in S$ and $s \\le m$\n  for every $s \\in S$.\n- $m$ is a **minimum** (or *least element*) of $S$ if $m \\in S$ and $m \\le s$\n  for every $s \\in S$.\n\nA set has at most one maximum: if $m_1$ and $m_2$ are both maxima then\n$m_1 \\in S$ gives $m_1 \\le m_2$ and $m_2 \\in S$ gives $m_2 \\le m_1$, so\n$m_1 = m_2$ by antisymmetry of the order, which is immediate from the trichotomy\naxiom of an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]]).\nThe same argument applies to minima, so we may write $\\max S$ and $\\min S$.","uses":["2.2"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"fs-sup-belongs-to-set","source_section":"Statement","quote":"**False claim:** if $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is nonempty and bounded above, then\n$\\sup S \\in S$.\n\nEquivalently, the false claim asserts that every nonempty set bounded above has a\nmaximum ([[def-max-min]]). It is refuted below by the open unit interval, whose\nsupremum exists, is unique, and lies outside the set.","uses":["1.1","2.1","3.1"]},{"fact":"L4","source":"def-complete-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.","uses":["1.2"]},{"fact":"L4","source":"def-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"An **ordered field** is a field $F$ ([[def-field]]) together with a subset\n$P \\subseteq F$, the set of **positive** elements (the *positive cone*),\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(O1) Trichotomy.** For each $x \\in F$, exactly one of the following holds:\n  $x \\in P$, or $x = 0$, or $-x \\in P$.\n- **(O2) Closure.** If $x, y \\in P$ then $x + y \\in P$ and $x \\cdot y \\in P$.\n\nWe write $x > 0$ for $x \\in P$ (read \"$x$ is positive\"), and define the order by\n\n$$a < b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; b - a \\in P, \\qquad a \\le b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a < b \\text{ or } a = b.$$\n\nThus $a > b$ means $b < a$, and $a \\ge b$ means $b \\le a$. An element $x$ with\n$-x \\in P$ (equivalently $x < 0$) is called **negative**.","uses":["1.2"]}],"derivations":[{"id":"step-1-1","claim":"$I$ is a nonempty subset of $\\mathbb{R}$ bounded above by $1$, so it is an instance of the claim, and its supremum exists with $\\sup I = 1$.","step":"1.1","inputs":["L1","L3"]},{"id":"step-1-2","claim":"$1 \\notin I$: membership in $I$ requires $x < 1$, and $1 < 1$ is impossible by irreflexivity.","step":"1.2","inputs":["L1","L4"]},{"id":"step-2-1","claim":"Hence $\\sup I = 1$ and $1 \\notin I$, so $\\sup I \\notin I$ and the claim fails on $I$.","step":"2.1","inputs":["step 1.1","step 1.2","L3"]},{"id":"step-2-2","claim":"Equivalently, $I$ has no maximum: a maximum of $I$ would have to be $\\sup I = 1$ and would have to lie in $I$, and $1$ does not.","step":"2.2","inputs":["step 1.1","step 1.2","L2"]},{"id":"step-3-1","claim":"The open unit interval is therefore a nonempty, bounded above subset of $\\mathbb{R}$ whose supremum exists and does not belong to it; the claim that a supremum belongs to its set is refuted, and so is the equivalent claim that boundedness above forces a maximum.","step":"3.1","inputs":["step 2.1","step 2.2","L3"]}],"routine_steps":[],"boundaries":[{"case":"empty","status":"checked","evidence":"step 1.1 imports the proved nonemptiness of I, so the witness meets the refuted claim hypothesis."},{"case":"zero","status":"checked","evidence":"step 1.1 fixes the witness interval above zero; no division-by-zero boundary occurs."},{"case":"one","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 1.1-2.1 explicitly check the upper endpoint 1 is the supremum and is excluded."},{"case":"degenerate","status":"checked","evidence":"step 2.2 checks that attainment cannot collapse to a maximum."},{"case":"endpoints","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 1.2 and 2.1 anchor the open upper endpoint."},{"case":"nonempty-choice","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The proof makes no choice from a set whose nonemptiness requires a separate discharge."},{"case":"iff-forward","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a forward implication proof."},{"case":"iff-reverse","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a reverse implication proof."}],"finite_smoke":[],"risk_review":{"status":"pending","reviewer":null,"notes":"Reserved for the independent Alpha proof-refuter if risk routing selects this item after A3."}},"cex-unbounded-set-has-no-sup":{"citations":[{"fact":"L1","source":"lem-of-naturals-positive","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field, with field structure as in [[def-field]], and for\n$n \\ge 1$ let $n \\cdot 1_F$ denote the canonical natural number\n$\\underbrace{1_F + \\cdots + 1_F}_{n}$, defined recursively by $1 \\cdot 1_F = 1_F$\nand $(n+1) \\cdot 1_F = n \\cdot 1_F + 1_F$. Then for every $n \\ge 1$:\n\n- $n \\cdot 1_F > 0$ and $(n+1) \\cdot 1_F > n \\cdot 1_F$;\n- the map $n \\mapsto n \\cdot 1_F$ is strictly increasing, hence injective, on $\\{1, 2, 3, \\dots\\}$;\n- $(m+n) \\cdot 1_F = m \\cdot 1_F + n \\cdot 1_F$ and $(mn) \\cdot 1_F = (m \\cdot 1_F)(n \\cdot 1_F)$ for all $m, n \\ge 1$.","uses":["1.1"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"thm-of-archimedean","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Every complete ordered field $F$ ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]) is Archimedean:\nfor every $x \\in F$ there is a natural number $n \\ge 1$ with $x < n \\cdot 1_F$,\nwhere $n \\cdot 1_F$ is the canonical natural of the ordered field $F$\n([[def-ordered-field]]). Equivalently, the canonical naturals are cofinal in $F$.","uses":["2.1"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"def-archimedean-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]). For a natural number\n$n \\ge 1$, write $n \\cdot 1_F := \\underbrace{1_F + \\cdots + 1_F}_{n}$ for the\n$n$-fold sum of the multiplicative identity, and $0 \\cdot 1_F := 0$. These are\nthe **canonical natural numbers** of $F$.\n\n$F$ is **Archimedean** if for every $x \\in F$ there is a natural number $n$ with\n\n$$x < n \\cdot 1_F.$$\n\nEquivalently, the canonical naturals $(n \\cdot 1_F)_{n \\ge 1}$ are **cofinal**:\nno single element of $F$ is an upper bound for all of them.","uses":["2.1"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"def-complete-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.","uses":["2.1","3.1","4.1"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"def-bounded-set","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Throughout, $\\mathbb{R}$ denotes the complete ordered field\n([[def-complete-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is a subset of it.\n\nThe notions **upper bound** and **bounded above** are already fixed by\n[[def-complete-ordered-field]] and are only recalled here, never redefined:\n$u \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is an *upper bound* of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$, and\n$S$ is *bounded above* if it has at least one upper bound. The dual notions are:\n\n- $\\ell \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is a **lower bound** of $S$ if $\\ell \\le s$ for all $s \\in S$.\n- $S$ is **bounded below** if it has at least one lower bound.\n- $S$ is **bounded** if it is both bounded above and bounded below, that is, if\n  there are $\\ell, u \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $\\ell \\le s \\le u$ for every $s \\in S$.","uses":["2.1","3.1","4.1"]},{"fact":"L4","source":"fs-every-set-has-sup","source_section":"Statement","quote":"**False claim:** every subset $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ has a supremum in\n$\\mathbb{R}$.\n\nThe least-upper-bound property of $\\mathbb{R}$ ([[def-complete-ordered-field]])\ncarries two hypotheses, that $S$ is nonempty and that $S$ is bounded above, and\nneither may be dropped. Two independent witnesses are given below, one failing\neach hypothesis on its own.","uses":["4.1"]},{"fact":"L5","source":"def-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"An **ordered field** is a field $F$ ([[def-field]]) together with a subset\n$P \\subseteq F$, the set of **positive** elements (the *positive cone*),\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(O1) Trichotomy.** For each $x \\in F$, exactly one of the following holds:\n  $x \\in P$, or $x = 0$, or $-x \\in P$.\n- **(O2) Closure.** If $x, y \\in P$ then $x + y \\in P$ and $x \\cdot y \\in P$.\n\nWe write $x > 0$ for $x \\in P$ (read \"$x$ is positive\"), and define the order by\n\n$$a < b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; b - a \\in P, \\qquad a \\le b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a < b \\text{ or } a = b.$$\n\nThus $a > b$ means $b < a$, and $a \\ge b$ means $b \\le a$. An element $x$ with\n$-x \\in P$ (equivalently $x < 0$) is called **negative**.","uses":["2.1"]},{"fact":"L5","source":"def-complete-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.","uses":["2.1"]}],"derivations":[{"id":"step-1-1","claim":"$A$ is a nonempty subset of $\\mathbb{R}$: taking $n = 1$ gives $1 \\cdot 1 = 1 \\in A$.","step":"1.1","inputs":["L1"]},{"id":"step-1-2","claim":"Let $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ be arbitrary.","step":"1.2","inputs":["assume-hyp"]},{"id":"step-2-1","claim":"$x$ is not an upper bound of $A$: the Archimedean property supplies a natural $n \\ge 1$ with $x < n \\cdot 1$, and $n \\cdot 1$ is an element of $A$, so the requirement $n \\cdot 1 \\le x$ for an upper bound fails by trichotomy.","step":"2.1","inputs":["step 1.2","L2","L3","L5"]},{"id":"step-3-1","claim":"Since $x$ was an arbitrary real number, no real number is an upper bound of $A$; hence $A$ is not bounded above.","step":"3.1","inputs":["step 2.1","step 1.2","L3"]},{"id":"step-4-1","claim":"A supremum of $A$ would in particular be an upper bound of $A$, and there is none, so $A$ has no supremum in $\\mathbb{R}$ even though $A$ is nonempty; the claim that every subset of $\\mathbb{R}$ has a supremum is refuted, and the boundedness hypothesis of the least-upper-bound property cannot be dropped.","step":"4.1","inputs":["step 3.1","step 1.1","L3","L4"]}],"routine_steps":[],"boundaries":[{"case":"empty","status":"checked","evidence":"step 1.1 exhibits 1 in A."},{"case":"zero","status":"checked","evidence":"The index range n>=1 excludes the zero-index edge case and step 1.1 anchors the first term."},{"case":"one","status":"checked","evidence":"step 1.1 explicitly handles n=1."},{"case":"degenerate","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 2.1-3.1 quantify over every proposed real upper bound, including negative or canonical values."},{"case":"endpoints","status":"not_applicable","reason":"No interval endpoint or finite-range endpoint occurs in this statement or proof."},{"case":"nonempty-choice","status":"checked","evidence":"step 2.1 uses the cited Archimedean existential after an arbitrary x is fixed."},{"case":"iff-forward","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a forward implication proof."},{"case":"iff-reverse","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a reverse implication proof."}],"finite_smoke":[],"risk_review":{"status":"pending","reviewer":null,"notes":"Reserved for the independent Alpha proof-refuter if risk routing selects this item after A3."}},"cex-empty-set-has-no-sup":{"citations":[{"fact":"L1","source":"def-complete-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.","uses":["1.1","3.1"]},{"fact":"L1","source":"def-bounded-set","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Throughout, $\\mathbb{R}$ denotes the complete ordered field\n([[def-complete-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is a subset of it.\n\nThe notions **upper bound** and **bounded above** are already fixed by\n[[def-complete-ordered-field]] and are only recalled here, never redefined:\n$u \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is an *upper bound* of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$, and\n$S$ is *bounded above* if it has at least one upper bound. The dual notions are:\n\n- $\\ell \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is a **lower bound** of $S$ if $\\ell \\le s$ for all $s \\in S$.\n- $S$ is **bounded below** if it has at least one lower bound.\n- $S$ is **bounded** if it is both bounded above and bounded below, that is, if\n  there are $\\ell, u \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $\\ell \\le s \\le u$ for every $s \\in S$.","uses":["1.1","3.1"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"cor-of-one-positive","source_section":"Statement","quote":"In any ordered field $F$ with positive cone $P$, the multiplicative identity\nsatisfies $0 < 1$; that is, $1 \\in P$.","uses":["2.1","3.1"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"lem-of-add-order","source_section":"Statement","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) with positive cone $P$, and\nlet $a, b, c, d \\in F$.\n\n1. **Translation invariance.** If $a < b$ then $a + c < b + c$.\n2. **Adding inequalities.** If $a < b$ and $c < d$ then $a + c < b + d$.","uses":["2.1","3.1"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"def-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"An **ordered field** is a field $F$ ([[def-field]]) together with a subset\n$P \\subseteq F$, the set of **positive** elements (the *positive cone*),\nsatisfying:\n\n- **(O1) Trichotomy.** For each $x \\in F$, exactly one of the following holds:\n  $x \\in P$, or $x = 0$, or $-x \\in P$.\n- **(O2) Closure.** If $x, y \\in P$ then $x + y \\in P$ and $x \\cdot y \\in P$.\n\nWe write $x > 0$ for $x \\in P$ (read \"$x$ is positive\"), and define the order by\n\n$$a < b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; b - a \\in P, \\qquad a \\le b \\;:\\Longleftrightarrow\\; a < b \\text{ or } a = b.$$\n\nThus $a > b$ means $b < a$, and $a \\ge b$ means $b \\le a$. An element $x$ with\n$-x \\in P$ (equivalently $x < 0$) is called **negative**.","uses":["2.1","3.1"]},{"fact":"L2","source":"def-complete-ordered-field","source_section":"Definition","quote":"Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \\subseteq F$.\n\n- $u \\in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \\le u$ for all $s \\in S$; $S$ is\n  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.\n- $u \\in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$\n  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \\le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.\n\n$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the\n**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty\n$S \\subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.","uses":["2.1","3.1"]},{"fact":"L3","source":"fs-every-set-has-sup","source_section":"Statement","quote":"**False claim:** every subset $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ has a supremum in\n$\\mathbb{R}$.\n\nThe least-upper-bound property of $\\mathbb{R}$ ([[def-complete-ordered-field]])\ncarries two hypotheses, that $S$ is nonempty and that $S$ is bounded above, and\nneither may be dropped. Two independent witnesses are given below, one failing\neach hypothesis on its own.","uses":["4.1"]}],"derivations":[{"id":"step-1-1","claim":"Every $w \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is both an upper bound and a lower bound of $\\emptyset$: the defining condition quantifies over no elements and so holds vacuously. In particular $\\emptyset$ is bounded, and its set of upper bounds is all of $\\mathbb{R}$.","step":"1.1","inputs":["L1"]},{"id":"step-1-2","claim":"Let $w \\in \\mathbb{R}$ be an arbitrary upper bound of $\\emptyset$.","step":"1.2","inputs":["assume-hyp"]},{"id":"step-2-1","claim":"The number $w - 1$ is also an upper bound of $\\emptyset$, by 1.1, and $w - 1 < w$, since $0 < 1$ gives $w - 1 < (w - 1) + 1 = w$ on adding $w - 1$ to both sides.","step":"2.1","inputs":["step 1.1","step 1.2","L2"]},{"id":"step-3-1","claim":"Hence $w \\le w - 1$ fails by trichotomy, so $w$ is not $\\le$ every upper bound of $\\emptyset$ and is therefore not a supremum of $\\emptyset$; as $w$ was an arbitrary upper bound, and every real is one, no real number is a supremum of $\\emptyset$.","step":"3.1","inputs":["step 2.1","step 1.2","L1","L2"]},{"id":"step-4-1","claim":"So $\\emptyset$ is a bounded subset of $\\mathbb{R}$ with no supremum in $\\mathbb{R}$: the claim that every subset of $\\mathbb{R}$ has a supremum is refuted, this time by a set that is bounded but not nonempty, and the nonemptiness hypothesis of the least-upper-bound property cannot be dropped either.","step":"4.1","inputs":["step 3.1","step 1.1","L3"]}],"routine_steps":[],"boundaries":[{"case":"empty","status":"checked","evidence":"steps 1.1-4.1 are the explicit empty-set boundary audit: vacuous boundedness and the absence of a least real upper bound."},{"case":"zero","status":"checked","evidence":"step 2.1 derives w-1<w from 0<1."},{"case":"one","status":"checked","evidence":"step 2.1 explicitly uses the unit displacement 1."},{"case":"degenerate","status":"checked","evidence":"step 3.1 rules out every candidate upper bound, including any equality case."},{"case":"endpoints","status":"not_applicable","reason":"No interval endpoint or finite-range endpoint occurs in this statement or proof."},{"case":"nonempty-choice","status":"checked","evidence":"The proof deliberately makes no element choice from the empty set; step 1.1 uses vacuous quantification only."},{"case":"iff-forward","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a forward implication proof."},{"case":"iff-reverse","status":"not_applicable","reason":"The statement is not a biconditional requiring a reverse implication proof."}],"finite_smoke":[],"risk_review":{"status":"pending","reviewer":null,"notes":"Reserved for the independent Alpha proof-refuter if risk routing selects this item after A3."}}}}
