items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md:57:1.1 Base case $n = 1$: by definition $1 \cdot 1_F = 1_F$, and $1_F > 0$. [L1, base]
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md:59:1.2 Inductive hypothesis: assume $n \cdot 1_F > 0$ for some fixed $n \ge 1$. [ih]
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md:61:1.3 By the recursion, $(n+1) \cdot 1_F - n \cdot 1_F = 1_F > 0$, so $(n+1) \cdot 1_F > n \cdot 1_F$. [given, L1]
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md:63:1.4 Additive identity: fix $m$ and induct on $n$; at $n = 1$, $(m+1) \cdot 1_F = m \cdot 1_F + 1_F$ by the recursion, and if $(m+n) \cdot 1_F = m \cdot 1_F + n \cdot 1_F$ then $(m+n+1) \cdot 1_F = (m+n) \cdot 1_F + 1_F = m \cdot 1_F + n \cdot 1_F + 1_F = m \cdot 1_F + (n+1) \cdot 1_F$. [given, algebra]
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md:65:2.1 Inductive step: $(n+1) \cdot 1_F = n \cdot 1_F + 1_F$ is a sum of two positives, by the hypothesis and $1_F > 0$, hence $(n+1) \cdot 1_F > 0$. [step 1.2, L1, L2]
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md:67:2.2 Iterating 1.3, for $m < n$ one has $m \cdot 1_F < n \cdot 1_F$, so $n \mapsto n \cdot 1_F$ is strictly increasing and therefore injective on $\{1, 2, 3, \dots\}$. [step 1.3, L2]
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md:69:2.3 Multiplicative identity: fix $m$ and induct on $n$; at $n = 1$, $(m \cdot 1) \cdot 1_F = m \cdot 1_F = (m \cdot 1_F)(1 \cdot 1_F)$, and the step $(m(n+1)) \cdot 1_F = (mn + m) \cdot 1_F = (mn) \cdot 1_F + m \cdot 1_F = (m \cdot 1_F)(n \cdot 1_F) + (m \cdot 1_F)(1_F) = (m \cdot 1_F)((n+1) \cdot 1_F)$ uses 1.4 and distributivity. [step 1.4, algebra]
items/lem-of-naturals-positive.md:71:3.1 By induction on 1.1, 1.2, 2.1, $n \cdot 1_F > 0$ for every $n \ge 1$; with the strict increase 2.2 and the identities 1.4, 2.3, every claim holds. [step 2.1, step 2.2, step 1.4, step 2.3, discharge-induction] ∎
items/lem-of-abs-value.md:59:1.1 Case $x > 0$: by [L1] $|x| = x > 0$, so $|x| \ge 0$ and $|x| \ne 0$; since $-x < 0$ we get $|-x| = -(-x) = x = |x|$ by [L1], and $-|x| = -x < 0 < x = |x|$, so $-|x| \le x \le |x|$. [assume-case pos, L1, L2, algebra]
items/lem-of-abs-value.md:61:1.2 Case $x = 0$: then $|x| = |0| = 0$, so $|x| \ge 0$ holds with $|x| = 0$ and $x = 0$, while $|-x| = |0| = |x|$ and $-|x| = 0 \le 0 \le 0 = |x|$. [assume-case zero, L1]
items/lem-of-abs-value.md:63:1.3 Case $x < 0$: by [L1] $|x| = -x$, and [L2] gives $-x > 0$, so $|x| > 0$ and $|x| \ne 0$; here $|-x| = -x = |x|$ by [L1], and $-|x| = -(-x) = x \le x$ while $x < 0 < -x = |x|$, so $-|x| \le x \le |x|$. [assume-case neg, L1, L2, algebra]
items/lem-of-abs-value.md:65:1.4 Case $x = 0$ or $y = 0$: then $xy = 0$, so $|xy| = 0$, and one of $|x|, |y|$ is $0$, whence $|x|\,|y| = 0 = |xy|$. [assume-case zerofactor, L1]
items/lem-of-abs-value.md:67:1.5 Case $x, y$ have the same sign (both positive or both negative): by [L4] $xy > 0$, so $|xy| = xy$, while $|x|\,|y| = xy$ by [L3] (for $x, y < 0$ this is $(-x)(-y) = xy$), hence $|xy| = |x|\,|y|$. [assume-case same, L1, L3, L4]
items/lem-of-abs-value.md:69:1.6 Case $x, y$ have opposite signs (one positive, one negative): by [L4] $xy < 0$, so $|xy| = -(xy)$, while $|x|\,|y| = -(xy)$ by [L3] (namely $x(-y)$ or $(-x)y$), hence $|xy| = |x|\,|y|$. [assume-case opposite, L1, L3, L4]
items/lem-of-abs-value.md:71:2.1 By trichotomy [L2] each $x$ lies in exactly one of the cases 1.1-1.3, and in each we verified $|x| \ge 0$, that $|x| = 0 \iff x = 0$, that $|-x| = |x|$, and that $-|x| \le x \le |x|$; hence all four hold for every $x$. [step 1.1, step 1.2, step 1.3, cases-exhaustive]
items/lem-of-abs-value.md:73:2.2 By trichotomy [L2] each pair $x, y$ lies in exactly one of the cases 1.4-1.6, and in each $|xy| = |x|\,|y|$; hence $|xy| = |x|\,|y|$ for all $x, y$. [step 1.4, step 1.5, step 1.6, cases-exhaustive]
items/lem-of-abs-value.md:75:3.1 Let $c > 0$: if $|x| < c$ then $-c < -|x| \le x \le |x| < c$ by [step 2.1] and [L2], so $-c < x < c$; conversely if $-c < x < c$ then both $x < c$ and $-x < c$, and since $|x|$ equals $x$ or $-x$ by [L1], we get $|x| < c$, so $|x| < c \iff -c < x < c$. [step 2.1, L1, L2] ∎
items/lem-of-inverse-unique.md:51:1.1 The additive identity is unique: if $0$ and $0'$ both satisfy $x + 0 = x$ and $x + 0' = x$ for all $x$, then $0' = 0' + 0 = 0 + 0' = 0$, using that $0$ is an identity, commutativity, and that $0'$ is an identity. [L1]
items/lem-of-inverse-unique.md:53:1.2 Additive inverses are unique: if $a$ and $b$ both satisfy $x + a = 0$ and $x + b = 0$, then $a = a + 0 = a + (x + b) = (a + x) + b = 0 + b = b$, using associativity and commutativity. [L1]
items/lem-of-inverse-unique.md:55:1.3 The same two arguments in the abelian group $(F \setminus \{0\}, \cdot)$ give uniqueness of the multiplicative identity, $1' = 1' \cdot 1 = 1 \cdot 1' = 1$, and of multiplicative inverses: if $x a = 1$ and $x b = 1$ with $x \ne 0$, then $a = a \cdot 1 = a (x b) = (a x) b = 1 \cdot b = b$ (using $a x = x a = 1$). [L1]
items/lem-of-inverse-unique.md:57:2.1 Therefore $0$, $1$ and, for each $x$, its additive inverse $-x$ and (for $x \ne 0$) its multiplicative inverse $x^{-1}$ are the unique elements with their defining properties, so all four notations are well-defined. [step 1.1, step 1.2, step 1.3] ∎
items/cor-archimedean-reciprocal.md:68:1.1 Since $\varepsilon > 0$, trichotomy gives $\varepsilon \ne 0$, so $\varepsilon^{-1}$ exists, and $\varepsilon^{-1} > 0$. [given, L2, L3]
items/cor-archimedean-reciprocal.md:70:2.1 Apply [L1] to $x = \varepsilon^{-1}$: fix a natural number $n \ge 1$ with $\varepsilon^{-1} < n \cdot 1_F$. [step 1.1, L1, choose]
items/cor-archimedean-reciprocal.md:72:3.1 Chaining the two displayed inequalities gives $0 < \varepsilon^{-1} < n \cdot 1_F$; in particular $n \cdot 1_F > 0$, so $n \cdot 1_F \ne 0$ and $1/(n \cdot 1_F)$ is defined. [step 1.1, step 2.1, L3]
items/cor-archimedean-reciprocal.md:74:4.1 Apply the second claim of [L2] with $a = \varepsilon^{-1}$ and $b = n \cdot 1_F$: $0 < (n \cdot 1_F)^{-1} < (\varepsilon^{-1})^{-1}$. [step 3.1, L2]
items/cor-archimedean-reciprocal.md:76:5.1 By [L3], $(\varepsilon^{-1})^{-1} = \varepsilon$, so the natural number $n \ge 1$ fixed in step 2.1 satisfies $1/(n \cdot 1_F) < \varepsilon$, which is the assertion. [step 4.1, L3] ∎
items/lem-of-triangle-inequality.md:54:1.1 By [L1], $-|x| \le x \le |x|$ and $-|y| \le y \le |y|$. [L1]
items/lem-of-triangle-inequality.md:56:2.1 Adding the two chains of [step 1.1] with [L2] and using $-(|x| + |y|) = -|x| + (-|y|)$ from [L3] gives $-(|x| + |y|) \le x + y \le |x| + |y|$. [step 1.1, L2, L3]
items/lem-of-triangle-inequality.md:58:3.1 By [L1] the value $|x + y|$ equals $x + y$ or $-(x + y)$; both $x + y \le |x| + |y|$ and $-(x + y) \le |x| + |y|$ hold by [step 2.1] and [L3] (the latter from $-(|x| + |y|) \le x + y$), so $|x + y| \le |x| + |y|$. [step 2.1, L1, L3] ∎
items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md:57:1.1 Assume $a > 0$, so $a \neq 0$ and its inverse $a^{-1}$ exists with $a \cdot a^{-1} = 1$; moreover $a^{-1} \neq 0$, since $a^{-1}$ has $a$ as its inverse while $0$ is non-invertible ($1 \neq 0$ by L3). [assume-hyp, L1, L3]
items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md:59:2.1 By trichotomy $a^{-1} \in P$ or $-a^{-1} \in P$; if $-a^{-1} \in P$, then $a > 0$ and $a^{-1} < 0$ give $a \cdot a^{-1} < 0$ by the sign rules, i.e. $1 < 0$, contradicting $0 < 1$; hence $a^{-1} \in P$, i.e. $a^{-1} > 0$, proving claim 1. [step 1.1, L2, L3, L1]
items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md:61:3.1 Assume $0 < a < b$; then $0 < b$ by transitivity, so by claim 1 both $a^{-1} > 0$ and $b^{-1} > 0$, and the sign rules give $a^{-1} b^{-1} > 0$. [assume-hyp, step 2.1, L4, L2, L1]
items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md:63:4.1 Multiplying $a < b$ by the positive $a^{-1} b^{-1}$ via the sign rules gives $a (a^{-1} b^{-1}) < b (a^{-1} b^{-1})$; since $a a^{-1} = 1$ and $b b^{-1} = 1$, this simplifies to $b^{-1} < a^{-1}$. [step 3.1, L2, algebra]
items/lem-of-inverse-positive.md:65:5.1 Together with $b^{-1} > 0$ from step 3.1, we conclude $0 < b^{-1} < a^{-1}$, proving claim 2. [step 3.1, step 4.1] ∎
items/thm-of-archimedean.md:55:1.1 Suppose, for contradiction, that $F$ is not Archimedean: there is some $x \in F$ with $n \cdot 1_F \le x$ for all $n \ge 1$, that is, $x$ is an upper bound of $A$. [assume-contra]
items/thm-of-archimedean.md:57:2.1 The set $A$ is nonempty, since $1 \cdot 1_F = 1_F \in A$, and it is bounded above by $x$. [step 1.1, L2]
items/thm-of-archimedean.md:59:3.1 By the least-upper-bound property, $A$ has a least upper bound $s = \sup A \in F$. [step 2.1, L1]
items/thm-of-archimedean.md:61:4.1 Since $1_F > 0$, we have $s - 1_F < s$; as $s$ is the least upper bound, $s - 1_F$ is not an upper bound of $A$. [step 3.1, L3]
items/thm-of-archimedean.md:63:5.1 Hence there is some $m \ge 1$ with $m \cdot 1_F > s - 1_F$. [step 4.1]
items/thm-of-archimedean.md:65:6.1 Adding $1_F$ to both sides, $(m+1) \cdot 1_F = m \cdot 1_F + 1_F > s$. [step 5.1, L2]
items/thm-of-archimedean.md:67:7.1 But $(m+1) \cdot 1_F \in A$, so $(m+1) \cdot 1_F \le s$ because $s$ is an upper bound of $A$, contradicting 6.1. [step 6.1, step 3.1, L2]
items/thm-of-archimedean.md:69:8.1 The assumption is therefore untenable, so $F$ is Archimedean. [step 7.1, discharge-contradiction] ∎
items/lem-of-q-embeds.md:62:1.1 Define $\iota$ on the integers by $\iota(n) = n \cdot 1_F$ for $n \ge 0$ and $\iota(-n) = -(n \cdot 1_F)$; by [L2] this is additive and multiplicative on $\mathbb{Z}$ and sends $1 \mapsto 1_F$. [L2]
items/lem-of-q-embeds.md:64:1.2 For a rational $x = p/q$ with $q \ge 1$ define $\iota(x) = \iota(p)\,(q \cdot 1_F)^{-1}$, which makes sense because $q \cdot 1_F > 0 \ne 0$ has an inverse. [L2]
items/lem-of-q-embeds.md:66:2.1 Well-defined: if $p/q = p'/q'$ with $q, q' \ge 1$, then $p q' = p' q$ in $\mathbb{Z}$, so [L2] gives $\iota(p)(q' \cdot 1_F) = \iota(p')(q \cdot 1_F)$, and multiplying by the positive $(q \cdot 1_F)^{-1}(q' \cdot 1_F)^{-1}$ yields $\iota(p)(q \cdot 1_F)^{-1} = \iota(p')(q' \cdot 1_F)^{-1}$; thus $\iota(x)$ is independent of the representative. [step 1.1, step 1.2, L2, L3]
items/lem-of-q-embeds.md:68:2.2 Multiplicativity: for $x = p/q$, $y = r/s$ one has $xy = (pr)/(qs)$, and $\iota(xy) = \iota(pr)((qs) \cdot 1_F)^{-1} = \iota(p)\iota(r)(q \cdot 1_F)^{-1}(s \cdot 1_F)^{-1} = \iota(x)\iota(y)$, using $(mn) \cdot 1_F = (m \cdot 1_F)(n \cdot 1_F)$ and $(uv)^{-1} = u^{-1} v^{-1}$. [step 1.2, L2]
items/lem-of-q-embeds.md:70:2.3 Additivity: with $x + y = (ps + rq)/(qs)$, $\iota(x+y) = (\iota(p)(s \cdot 1_F) + \iota(r)(q \cdot 1_F))(q \cdot 1_F)^{-1}(s \cdot 1_F)^{-1} = \iota(p)(q \cdot 1_F)^{-1} + \iota(r)(s \cdot 1_F)^{-1} = \iota(x) + \iota(y)$, using the additive and multiplicative identities of [L2]. [step 1.2, L2]
items/lem-of-q-embeds.md:72:2.4 Positivity: if $x = p/q > 0$ in $\mathbb{Q}$ with $q \ge 1$, then $p \ge 1$ by [L1], so $\iota(p) = p \cdot 1_F > 0$ and $q \cdot 1_F > 0$ by [L2], whence $(q \cdot 1_F)^{-1} > 0$ by [L3] and $\iota(x) = \iota(p)(q \cdot 1_F)^{-1} > 0$ by [L4]. [step 1.2, L1, L2, L3, L4]
items/lem-of-q-embeds.md:74:2.5 Uniqueness on $\mathbb{Z}$: let $\psi : \mathbb{Q} \to F$ be any field homomorphism; then $\psi(1) = 1_F$, additivity forces $\psi(n) = n \cdot 1_F = \iota(n)$ for $n \ge 1$, and $\psi(0) = 0$, $\psi(-n) = -(n \cdot 1_F)$, so $\psi = \iota$ on $\mathbb{Z}$. [step 1.1, L5]
items/lem-of-q-embeds.md:76:3.1 Unit: $\iota(1) = \iota(1/1) = \iota(1)(1 \cdot 1_F)^{-1} = 1_F$; hence $\iota$ is a field homomorphism $\mathbb{Q} \to F$. [step 2.2, step 2.3, L2, L5]
items/lem-of-q-embeds.md:78:3.2 Order: for $x < y$ in $\mathbb{Q}$ we have $y - x > 0$, so $\iota(y) - \iota(x) = \iota(y - x) > 0$ by 2.3 and 2.4, that is $\iota(x) < \iota(y)$; thus $\iota$ is order-preserving. [step 2.3, step 2.4]
items/lem-of-q-embeds.md:80:4.1 Injectivity: if $x \ne y$ then $x < y$ or $y < x$, and 3.2 forces $\iota(x) \ne \iota(y)$; so $\iota$ is injective, an embedding of ordered fields. [step 3.2]
items/lem-of-q-embeds.md:82:5.1 Uniqueness on $\mathbb{Q}$: for $p/q \in \mathbb{Q}$, $\psi(p/q) = \psi(p)\psi(q)^{-1} = \iota(p)(q \cdot 1_F)^{-1} = \iota(p/q)$ since $\psi$ preserves products and inverses; hence $\psi = \iota$, so $\iota$ is the unique field homomorphism $\mathbb{Q} \to F$. [step 2.5, step 1.2, L5] ∎

